          <!-- ====== EPIGRAPH ====== -->
          <section id="epigraph" class="content-section">
            <h2 class="section-title">T. S. Eliot</h2>

            <blockquote><p>I confess, however, that I am not myself very much concerned with the question of influence, or with those publicists who have impressed their names upon the public by catching the morning tide and rowing very fast in the direction in which the current was flowing; but rather that there should always be a few writers preoccupied in penetrating to the core of the matter, in trying to arrive at the truth and to set it forth, without too much hope, without ambition to alter the immediate course of affairs, and without being downcast or defeated when nothing appears to ensue.</p></blockquote>
          </section>

          <!-- ====== LETTER ====== -->
          <section id="editor-letter" class="content-section">
            <h2 class="section-title">Letter from the Editor</h2>
            <p class="essay-author">By Jake Meador</p>

            <blockquote><p>letter from the editor</p></blockquote>
            <p>Dear Readers,</p>
            <p>If you were around for our first few issues of the print journal, you’ll recall that we usually had a theme for each issue that most of the essays and reviews were organized around. Over time, we’ve drifted away from that, piecing each journal together less around a pre-selected theme and more around the books that happen to be coming out, the authors God has brought to us, and the conversations that catch our attention.</p>
            <p>What is interesting is that even with that far less deliberate method, themes still have a way of announcing themselves in the final phases of the project. And so it is again with this issue. I am writing this letter shortly after the release of Pope Leo XIV’s encyclical, “Magnifica Humanitas.” This issue was already drafted in full by the time the encyclical was released, so none of the pieces you’ll find inside address the pope’s first encyclical directly. Yet even so, there are thematic overlaps I found quite striking.</p>
            <p>Our lead essay comes from my good friend Brian Auten and it is hard to summarize: It’s about the experience of Japanese-Americans during internment. It’s about public schools. And it’s about the memory one man has over 50 years removed from his time in a kindergarten classroom being taught by a Japanese-American teacher who had spent time in an internment camp during the war. But at bottom I think the essay is about what it means to see a person, what it means to honor what Leo would call “the ontological dignity” each person possesses, and the specific ways that tricky and complicated work can be done in the aftermath of injustice. It’s one of my favorite things we’ve ever published and I’m thrilled to share it with you.</p>
            <p>We also have an essay by Annie Crawford that will, I think, become the canonical piece I refer people to when they ask me why they should read <em>That Hideous Strength</em>, the C. S. Lewis novel that has found a new and enthusiastic audience over the past ten years. Crawford’s work has the same sort of breadth and penetrative insight that I associate with another great reader of Lewis’s–Michael Ward. It also reminds me of the much-quoted line about Lewis that “what he thought about everything was present in what he said about anything.” Close readers of Lewis, like Crawford, serve us well by helping us to see how true that actually is by drawing our attention to the many different ways that Lewis made the same sort of arguments.</p>
            <p>There is much else to enjoy in this issue: Kirsten Sanders examines our cultural obsession with the end of the world. Tessa Carman considers the historical roots of homeschooling within America’s democratic life in ways that surprised me and caused me to see the issue in a new way–which I wouldn’t have thought was possible before reading, given that I’ve been around homeschoolers for virtually my entire life and was homeschooled myself. In my essay, I consider Wendell Berry’s evolving relationship to Christianity over the course of his work and, particularly, how he finds himself needing Christianity to anchor the critique he wants to make of industrialism and contemporary common life. In many ways, he ends up saying something very similar to what Lewis says in <em>That Hideous Strength</em>.</p>
            <p>Bradley Blaylock makes the case for why anxiety can provoke us toward maturity with the help of Soren Kierkegaard. Josh Heavin presents union with Christ to us as a many-sided jewel and has the imagination and theological sophistication required to help us see its many sides with clarity and gratitude. Vika Pechersky considers the benefits and limitations of offering intellectually sound answers to hard questions, while James Wood reviews a recent and much welcome book on post-liberalism. Agnes Howard’s piece on cognitive labor and domestic life is perhaps my favorite thing I’ve ever read on that topic.</p>
            <p>This issue also includes reviews from Ashley Hales, Beatrice Scudeler, Daniel K. Williams, Matthew Loftus, and Nadya Williams. What seems to link so many of the pieces you will read here, I think, is the difficulty and necessity of paying attention—paying attention to people, paying attention to reality, and paying attention to God. To do that well is not easy; it requires a certain patience from us, as well as a certain willingness to question ourselves and let others challenge us and make demands on us. Our hope is that the essays and reviews in this issue of the journal will help us all learn how to steward our attention well.</p>
            <p>Thank you for reading.</p>
            <p>Under the Mercy,</p>
            <p class="contributor-bio">Jake Meador is the editor-in-chief of Mere Orthodoxy. He lives in his hometown of Lincoln, NE with his wife and four children.</p>
          </section>

          <!-- ====== ARTICLE 1 ====== -->
          <section id="being-a-public-school" class="content-section">
            <h2 class="section-title">Being a Public School Kid</h2>
            <p class="essay-author">By Brian Auten</p>

            <p>Since the 1960s, conservative Christians have railed against public schools as little more than “leftist indoctrination factories.” Christian critics of “government schools” (as the cool Rushdoony kids say) regularly accuse administrators and teachers of hijacking captive public school audiences, pushing left-wing, anti-American history, weaponizing libraries, and turning their students against “traditional,” conservative, or Christian values.</p>
            <p>I’m not going to litigate the truth or prevalence of these claims. Instead, I’m going to offer a personal story about public school.</p>
            <p>Cards up front: I am a public school kid. Our two kids are public school kids, and my wife, who was a public school kid, has worked for decades in public schools.</p>
            <p>And while some of my conservative Christian friends might be tempted to label my story as an example of indirect, on-the-sly “leftist indoctrination” or an early, slippery slope version of “woke,” I’m going to argue differently.</p>
            <p>It was something very lovely, and something we desperately need more of today.</p>
            <p>But first, I have to talk about 1974.</p>
            <p>***</p>
            <p>It was an auspicious year in America.</p>
            <p>President Nixon’s resignation stunned the nation in August, and just weeks later, President Ford pardoned his predecessor and offered a conditional amnesty for Vietnam War draft evaders. In December, the Senate accused the Pentagon of spying on the White House, and Seymour Hersh broke his blockbuster story about the CIA’s domestic operations against antiwar activists.</p>
            <p>When historians talk about the feeling or mood evoked by this period — the zeitgeist —they simply call it “post-Watergate.”</p>
            <p>And if one locale incarnated “post-Watergate” more than any other, that place was Los Angeles.</p>
            <p>Earlier, in May, the Los Angeles Police Department’s SWAT surrounded a house used by the Symbionese Liberation Army (SLA). Television viewers across the country watched live as the siege concluded with gunfire and the house engulfed in flames. Mere days before Nixon’s resignation, Muharem Kurbegovic, a Yugoslav-American aerospace engineer arrested for lewd conduct and fired from his job, killed three and injured 36 in a bombing at Los Angeles International Airport.</p>
            <p>And it wasn’t just the violence. Los Angeles was at the forefront of America’s “post-Watergate” cultural transformation, whether in the debut of folk rock’s melancholic “Los Angeles sound,” or in the introduction of <em>All in the Family, M*A*S*H</em>, and <em>The Mary Tyler Moore Show</em> — the controversial, socially conscious “greatest lineup in television history.”</p>
            <p>In 1974, Los Angeles was the vanguard. It premonished the almost-dawning American culture war.</p>
            <p>It was also the year I entered Mrs. Mary Higuchi’s kindergarten class at John Adams Elementary School in Los Angeles’ South Bay suburb of Torrance.</p>
            <p>Historically, Los Angeles’s South Bay—including Torrance and Gardena—has had one of the largest concentrations of Japanese residents in Southern California. Outside of Honolulu, Torrance has the largest Japanese population of any city in the United States. When Japanese immigrants came to California, many leased or bought South Bay farmland and also built nurseries and garden centers. Though much of the farmland was eventually replaced by tract homes and designer neighborhoods, the Japanese-American community remained. In fact, in the late 50s and early 60s, Honda, Datsun/Nissan, and Toyota all flocked to the South Bay to set up US headquarters.</p>
            <p>In 1974, John Adams Elementary was filled with Tanakas, Kikunos, Yoshiokas, Kamiyas, Uyematsus, Sumitomos, Nishihiras, Muranakas, and Oshimas. One of my good childhood friends was a Kashiwai. It was a mix of older Japanese-American families and newcomers, some direct from Japan and others via Okinawa or Hawaii.</p>
            <p>Growing up in Torrance didn’t just mean Farrell's Ice Cream Parlour and pizza, but also Children’s Day parties with koinobori (carp streamers). We all watched KWHY-TV and reenacted scenes from Japanese <em>tokusatsus</em> (live action) and robot cartoons like Kikaida, Kamen Rider, and Brave Raideen. My dear grandmother went out of her way one birthday to drive me to a Japanese toy store in Gardena so that I could get the chest missile-firing UFO Senshi Diapolon figure I’d seen on a commercial. I learned how to write my first name in Japanese script after my friend dropped it in a birthday card.</p>
            <p>And in Mrs. Higuchi’s class, we bowed upon entering. We greeted her every day with “Konnichiwa, sensei!” We learned our numbers to ten in Japanese. If you ask me today to name the four primary islands of Japan, I will give them to you in order from northeast to southwest — precisely in the way Mrs. Higuchi taught me. My mom recalls Mrs. Higuchi keeping a very decorative class, complete with live fish swimming in containers that hung from the ceiling. I definitely remember tie-dyeing homemade kimonos as a class — which, let’s be honest, is 1970s California cultural fusion at its finest. It was a special and fond year for me, and my mom knitted Ms. Higuchi a shawl in appreciation.</p>
            <p>I’d always understood that one of the reasons — if not <em>the</em> reason — Mrs. Higuchi shared her culture and taught us rudimentary Japanese was because we had a non-English speaking Japanese girl in our class. If it’s accurate, it’s easy to see how inviting and wonderful of a classroom Mrs. Higuchi’s approach would make for a new, tentative, and perhaps scared foreign language speaker.</p>
            <p>That said, looking up Mrs. Higuchi decades later gave me a deeper appreciation of what she did for me and my classmates. It unveiled why what she did wasn’t perhaps merely about making a more comfortable classroom for a Japanese girl, if my memory is even correct.</p>
            <p>I had always pictured Mrs. Higuchi as being in her early or mid 20s when she was my kindergarten teacher. Kids don’t know better, and I was definitely off on my assessment.</p>
            <p>You see, in 1974, Hatsuko Mary Yoshioka Higuchi was actually 35 years old.</p>
            <p>And when she was just three years old, on 6 August 1942, Hatsuko Yoshioka, her parents, Goro and Kiyoko Yoshioka, and her younger sister, Etsuko, were removed from their South Bay home and arrived at the Poston War Relocation Center, just to the east of the Colorado River in Arizona’s Sonoran Desert, where they were interned for three years as Family Number 42128.</p>
            <p>***</p>
            <p>A standard high school American history textbook will certainly address Japanese-American internment, but it often won’t give a good sense of the rapidity with which it all happened. It will outline the locations of the different internment camps in the American West and Arkansas, but it almost assuredly lacks details on the organizational and bureaucratic players behind the camps, or about the internal politics of the camps themselves.</p>
            <p>The US War Relocation Authority (WRA) started construction on Poston in March, just a month following President Roosevelt’s Executive Order 9066. The camp was still being built in May and June, as the first Japanese American families arrived. Unlike most camps, the incoming families weren’t first filtered through “assembly centers” (like the one at Santa Anita Racetrack). Instead, they came directly from their homes, farms, and nurseries in Southern and Central California. By September, the Poston War Relocation Center was, in effect, Arizona’s third-largest city. Its population was nearly half the size of Tucson, and 40% of Phoenix.</p>
            <p>Poston was different from all of the other Japanese internment camps in one, specific way. It was initially managed jointly by the WRA and the US Office of Indian Affairs (OIA). It was located wholly within the bounds of the Colorado River Indian Reservation, built on Mohave and Chemehuevi land. The two tribes had not wanted to house the camp, but, overruling tribal elders, the WRA and OIA considered Poston a bureaucratic win-win. The WRA got its camp, and the OIA believed it would ultimately reap longer-term infrastructure benefits (like new roads and irrigation systems) without having to break its own budget.</p>
            <p>Ariel Wiebe, a doctoral student at University of New Mexico, recently explained how the OIA saw the internment camp through the lens of social science. The OIA believed that lessons learned from the “colony’s” governance could assist in postwar reconstruction efforts. Wiebe highlights many of the tensions that rarely make it into high school textbooks — camp conflicts between first-generation Japanese immigrants (issei) and second-generation JapaneseAmericans (nisei), labor unrest, fights over wages, and fears of law enforcement informants.</p>
            <p>Poston was actually three separate camp “units” (Poston I, Poston II, and Poston III), eventually barbed wire-fenced as a single entity. Each of the three units were sub-divided into what might be thought of as “neighborhood” blocks, containing about 14 individual housing barracks, a mess hall, communal restrooms and showers, and laundry facilities. In each of the housing barracks, four families were typically allotted 500 square feet of living space each.</p>
            <p>The Yoshioka family was assigned to Poston III, Block 318. This meant one single room — about the size of Mrs. Higuchi’s elementary classroom — for her issei father, kibei nisei mother, and, at first, Hatsuko and her sister. Another sister and brother would be born at Poston, eventually making the family a household of two parents and four Japanese-American children straddling the nisei-sansei divide. (Third-generation Japanese-Americans are often referred to as “sansei.”)</p>
            <p>Like others at Poston, Hatsuko’s parents divided the family’s space into private “bedrooms” with sheets. And for a good part of the year, that space was the only spot to escape the heat. Poston’s average summer daytime temperature runs about 105 degrees, and it’s almost always above 100 between May and September, which makes it unsurprising that Poston I, II, and III were colloquially called “Roasten," "Toasten" and “Dustin.”</p>
            <p>In a 2019 oral history, Mrs. Higuchi’s memories of Poston were understandably fleeting— just like my own memories of her class. Her remembrances centered around the family’s living quarters, the weather, and, unsurprisingly for her age at the time, play: "Well, we played a lot, I know...I don't really remember that much more except for we used to just play around, run around all over. During the daytime, mainly because it was so hot outside, we would play indoors. We would just run through the house and jump on the bed, and we would use it as a, what do you call it, like a trampoline, and we would just jump all around."</p>
            <p>"Well, we didn't have a playground with equipment, swings and so forth, however, we did play outdoors a lot. I guess more indoors because of the heat. I don't know, we enjoyed coloring, paper dolls. But I really don't remember that much more because we were so young at that time, three, four, five."</p>
            <p>Interned at Poston, Mrs. Higuchi’s father, Goro Yoshioka, a first-generation immigrant vegetable hauler, became a mess hall cook. Kiyoko, her mother — who had been born an American citizen in Santa Monica, California, sent back to Japan by an overwhelmed, widowed father at the age of six, and returned in her early twenties to work at a Southern California cannery — settled into the camp’s sewing classes and learned how to knit. Mrs. Higuchi’s most vivid memories were of her mother’s struggles with childrearing and day-to-day domestic life: "[My mother] kept herself pretty busy with the four kids, and everything had to be hand washed, of course. Washing all the diapers, the cloth diapers and always hanging them, and I could remember the rope being strung across the room with clothes dripping onto, the water dripping onto the floor, because she didn't have a wringer or anything, you just did the best you can to get the water out. And mainly because there was severe dust storms in Arizona, so she had to dry most of her clothes indoors."</p>
            <p>"We did go to the mess hall and eat, but my mother found it really difficult because when we went there, we just wanted to play, run around, and not eat. And so she had a hard time getting us to sit down and eat, and caring for the little ones. So oftentimes we would just stay in the barracks and my father would bring home the food for us and we would eat there in the barracks."</p>
            <p>And then, after three years, just as rapidly as it had been constructed and populated, Poston closed.</p>
            <p>A little over 100 days after Japan’s surrender, all of the interned Japanese-Americans at Poston had left. Most took buses or trains back to their old California hometowns. The camp and all its infrastructure then reverted to the Colorado River Indian Reservation, which, in 1945, added Hopi and Navajo tribes to its jurisdiction.</p>
            <p>***</p>
            <p>Mrs. Higuchi dates her family’s return to the South Bay to her start of her first grade year. She was six years old, and had just completed kindergarten at Poston.</p>
            <p>Her family came back with very little, and they returned to very little.</p>
            <p>They hadn’t been allowed many possessions and heirlooms at Poston, so her parents had left boxes and suitcases with a neighbor. Mrs. Higuchi relates what happened when they went to collect their belongings: "...And when we came back, a friend, our neighbor, had said that they would take care of our suitcases and all our boxes of goods that we were not able to take to camp. But when we came back, we went into that barn, and I remember my mother crying because everything was broken into. There were just a few broken dishes, empty boxes all torn apart, there was nothing there. And so all her wedding gifts, I know she was just heartbroken to come back to that."</p>
            <p>Goro Yoshioka didn’t have a truck, so he wasn’t able to get back into vegetable hauling. They, however, had access to land for rent in the South Bay, so Hatusko Yoshioka and her family learned to farm. The years between 1945 and 1951, as Mary Yoshioka wrote to her training teacher, were “the happiest days of [her] life.” This comes across in her oral history, too. Mrs. Higuchi talked of her father’s baked goods, his pier-fishing trips with her brother, Tetsuo, playing tag and hide-and-seek in the cauliflower fields, doing homework and art at the dining room table in the evenings, her mother reading them Japanese stories, and walking alongside her father as he irrigated the crops. The kids went to public school, and as their Japanese names were considered difficult to pronounce, the teachers soon bestowed them more “American” names: Mary (Hatsuko), Betty (Etsuko), Mitzi (Mitsuko), and Jimmy (Tetsuo).</p>
            <p>Her parents saved enough money to purchase their own property, but, in November 1951, right before they could move, Goro Yoshioka, who had been ill on-and-off at Poston, died of heart failure. Mary Yoshioka was in the middle of seventh grade, and during the Christmas holiday, she, her mother, and her three younger siblings moved to Torrance. Kiyoko Yoshioka, now widowed, had a house physically moved from Gardena to Torrance, and — having no other option — transitioned into a life of farming romaine lettuce and cauliflower, and into single parenthood.</p>
            <p>In the face of this, Mary’s mother was adamant — adamant — that her children get a solid education, including piano and Saturday Japanese language classes. “Every day,” Mrs. Higuchi recalled her mother saying, “it was benkyo shinasai, ‘study, study, study,’ benkyo shinasai.”</p>
            <p>And Mary Yoshioka was the oldest child par excellence.</p>
            <p>Farming at home, she became president of the Girls’ League, and in her high school senior year (1956-1957), she was Torrance High’s homecoming queen, rode in the parade celebrating Torrance’s designation as an “All America City,” and, scholarships in hand, she graduated and went to UCLA. That same fall, she’d even had the time to represent Gardena Valley as a Nisei Week Festival Queen contestant and win the local Miss Walteria pageant.</p>
            <p>Mary majored in education, and she didn’t shy away at all from extracurriculars. “I was very active at UCLA,” she said, “I was in social clubs, I was in all the big social clubs that were really popular.” These included the California Club, the Prytanean Women’s Honor Society, and UCLA’s Bruin Belles Service Association. “I was Bruin Belles president,” she added, “where we would go meet the football teams, and any visitors coming[.] [W]e would be the official greeters.” After graduation, she began her student teaching. By the end of 1964, she’d started full-time at John Adams and married Mokoto Higuchi, a recent Loyola law graduate, a fellow UCLA alumnus, and the past president of UCLA’s Nisei Bruins.</p>
            <p>And, by 1974, she’d even made her own splash as an elementary educator. From 1967 to 1970, Mrs. Higuchi and another John Adams teacher, Ms. Karen McDonough, experimented with whether sex-segregated first-grade classrooms helped young boys read sooner and better. Their approach, based on earlier studies from a school in Virginia, made the local papers, which played up the school’s program as a type of “good” and “novel” form of “segregation” in the Torrance Unified School District.</p>
            <p>***</p>
            <p>It’s fuzzy, but I can remember one particular moment of correction in Mrs. Higuchi’s class. Being an oldest child myself, I was very upset that I’d not been able to draw a perfectly round circle for an art project, so I’d cajoled our teacher’s aide to draw one for me. Mrs. Higuchi would have none of it, and told me that no matter the result, I was to be doing my own artwork.</p>
            <p>I wouldn’t learn until long, long after I’d left the South Bay how important art was to her.</p>
            <p>Her mother Kiyoko didn’t enjoy drawing as much as her late father, but Mrs. Higuchi believed she’d inherited her mother’s close attention to “the shapes and beauty of things.” She described her mother as entranced by nature, and — perhaps not surprising given Poston’s history — her mother had a lifelong fascination with Native American culture. Mrs. Higuchi attributed most of her own interest in art, though, to her father. “He used to spend so much time drawing every night with us,” she recalled, “I think probably my interest [in art] comes from his spending time with us in that way.” Goro Yoshioka not only drew, but he worked with his hands. In Poston, he hand-carved birds and butterflies, made vases, and fashioned Sonoran desert ironwood into sculpture.</p>
            <p>And later, Mrs. Higuchi met an artist who invested in her just as her own father had.</p>
            <p>For many years, she taught art classes at Torrance’s Joslyn Recreation Center. It was there, in 1989, that she began a friendship with Henry Fukuhara, the prominent Japanese-American watercolorist and mixed-media artist.</p>
            <p>Born in 1913, Fukuhara demonstrated an early aptitude in art at Santa Monica High School. Financial constraints meant Fukuhara wasn’t able to complete art school, yet, when he was only in his mid-20s, the Automobile Club of Southern California published some of his linoleum-cut block landscape prints, and they were displayed at the Los Angeles Museum. Money was tight, and Fukuhara’s artistry took a more applied turn as he ran a landscaping business and nursery with his brother.</p>
            <p>Then came the war.</p>
            <p>As was Mrs. Higuchi’s family, Fukuhara, his wife, and children were interned, held with over 10,000 other Japanese-Americans at the Manzanar War Relocation Center. There, on the desolate, eastern slope of California’s Sierra Mountains, Fukuhara’s prowess landed him employment in the camp’s drafting department, where he surveyed locations for gravesites. In his spare time, he did his own sketch work. He painted his first watercolor. He also received permission to travel to other internment camps, which he also sketched.</p>
            <p>One way to obtain passage out of the internment camp system was through furlough work, typically on farms facing wartime labor shortages. This is something Fukuhara did on and off. Eventually, by promising to move away from the West Coast, he and his family left Manzanar permanently and moved to Long Island, New York. He published a book of his internment sketches in 1944, which were exhibited for Manzanar’s own residents for two weeks that same year. Thankfully, the California Historical Society has made Fukuhara’s prints available for online viewing.</p>
            <p>Fukuhara, his father, and his brother ran a successful greenhouse business in New York for over 40 years. He didn’t jump back into art until the 1970s, but over that decade and into the next, his watercolors were displayed in galleries and public buildings across Long Island. He retired to Southern California in the late 1980s, moving back into his old, childhood home. Teaching through Santa Monica College’s Emeritus program, he continued to run exhibitions, and offered Thursday and Saturday workshops (or “paint outs”), giving students the opportunity to explore their craft, plein air, around greater Los Angeles.</p>
            <p>“[Fukuhara] really encouraged me,” said Mrs. Higuchi. “I mean, there was a special bonding that occurred and he was just fatherly, like missing my long-gone father.” Mrs. Higuchi describes the kind of mentor everyone hopes for. She began to attend his “paint outs.” He wrote her weekly letters, pointed her to important books and exhibitions, and connected her to other international watercolorists.</p>
            <p>In 1998, Fukuhara — with a core group that included Mrs. Higuchi — began taking students to Manzanar, which became multi-day “paint outs” in and around the former camp. Fukuhara held his Manzanar workshops until 2005, at which time a friend, Al Setton, took over for him. Fukuhara died in 2010, at the age of 96. Mrs. Higuchi described Fukuhara’s approach to the Manzanar “paint-outs”: “[W]e went there for the weekend, and he would do a [demonstration piece] of Mt. Whitney or go to the Manzanar site, and he would paint there. And then he'd let us go off on our own and paint. [A]nd at maybe two o'clock, we would gather together and we [would] critique[.] [W]e would put all our paintings against the buildings at the [Manzanar] visitor center. And we would sit out there and he would [critique] our work, and that was quite an experience.”</p>
            <p>Fukuhara inspired Mrs. Higuchi’s exploration of her own family’s internment experience through mixed media, especially after she retired from Torrance Unified School District in 2003. Her “Executive Order [EO] 9066” collection is the most well-known, with individual paintings from the series shown over the last two decades across museums, exhibition halls, and university galleries in California and the Western United States.</p>
            <p>In her EO 9066 series, Mrs. Higuchi often blurs or sometimes removes the faces from the Japanese American subjects. They’re anonymized, reduced to a sole racial category, reflecting the US government’s disregard of the individual in internment. She weaves barbed wire in and around the figures, framing them on one side or the other with an ever-present guard tower. Some paintings later in her series focus on the child’s world of play in the camps, but no gallerygoer ever escapes the War Department’s order, “Instructions to All Persons of Japanese Ancestry,” What the War Department plastered on posters up and down the West Coast explodes across many of Mrs. Higuchi’s EO 9066 canvasses. It is an essential element of her first five.</p>
            <p>Sometimes, however, Mrs. Higuchi’s use of wartime phrases is part of a more complex, layered conversation. Take, for example, EO 9066 Series 11, which depicts Spider Rock in Arizona’s Canyon de Chelly National Monument. The War Department’s order is there, but it’s barely discernible. You can see some of it buried on the side of a three-table-topped mountain on the right. Glancing to the sharp peak on the left, one finds starkly carved words from a January 1942 newspaper column by Hearst sportswriter Henry McLemore. These words, calling for “herd[ing]” and “pack[ing off] Japanese-Americians to the “Badlands,” are actually emblazoned at Manzanar’s Visitor Center. One easily observes in the painting McLemore’s poisoned conclusion, “Personally, I hate the Japanese. And that goes for all of them.” Ghostly guard towers surveil the camps on each peak. The piece, Mrs. Higuchi explains, was all but complete but for an announcement from the US Department of Justice. In May 2011, President Obama’s Acting Solicitor General publicly admitted that his predecessor, Charles Fahy, hid a 1942 Office of Naval Intelligence report from the Supreme Court in the Hirabayashi, Yasui, and Korematsu cases. The report assessed, at the time, that Japanese Americans on the West Coast didn’t constitute a military threat. Mrs. Higuchi splashed the center of the canvas, in blood red, with language from the ONI report, scrawling parts of her own journal entry about the announcement below it. “Today,” one clearly distinguishes in her cursive, “I feel anger.”</p>
            <p>Mrs. Higuchi’s artwork became a doorway, just slightly ajar, into her mother’s struggles and fears during internment. Kiyoko Yoshioka traveled to Manzanar with her daughter, who laughs as she recalls her mother’s High Desert napping. As for many other elderly Japanese Americans, it was hard for her mother to talk about Poston. Even when Mrs. Higuchi gently asked while painting, she encountered anguished hesitancy. “Hatusko,” her mother would answer, “hanashitakunai” [“I don’t want to talk about it.”]. Mrs. Higuchi would in turn plead, “kodomo no tame ni” [“For the sake of the children.”]. Eventually, Mrs. Higuchi was able to convince her mother to give a few of her thoughts on paper. “Kyampu no koto omoidasu,” [“I remember the camp.”], her mother wrote. Mrs. Higuchi described further: “[her memory of Poston] makes her very sad, mainly because she thought about the children. ‘What's going to happen to my children, kodomotachi?’ [‘The children..’] ‘Kodomotachi,’ she said, We would come home, and come home fighting and crying, and she said, ‘Watashi mo…' [‘I also (was crying)’] When we came home, she, herself, ‘Namida ga, boroboro,’ [‘The tears were pouring out.’] the tears came down her face as well. She just could not help crying, because all she kept thinking is, ‘What is going to happen to my children?’”</p>
            <p>Today, evangelical Christian subculture is fixated on the so-called wrong uses of empathy. Over and over again, one hears about toxic or “untethered” empathy, and how people “manipulate” empathy, or use it for “emotional blackmail.” Sympathy, as two scholars have related, is giving a hand to someone trapped in a pit, while empathy — well, that’s like jumping down into the pit so that you can better relate to “pit living,” or adopting the grievances of everyone unlucky enough to have fallen into a pit. Sympathy, with its proper distance and boundaries, is taken as a good. Empathy, on the other hand, they say, leads to “emotional fusion.” It assumes any and all victims are in the right, regardless of what those victims stand for.</p>
            <p>I can’t tell you how much I despise this debate.</p>
            <p>This war on empathy has done little but produce a greater coarsening among fellow Christians in the public square, particularly when one talks about the more distasteful, horrible moments of American history. It’s become much, much too easy for conservative Christians to yell “toxic empathy!” or “woke!” or “emotional fusion!” in any debates that could involve race, class, civil rights, or political liberalism. I’m even willing to wager that some of my fellow conservative Christians take this very article as tacking in the direction of “toxic empathetic” — intentionally manipulative — for no other reason than it addresses internment.</p>
            <p>Setting empathy against sympathy is, in the end, a losing tactic.</p>
            <p>Mrs. Higuchi never invited our kindergarten class to "actively share” in her family’s lasting emotional pain. She never addressed her family’s internment to engender pity or allow us to acknowledge pain from a safe distance. Again, I’d not known at all about her early childhood experience until looking her up decades later. To the best I can recall, she didn't read to us any books about Japanese-American internment. (In fact, it doesn’t appear there were any kindergarten-level picture books on internment until the early 1990s.)</p>
            <p>I think we can all agree there are very few, if truly any, hand-holds for kindergarteners when it comes to Japanese-American internment. You can't teach them <em>Korematsu vs. United States</em>. You can't teach them Judge Robert Jackson’s warning about how criminalizing inheritable characteristics is a “loaded weapon” in the wrong government hands. You’re not going to get them to understand whether the judicial validity of a curfew order should have been extended to forcible relocation. There's something to be said about age-appropriateness, ensuring young students know America’s good and true ideals deep deep down, which makes the cracking and breaking of those ideals ever more striking to kids as they mature.</p>
            <p>There was nothing remotely directly or indirectly “woke” or “leftist” about Mrs. Higuchi’s public school classroom. There was no “indoctrination.” It wasn’t about empathy or sympathy. Instead, it was something much more classically humanist — perhaps even personalist. She taught love. She taught us to love Japanese language, to love Japanese culture, and to love Japanese Americans, and to love them all <em>together with one another</em>. She taught us to love <em>diﬀerence</em>, to <em>honor</em> difference, to <em>enjoy</em> difference, and enjoy it <em>together with one another</em>. She taught us <em>connection together with one another</em>.</p>
            <p>Her classroom acknowledged the South Bay’s Japanese-American community. Her approach said, “Our community is worth bringing into the classroom. Our language and traditions are worth bringing into the classroom.” To a first-generation, non-English speaking Japanese girl, her message was “I want you to be at ease, so I'm going to teach all of these other students some of <em>your</em> language, and I want you to know that it is ok for you to speak your language here.” To those in the class, like me, who weren’t of Japanese heritage, it was, “Behold your neighbors. They are who you live around. Love them and appreciate them for who they are.”</p>
            <p>That is how you teach kindergartners about internment.</p>
            <p>You teach them to love and honor their neighbors, the neighbors’ heritage, and their neighbors’ culture, and in doing so, you make the concept of internment incomprehensible to them — impossible to even countenance. Moreover, again as they mature, you also make them horror-struck that it was ever considered a viable option to begin with.</p>
            <p>“What is going to happen to my children?” One doesn’t have to declare oneself for Tribe Empathy or Tribe Sympathy to know — really know — a mother’s lament. Hers is a quintessential human fear, and it’s not difficult at all to grasp how much pain it caused her at Poston.</p>
            <p>Mrs. Higuchi has continued to give talks over the last decade or so. She has visited other internment camp sites, attended pilgrimages, sat on internment-related panel discussions, and painted. She still tells her audiences about her mother’s overall hesitancy (“I don’t want to talk about it.”), but she emphasizes to them, as she did to her mother, it <em>is</em> something that we all must talk about, <em>kodomo no tame ni</em> [“For the sake of the children.”].</p>
            <p>In today's atmosphere, one could offer an even greater sense of urgency -- <em>Kore wa shinken ni hanasanakereba narimasen</em> [“We must talk seriously about this.”]</p>
            <p>For me and for my fellow public school kindergarteners, Mrs. Higuchi fit a larger world into a Torrance elementary classroom. It made 1974 a little less auspicious for all of us.</p>
            <p>And it was lovely.</p>
            <p><em>Mukashi osewa ni natta koto o, ima mo taihen kansha shite orimasu</em>.</p>
            <p class="contributor-bio">Brian J. Auten is an independent scholar of Cold War strategy, politics, and religion. He is the author of <em>Carter’s Conversion: The Hardening of American Defense Policy</em> (University of Missouri Press, 2008).</p>
          </section>

          <!-- ====== ARTICLE 2 ====== -->
          <section id="understanding-anxiety-with-soren" class="content-section">
            <h2 class="section-title">Understanding Anxiety with Soren Kierkegaard</h2>
            <p class="essay-author">By Bradley Blaylock</p>

            <p>Our culture has a thriving self-help market. Resources, mindfulness practices, and therapeutic discourse have become normalized. In this climate, anxiety has gained a sort of dual role. Anxiety can now function as both a diagnosis of genuine unwellness, and it also functions in a way that shows someone’s cultural awareness.</p>
            <p>In much of the self-help literature, though, anxiety is seen as something to eliminate as swiftly as possible. But what if in our rush to eliminate anxiety we are missing a chance at moral engagement and serious meaning-making? Perhaps anxiety is not simply a problem to be overcome, but a pathway to unlock a deeper understanding of the self and moral formation. This is the proposition of Soren Kierkegaard. The 19th-century Danish Philosopher offered valuable insights that can both challenge us and offer insights to us in our choice-saturated world.</p>
            <p>In moral philosophy, Soren Kierkegaard’s concept of anxiety is one of the more provocative ideas. For our purposes here, I hope to show that Kierkegaard’s concept of anxiety is particularly helpful in discussions of conscience and moral formation. While the traditional understanding of the conscience and Kierkegaard’s understanding of anxiety are not altogether the same, the two can work in concert to provide meaningful moral insights.</p>
            <h3 class="section-heading">Kierkegaard’s Understanding of Anxiety</h3>
            <p>Kierkegaard distinguishes between “fear” and “anxiety.” Fear for Kierkegaard is directed towards a specific object or threat. Anxiety, however, is a more diffuse existential condition related to freedom of choice. He famously speaks of anxiety as the “dizziness of freedom.” Here Kierkegaard evokes feelings similar to staring over a cliff face. The pit of unease one feels at discomforting heights is similar to the anxiety Kierkegaard posits all humans feel when confronting the vastness of human freedom and all the possibilities this entails. In his work, “The Concept of Anxiety,” Kierkegaard states, “Anxiety is the dizziness of freedom, which emerges when the spirit wants to posit the synthesis and freedom looks down into its possibility, laying hold of finiteness to support itself.” This statement reveals a few key aspects of understanding Kierkegaard’s concept of anxiety. First, anxiety is inextricably related to freedom. It could be said that for Kierkegaard, anxiety is the psychological manifestation of our capacity to choose. Second, anxiety is felt when we become aware of the possibilities before us and when we become aware of the personal responsibility we have for our decisions. Last, anxiety focuses on the paradoxical relationship between the infinite (our freedom and possibilities) and the finite (our actual choices).</p>
            <p>Kierkegaard defines anxiety similarly in earlier sections of his work, “Anxiety is freedom’s actuality as the possibility of possibility.” Here we see clearly that for Kierkegaard anxiety is not just a byproduct of freedom, but it is the existential experience of freedom inherent to human nature. It is through experiencing anxiety that we become self-aware that we are decision-making creatures who have the capacity to shape our lives and the contexts around us.</p>
            <p>This understanding of anxiety is quite different from what we often find in modern conceptions. Anxiety is often seen as a purely negative state that happens to an individual and is something to overcome. Kierkegaard, though, sees anxiety as an inescapable reality of life and is something to confront and go through to get to moral formation, not something to eliminate.</p>
            <h3 class="section-heading">Anxiety’s Relationship to Sin</h3>
            <p>Kierkegaard posits that anxiety has a unique relationship to sin. According to Kierkegaard, anxiety <em>qua</em> anxiety is not sin. It is however intimately related to sin. Kierkegaard argued that anxiety is the psychological state that precedes and even makes possible the leap into sin.</p>
            <p>makes this argument from his interpretation of the fall of Adam and Eve. He argued that before eating the forbidden fruit Adam experienced anxiety related to God’s prohibition. Kierkegaard writes, “the prohibition induces in him anxiety.” Another way to think of his statement here is that moral law creates anxiety. Without moral law, there is no possibility of transgressing the moral law. Once moral law is acknowledged, the possibility of transgressing the moral law becomes known. This state of knowing one can commit transgressions gives way to anxiety.</p>
            <p>This could be described as a precondition for the possibility of sin. Kierkegaard makes this concept clearer by stating that, “the history of the individual life proceeds in a movement from state to state. Every state is posited by a leap.” The leap here that Kierkegaard is referencing is a leap into sin. This leap is a moment of individual decision that moves the individual from the possibility of transgression into actual transgression. A leap from innocence to guilt.</p>
            <p>Now, let’s set aside the theological question that arises as to whether or not anxiety could have existed before the fall. I will leave that question to the theologians. But if we assume the definition of anxiety, we find an important insight into the existential dimension of moral choice. Kierkegaard’s idea of anxiety suggests to us that our ethical decisions are not made in a neutral state, but instead, they are made against a backdrop of fundamental freedom and the anxiety that it generates. Moral decision-making and moral formation is not just about knowing right from wrong, but it is also fundamentally about confronting the possibilities of our freedom.</p>
            <h3 class="section-heading">Anxiety and Self-Awareness</h3>
            <p>Kierkegaard’s concept of anxiety is also tied strongly to his understanding of self-awareness and self-understanding. Kierkegaard believed that anxiety served as a catalyst for causing self-reflection. This self-reflection forces the individual to confront both his potential as well as his limitations. Kierkegaard elaborates on this thought in, “The Sickness Unto Death” saying, “The self is a relation that relates itself to itself.” This explanation of the self unpacks Kierkegaard’s idea that the human consciousness is by nature reflexive. Dogs do not think about their thinking, but under Kierkegaard’s understanding of the self, thinking about our thinking is what makes human consciousness unique. It’s an awareness of our own awareness.</p>
            <p>Anxiety is a critical aspect of this self-relation. It could be said that in Kierkegaard’s work anxiety is the self ’s knowledge of self-possibility. Kierkegaard claims that “anxiety is a qualification of dreaming spirit, and as such it has its place in psychology. Awake, the difference between myself and my other is poised; sleeping, it is suspended; dreaming, it is an intimate nothing.” This highlights the notion that anxiety occurs in the space between who we are currently and who we could be. This space potentially provides opportunity for reflection and growth.</p>
            <p>This function of anxiety is in some ways similar to the role of conscience in moral reflection. Anxiety and conscience both serve as internal mechanisms that prompt self-assessment and moral reflection. One key difference though, is that conscience often operates via judgment of past actions through feelings of guilt or approval. Anxiety operates in a more forward-thinking way, with more concern towards the possibilities before the individual.</p>
            <p>Self-reflection brought on by anxiety over the self ’s possibility is not always comfortable. In fact, it can be outright unsettling. But this is the exact type of discomfort that Kierkegaard thought made anxiety such a powerful tool for personal growth and moral formation.</p>
            <h3 class="section-heading">Anxiety and Moral Development</h3>
            <p>Probably Kierkegaard’s most well-known idea is his stages of life’s existence. Kierkegaard argued there were three stages: the aesthetic, the ethical, and the religious. Each stage represents different ways an individual can engage with life in search of meaning-making. Each stage of life is a more profound and authentic mode of existence than the former.</p>
            <p>Anxiety plays a crucial role in moving from one stage to another. Remember, anxiety is the backdrop that marks our freedom and our knowledge of our own possibility. So then, it must be anxiety that spurs us on to move from one stage to the next as we confront the limitations and deficiencies of our current mode of living. Facing the anxiety of our own freedom forces us to confront who we are currently, and who we want to be, and spurs us to make a leap from one to the other.</p>
            <p>Those living in the aesthetic sphere live for pleasure and immediate sensations. Usually, this sphere is marked by an avoidance of commitment and responsibility. Anxiety in this stage is likely to manifest in individuals as a sense of dissatisfaction and emptiness with their mode of existence. As this anxiety continues to grow, it can motivate the individual to make a move toward the ethical stage.</p>
            <p>The ethical stage is a life marked by taking moral responsibility seriously. It’s a serious attempt at being a good person. The problem with existing in the ethical sphere though, is that nobody lives up to their own ethics. As an individual begins to recognize that the actuality of their moral life is not in congruence with their moral ideals, anxiety begins to bubble up. When this anxiety makes it clear that even our best efforts are not enough, it can push an individual to take the final leap into the religious sphere. The individual who makes a leap into the religious sphere has begun to reckon themselves with the divine.</p>
            <p>This development, which is in large part moral, demonstrates the value of anxiety in moral formation discussions. In this way, anxiety’s moral function is starkly different from that of the conscience. The conscience often acts as a voice guiding us in distinguishing between right and wrong moral actions. Anxiety, however, pushes us to question and transcend our moral framework until it matches the divine.</p>
            <h3 class="section-heading">The Existential Dimension of Anxiety</h3>
            <p>Anxiety was not in Kierkegaard’s system purely a psychological state, but instead, anxiety is something fundamental to human existence. “This anxiety belongs so essentially to the child that he cannot do without it.” This quote highlights the fundamentalness of anxiety according to Kierkegaard. Anxiety is fundamentally related to our nature as finite, free creatures, who are capable of sin.</p>
            <p>According to Kierkegaard, one of the most intense sources of anxiety is our confrontation with our own impending death. Awareness of our own mortality forces us to face the finitude of our existence. This heightens the felt responsibility for our choices. We have to recognize at some point the reality that there simply isn’t time. You may never have a chance to reconcile with those you have wronged. Mortality heightens the responsibility for our choices. As Kierkegaard writes in The Sickness Unto Death,” “When death is the greatest danger, one hopes for life; but when one becomes acquainted with an even more dreadful danger, one hopes for death.” This quote highlights the seriousness of the fear of death and points to the reality that there is only one thing one must have more anxiety over, existing before God.</p>
            <p>This existential anxiety is not, however, a purely negative thing for Kierkegaard. It’s seen by Kierkegaard as a catalyst for spiritual and moral growth. Kierkegaard famously says, “To venture causes anxiety, but not to venture is to lose oneself.” Here Kierkegaard is affirming that we can only become truly ourselves when we confront our anxiety by making choices in the face of our knowledge of self-possibility.</p>
            <p>The existential aspect of anxiety adds a deeper layer to our understanding of moral formation. Kierkegaard's understanding of anxiety as related to morality suggests that our ethical choices are not simply about following rules, but are more about the kind of self we want to cultivate through our decisions. Working in concert with anxiety, conscience can be seen not simply as a moral judge, but as a critique of our most basic projection of self.</p>
            <h3 class="section-heading">Anxiety, Faith, and Transcendence</h3>
            <p>To the surprise of no one, anxiety also plays a crucial role in religious experience and faith in Kierkegaard's conception of it. Kierkegaard sees anxiety as a prerequisite to authentic religious decisions. This is because it is only via anxiety that we become aware of our need for transcendence.</p>
            <p>Kierkegaard famously asserted that faith is a leap and that the move into faith can be known as a leap of faith. This leap of faith is connected with his understanding of anxiety. If faith is to be understood as a leap of faith then it must be understood in light of being comfortable with risk and uncertainty. He puts it this way, “Faith is the contradiction between the infinite passion of inwardness and the objective uncertainty.” Though this leap is not blind and free of reason it is a leap made with awareness of the impossibility of certainty.</p>
            <p>Necessarily, anxiety plays a critical role in this leap. In the first place, it is through anxiety that we come to realize our need for faith. It is anxiety that creates within us existential angst that only a leap of faith can address. If, as said earlier, anxiety is the dizziness of freedom, then we should say that this dizzying anxiety creates the space for faith.</p>
            <p>This perspective on anxiety promises to be an interesting complement to understanding the conscience. If the conscience is a thing to be shaped to match God's moral law, then anxiety is what makes us receptive to that voice of conscience in the first place.</p>
            <h3 class="section-heading">Comparison with the Concept of Conscience</h3>
            <p>Kierkegaard’s understanding of anxiety contains both interesting similarities and important distinctions from popular and traditional notions of the conscience. Both conscience and anxiety act as internal mechanisms that have the potential to prompt moral reflection and foster spiritual growth, and moral formation. Both conscience and anxiety involve one degree or another of self-awareness and an engagement of right and wrong.</p>
            <p>However, there are some key differences between conscience and anxiety. Traditionally, conscience is understood as a specific moral faculty. This understanding of conscience usually presents it as having the faculty of judging whether a particular action or action is right or wrong. Anxiety, for Kierkegaard, is a more subtle existential condition inherent to the human condition that underlies all of our free choice.</p>
            <p>Further, conscience typically can be seen as looking backward in order to judge past actions or provide future guidance on actions. Anxiety, however, is purely forward-looking in nature. Anxiety offers less to say about specific moral judgments, but anxiety has more to do with the overarching project of becoming an authentic self.</p>
            <p>Even with these differences though, Kierkegaard's concept of anxiety can and should complement our understanding of the conscience. Anxiety reminds us that our moral decision-making is not simply about applying principles of ethical rules but about confronting the fundamental questions of who we are currently and who we want to become. Anxiety can help us hear that voice of conscience not as a nagging voice to quiet, but as an expression of our existential state as humans. Anxiety can prompt us forward on our journey of self-becoming.</p>
            <h3 class="section-heading">Contemporary Relevance and Applications</h3>
            <p>Before we apply Kierkegaard’s thoughts to our age, the question first must be asked, “Is Kierkegaard’s understanding of anxiety compatible with modern understandings?” I believe yes. Though shrouded in unique language, I believe there are several similarities. Both Kierkegaard and modern sentiments view anxiety as a universal human experience. According to both, anxiety is not abnormal to the human experience. Kierkegaard’s concept of anxiety is uniquely tied to possibility and to the responsibility of choice. This is strikingly similar to modern conceptions. We may couch it in a different language, but modern views of anxiety center around the uncertainty of the future and the decision-making process. In this way, both Kierkegaard and we tie anxiety to our awareness of future possibilities and our choices.</p>
            <p>People today are overloaded with information. It has become common to be constantly plugged in. Artificially speaking, people are more connected today than they have ever been. They are more connected to the news of their local, state, national, and global community than they have ever been. In our overly connected world, the abundance of choice and possibility can be overwhelming. This only intensifies the dizziness we experience when looking at such possibilities. For many, this has led to a perpetual state of low-grade anxiety that relates to decision fatigue, fear of missing out, and fear of a better offer. Kierkegaard's understanding of anxiety can help us to see this anxiety in our day not just as something to be medicated out of our psyche but instead as an invitation to deeper moral reflection and more authentic choice-making. Knowing that anxiety is not a thing to be dismissed but something we must reckon with, we can stare these vast possibilities in their face, knowing they are a natural part of human existence. We can then make decisions with confidence.</p>
            <p>As our world becomes ever more digital and technologically connected, Kierkegaard's understanding of anxiety becomes more and more pertinent. Social media and the rest of the internet have exponentially expanded possibility in our lives. We have more information and more connections than ever before. This has not quietened the backdrop of anxiety, but instead allowed it to become more noticeable. Social media platforms are a breeding ground for comparison, distorted self-presentation, and authenticity.</p>
            <p>Kierkegaard pointed out that our anxiety is uniquely tied to our self-awareness. Anxiety draws our attention to the reality of possibility. Acknowledging the reality of possibility allows us to live a more authentic life in a socially digital age. Reckoning with our anxiety moves us to honestly assess who we are and who we could be. Think of how free to be authentic a person could be if they recognized their potential to become! There needs to be no comparison because I know I can grow. There is no need to distort the representation of my reality because I know that my reality now is not necessarily equivalent to my future reality.</p>
            <p>We also are now often more aware of injustice than we have ever been before. Due to the expansion of the internet, we now have access to any and all stories of injustice. A robbery three states over would have never been on one’s radar sixty years ago, but now it’s possible that it is in front of your very face. Complex ethical issues are constantly being thrown in our faces. There can be a real sense of anxiety that permeates from this knowledge of injustice around us. Kierkegaard’s concept of anxiety as a precursor to moral growth helps us see this sense of dread at injustice not as something to cower from, but something to spur us to act. Our anxiety over the injustice of the world is an invitation to embody Christ.</p>
            <p>Perhaps the most relevant observation of Kierkegaard for us in the contemporary world is the anxiety of confronting the self ’s possibility. If we pause and think about it, we know that we could be a radically different person ten years from now. I sense this anxiety in younger generations regularly. Who will I become? What will my life look like in ten years? Am I doing what I’m “supposed” to be doing? This anxiety that many of us feel is exactly what Kierkegaard is getting at. We are often taught to simply ignore this anxiety. Just let life come! While there is something to be said for that, on some level, anxiety over the self ’s possibility is a good thing. The call of the Christian life is to die to self and to live as Christ. It is only when we recognize the possibility of ourselves that we can decide to shape the self. We will only then realize ourselves fully before God. Knowing the potential of our possibility is a call to cultivate holy habits. Reckoning with and making spiritually forming choices in the face of this anxiety is a better path to spiritual formation than ignoring or massaging the anxiety.</p>
            <p>If anxiety can be viewed not simply as a problem to defeat but as a fundamental aspect of human existence, then I believe Kierkegaard offers insights that can deepen and enrich our understanding of moral formation.</p>
            <p>It would be good to remember that our ethical choices are not made in a vacuum, but instead against a backdrop of our fundamental freedom and the anxiety produced from it. Ultimately by bringing in Kierkegaard’s understanding of anxiety into conversations of moral formation with the conscience, we develop a richer and more nuanced understanding of moral formation. This combined perspective offers us a vision of the ethical life that is not simply following rules but is fundamentally about the project of selfbecoming in the face of possibility. As we attempt to navigate moral formation in our own day perhaps Kierkegaard's insights on anxiety can be helpful.</p>
            <p class="contributor-bio">Bradley Blaylock serves as a professor of theological studies at Highlands College in Birmingham, AL. He is a PhD candidate at Southwestern Baptist Theological Seminary studying the philosophy of religion.</p>
          </section>

          <!-- ====== ARTICLE 3 ====== -->
          <section id="homeschooling-is-more-normal" class="content-section">
            <h2 class="section-title">Homeschooling Is More Normal than You Think</h2>
            <p class="essay-author">By Tessa Carman</p>

            <p class="essay-heading">Dixie Dillon Lane. <em>Skipping School: A History of American Homeschooling and How It Went Mainstream</em>. Eerdmans, 2026. $28.99. 216 pp.</p>
            <p>It was a sunny March day, nearly seventy degrees, so I packed up the four kids, ages ten months to ten years, and we drove to the state park. The older two had dressed the three-year-old and packed food and water. They noticed old nests tucked in the trees as we walked the trails. We remarked how the world was ready for spring, and that new nests would soon be built. Every once in a while, my three-year-old stopped us so that we could listen to the birds.</p>
            <p>As energy flagged and water ran low, we recited some poems, nursery rhymes, and folk songs to keep our spirits up. Later we explored the interpretive center, where we read about the history of Machicomoco Park and happened upon three archeologists at work; they welcomed us and answered the kids’ questions. We concluded our exploration with a walk onto a dock, where we looked for oysters and other salt marsh critters and stared at the glinting river water.</p>
            <p>It was one of those days that fill me with gratitude that we get to homeschool. None of this would have been possible had the kids jumped on the school bus that morning and spent most of the day inside. I also enjoy the game of translating the things they learn outside of formal school into educational jargon: today, for instance, we worked on environmental stewardship, problem solving and group dynamics, perseverance, hands-on historical study, research initiative, critical thinking, situational preparedness, familial bonding, local history, social and neighborly skills, immersive natural experience—you get the idea.</p>
            <p>Modern American homeschooling has entered a new era quite different from its beginnings in 1950s America, where “living room schools” existed in a legal gray area. Compulsory universal education as we understand it today—that is, the state requirement to attend an approved school between ages five and eighteen—was still rather new in the mid-twentieth century. Still, it was sufficiently established that, by contrast, schooling at home was clearly countercultural (unlike, say, Abraham Lincoln-period homeschooling), and sometimes even illegal.</p>
            <p>Now, in our post-Covid era, homeschooling has entered the mainstream. Homeschoolers are still a minority, but a significant one, as demonstrated by the Homeschool Days at the National Gallery in Washington, D.C., and Mount Vernon (not to mention innumerable podcasts, curriculum providers, and homeschool conventions and consultants). People no longer look askance if you take your school-age children to Costco on a weekday morning (though if you bring more than three kids, they might). Some states provide funds for homeschooled families to support their children’s education. If globe-trotting is your thing, you can even “worldschool,” spending most of your school year studying California Redwoods, Niagara Falls, or Tuscany onsite. Or you can simply travel to England with your family to visit Charles Dickens’s grave while your teenager reads <em>A Tale of Two Cities</em> in his online literature class (as some of my students have done).</p>
            <p>How did this shift happen? And why should we care about the story of homeschooling and our homeschooling neighbors? American historian and homeschooling mother of four Dixie Dillon Lane answers these questions in her new book, <em>Skipping School: A History of American Homeschooling and How It Went Mainstream</em>. Lane’s project moves beyond sensationalist and alarmist opinion reporting on homeschooling. Instead, she soberly presents a clear narrative of homeschooling that sheds light both on American society today and, through the stories of real families, on the motivations of actual homeschoolers. Lane shows that religious motivations are only one factor. Homeschooling in America today is “the site of a flourishing range of pedagogies, curricula, and experiments in education,” reflecting the diversity of Americans.</p>
            <p>***</p>
            <p>This would be an important book even if it were dryly written. But thankfully, Lane writes with humor, clarity, and ease. And one great benefit to Lane’s study is that she is both scholar and practitioner. Like Catherine Ruth Pakaluk and her parents-of-five-plus-children subjects in <em>Hannah’s Children</em>, Lane is both scholar and insider. This allows her to gather valuable information from her subjects that an outsider (especially a hostile one!) would not be able to. This doesn’t mean that outsider perspectives are not needed; rather, insider perspectives are sorely lacking in society at large. Lane’s book fills that gap in a scholarly and winsome way. As she writes: “Surely we can examine the homeschooling phenomenon across time in a way that is analytically robust and evidence-based but is also respectful of the experiences of those who actually lived it.” “We can do better” than the run-of-the-mill clickbait takes, she adds. And she does.</p>
            <p>Lane opens with a scene that could be the inspiration for a movie: A mother of seven is on trial in Los Angeles in 1953 for not sending her oldest children to school. She’s asked, “Are you a Communist?”</p>
            <p>Lane notes that the 1950s and ‘60s were generally characterized by “domestic conflict and suspicion.” If you weren’t sending your kids to school, maybe you were a Communist. Or maybe you took your kids out of school because you were afraid of Communism. Or maybe you welcomed more state oversight because, well, Communism. Such ideas were in the air, all while the nation sought to strengthen its defense, both ideologically and otherwise, against the Soviet threat. Schools were a prime domain for strengthening national defense— hence the increased consolidation. Steadily and surely, local communities had less and less say in what went on in public schools, and children were more isolated from their families (a Communist dream, one might note).</p>
            <p>Lane situates the story of homeschooling in America, then, within a broader context that brings out perhaps her greatest insight: Modern homeschooling is part of a long American tradition of parental involvement in children’s education and community that began arguably with the Massachusetts School Laws of the 1640s.</p>
            <p>Indeed, public schooling has changed significantly, arguably with each decade, especially since the 1925 Supreme Court decision in <em>Pierce v. the Society</em> <em>of Sisters</em> that upheld the right of parents to have primary say in where their children are educated. This decision overturned an Oregon law that required parents to send children to public schools within their district; <em>Pierce</em> thus preserve the right of parents to send their children to private schools as well. “The child is not the mere creature of the State,” wrote Justice James McReynolds. And further: “The fundamental theory of liberty upon which all governments in this Union repose excludes any general power of the State <em>to standardize its children</em> by forcing them to accept instruction from public teachers only” (emphasis added).</p>
            <p>Lane makes an argument that may seem surprising at first glance: <em>Homeschooling is more normal than you think</em>. That is, homeschooling is part of a continuous, though sometimes suppressed, American belief in (and desire for) “parental influence and local control of education.” In this context, homeschooling is just one aspect of an overall trend in American life: we dwell in a tension between the steady increase of state control and the continual resurgence of resistance to state overreach, and all the debates and battles and distinctions in between.</p>
            <p>Tracing the story chronologically, Lane brings us from the Soviet scare era and the time of under-the-radar homeschooling to its legal recognition and the growth of homeschool cooperatives, to the Covid era where homeschooling gained new adherents, to today’s community-based homeschoolers, who reflect the diversity of America. Her history of American homeschooling helpfully complicates the narrative of the history of American public schooling, which is one of increased overall consolidation. Homeschooling is one thread of resistance to the expanding consolidation. It forces us to ask hard questions about what good education actually means for our children, in and out of public schools, and what the good life means for our communities. In other words, what <em>is</em> actually good for our kids, and not just convenient for society?</p>
            <p>***</p>
            <p>The stories Lane shares of first- and secondgeneration homeschooling families are some of the best parts of the book. For example, there’s Skylar, a high-achieving scholar who graduated college at age nineteen and is now a second-generation homeschooling parent. She found out she had severe ADHD only in her last year of college. Because of the personalized path her parents gave her, those things hadn’t become labels to keep her back from any successes. The flexibility of homeschooling (“unschooling” in Skylar’s case) had played to her strengths and strengthened her weaknesses. One can only imagine what would have happened if she had been stamped and ticketed from an early age.</p>
            <p>This points to one larger issue that homeschooling brings to the fore: sphere sovereignty. In a well-ordered society, children are not pitted against parents; men are not pitted against women; cities do not siphon resources from rural areas; and one’s love of family, neighbor, culture, church, stranger, and nation are not in conflict. The history of American education shows what happens when spheres become disordered, and when systems become more important than persons. Skylar’s story, by contrast, shows what can happen when parents who know and love their children have the freedom to fulfill their duties by them—and do what will help their children thrive.</p>
            <p>Lane mentions fear as a typical reason people assume homeschoolers do what they do. She notes an undercurrent of fear, for example, at a homeschooling conference, while also noting the need for homeschoolers to network in order to defend their rights from state overreach (this is how the Home School Legal Defense Association began). Her examination shows how important it is to distinguish <em>what</em> is feared (low academic standards? gang violence? bullying?), how proportional the fear is, and whether the response is reasonable and just. For example, public school parents today are fighting against the undermining of parental authority in California, where educators are prohibited from “sharing details about children’s gender identity with parents.” These parents have much in common with some homeschooling and private school parents. Both groups want to respect and support parental authority; they want their children to be taught in a context that respects them as persons rather than isolating and manipulating them.</p>
            <p>This brings us to another key point Lane makes: Today’s homeschoolers are <em>postinstitutional</em>. I thought this an interesting term, since it’s not clear that a typical homeschooler in the pioneering days of the 1950s and 1960s could precisely be termed <em>anti-institutional</em>, unless <em>institution</em> were mainly understood as bureaucratic. Granted, the twentieth century was a time of great institutional skepticism, but there is a difference between suspicion or opposition to an institution as such and to a diseased specific instantiation. One can be supportive of the institution of marriage and support a wife who leaves an abusive husband. Similarly, many families have had clear reasons for pulling their children out of schools for a time. The family is a more fundamental institution than the school and indeed the state. Families desire a healthy village life for their children—and sometimes a healthy village has to be rebuilt.</p>
            <p>To put it another way, if someone moves out of a crumbling house to live in a tent, one is not necessarily anti-house. Rather, one recognizes that the house is crumbling and perhaps has a bad foundation, so a new one needs to be built.</p>
            <p>***</p>
            <p>But to be <em>postinstitutional</em> is a bit more complicated than that. Once social stigma decreased for homeschoolers, families have continued to network and build their own associations—and institutions. One example of this is Catholic families who choose to homeschool <em>instead</em> of sending their children to parish schools, because the education at the latter is hardly different from district schools and therefore seen as not worth the money. There is a whole other story to be told about dying parish schools revived by joining the classical movement—and of homeschooled families who have banded together to start alternative schools (Chesterton Academy high schools, which are Catholic and classical, are one example). Last but not least, I suspect the increasing reliance on screens in many public and some private schools will offer an increasingly common incentive for families to opt out of screen saturation to seek true personalized attention from actual persons by homeschooling or joining alternative movements like Waldorf and traditional classical schools.</p>
            <p>The disintegration of community in the twentieth century is a significant background factor to the story of homeschooling. It would take another study to examine how the upheavals of the twentieth-century affected the fabric of American society and of schooling choices: Not only did we see two world wars and the Soviet scare, but also unprecedented technological changes to everyday life. Consider rural electrification, radio and television, the colonization of roadways by the automobile, the rural-to-city movement, and more—and all this even before the introduction of the personal computer and the World Wide Web. The story of homeschooling and of American society cannot be understood apart from these changes.</p>
            <p>Many homeschooling families desire to <em>rebuild a village</em>; in a more rooted time, this wouldn’t be such a focus. But nowadays, many homeschoolers want their children and their whole family to be part of a more organic, human culture. They want to know their neighbors, and they want their children to hang out not just with children their own age (as they would at school), but with kids of many ages and stages. One homeschooling couple I know are now grandparents. They told me how their children used to do chores for elderly neighbors. Now that these children are adults, they still count those elderly neighbors among their closest friends. Many families see public school as an obstacle to precisely the more thickened community they desire for their children, or to the proper development and personalized attention their sons and daughters need.</p>
            <p>One of my favorite vignettes from Lane’s book is of Rodney and Renée Brooks, an African American family who chose to live in an “impoverished neighborhood” of South Central Los Angeles in order to serve their neighbors. Rodney, an Independent Baptist minister and police lieutenant, and Renée taught their children at home. They didn’t send their children to district schools because they didn’t want them to grow up with “the violent gang mind.” But they would invite the neighbor kids— many from broken homes—over to their house after school hours to give them a safe haven and the experience of a loving home. Renée would require “clean language and respectful behavior,” and she would tell the kids, if they behave, “we’re going to bake cookies.”</p>
            <p>The drive toward total control over society and the rejection of proper levels of authority — including the family—affect us all. This culture transforms what a school and society could be. This is why both homeschooling families and people like a friend of mine in Chicago, a regenerative farming expert involved at her children’s public school, are batting for the same team. Both want to raise children who are curious and competent, who have ordered loves and respect for legitimate authority, but are not easily controlled shills for a totalitarian state.</p>
            <p>We live in a strange time, where fundamental relational tasks like teaching, care of children, and care for the sick and the elderly are respected only when professionalized and performed by someone in a paid position. The history of homeschooling, however, shows it to be a crucial part of the timeless civilizational project of caring for our families and loving our neighbor. And so, homeschooling matters because by its very nature—in all its non-standardized, messy, unwieldy glory—it is a movement against the professionalization, standardization, and replacement of the human being.</p>
            <p class="contributor-bio">Tessa Carman is a homeschooling mother, teacher, and writer. She is a member of the editorial board of Mere Orthodoxy.</p>
          </section>

