          <section id="what-is-union-with" class="content-section">
            <h2 class="section-title">What is Union with Christ?</h2>
            <p class="essay-author">By Joshua Heavin</p>

            <p>Union with Christ is at the very heart of the Christian faith, yet its meaning and implications are lost upon the vast majority of Christians today. What is union with Christ, and why does it matter in the Christian life?</p>
            <p>Through faith, the Holy Spirit unites us with the incarnate Son of God in his death, resurrection, and ascension, such that we who are in Christ know God as our Father. Union with Christ is thus first and foremost about knowing Christ, and with him, all his saving benefits.</p>
            <p>Union with Christ is simultaneously profound and inexhaustible, yet extremely basic and easily understandable. On one hand, union with Christ is a profound mystery because the whole constellation of all divinely revealed Christian doctrines converge upon and inter-relate in our union with Christ: the Trinity, the person and work of Christ, the church, the sacraments, and far more! Union with Christ is described in Holy Scripture with a wide range of expressions and images, and various aspects of union with Christ have been differently emphasized throughout the history of Christian worship and witness. The Byzantine writer Nicholas Cabasilas writes in his work “On the Life in Christ” (pgs. 5–6) that our union with Christ “is closer than any other union which man can possibly imagine and does not lend itself to any exact comparisons,” and hence Scripture uses a wide range of images and metaphors to speak about union with Christ. Similarly, New Testament scholar G. K. Beale characterizes union with the risen and ascended Christ to a multi-faceted gem, composed of seemingly quite different sides that nonetheless are integrated with one another. Far, far more can be said about this endless topic than is possible in a short essay.</p>
            <p>Yet, on the other hand, union with Christ is so simple that even little children can understand it quite well: it is Christ, dwelling in our hearts by faith (Eph 3:17). All of the saving benefits which God objectively accomplished in Jesus Christ are subjectively applied to us as the Holy Spirit unites us with Christ through faith. Through this bond, communion, sharing, oneness, participation, and mutual indwelling of Christ being in us and us being in Christ, we know Christ and all his benefits.</p>
            <p>This essay will look at four aspects of our union with Christ: first, its scriptural and theological basis; second, how union with Christ is the central doctrine of salvation; third, we will explore the means of grace whereby Christ unites us with himself by his Word and Spirit in the church; and fourth, we will note only a few of the practical and pastoral implications of union with Christ for Christian living, especially in our lived experiences of suffering, virtue formation, mission, and worship.</p>
            <h3 class="section-heading">The Scriptural and Theological Basis of Union with Christ</h3>
            <p>The Holy Spirit’s work of uniting believers with Jesus Christ presupposes two prior unions: the union of three persons in one God, the Holy Trinity; and second, the basis of our union with Christ is the union of two natures, truly human and truly divine, in the one person of Jesus Christ in his incarnation.</p>
            <p>The Christian faith gives astonished and delighted testimony to the God of the gospel. God is the one who lives and loves in freedom, and Christian faith contemplates God, and all else in relation to God. Before ever worlds were made, the Father and the Son relate to one another as “Father” and “Son,” and the Holy Spirit is from the Father as the bond of love in an eternal communion of unapproachable majesty, blessedness, and the glorious splendor of undivided light.</p>
            <p>Not out of any deficiency or want in God, but out of the overflowing fullness and joy of God’s life as Trinity, God relates outwardly to creatures in his works from creation to salvation to the consummation of all things. With respect to God’s work of creation, our union with Christ rests on humanity having been created as creatures who are compatible with God. There is a difference between God the Creator and all else which is his creation; God alone is infinite and unchanging and eternal, and all else is finite, ever-changing, and temporal. So, while God’s intention is for humanity to become “partakers of the divine nature” (2 Pet 1:4), that never means we transgress the Creator-creature distinction. Yet, there is a compatibility whereby humans can have union and communion with God because we are created in the image and likeness of God, and the archetypal image of the invisible God, after which we were fashioned, is Jesus Christ (Gen 1:26–27; Col 1:15).</p>
            <p>The one God, who is Father, Son, and Holy Spirit, quintessentially and definitively revealed himself in the fullness of time when the eternal Son of God took to himself a truly human nature. This is the desire of nations, the mystery which angels long to look into, which we marvel over and celebrate with wonder at Christmas, that Jesus Christ is truly divine and truly human. Because God became man in Jesus Christ, now we in him can become participants in God’s life and perfections. The renewal of the whole cosmos and redemption of individual sinners was accomplished in the humiliation and exaltation of Jesus Christ. After rising from the dead, Jesus lifted up his hands and bestowed a blessing upon his people, and while he was blessing them he was taken up into heaven (Lk 24:50–51). Having obtained salvation, he now bestows it upon his people in benediction, as the Holy Spirit in the new covenant was poured out at Pentecost to create a communion of saints (Acts 1–2). Christ is now at the right hand of God in heaven, “he is not here; he is risen” (Matt 28:6). But Christ becomes our contemporary and we become contemporaries with him, as the Spirit joins us with Christ as co-participants in dying and rising with Christ (Gal 2:20, 6:14; Rom 6:1–4; Col 2:13–15, 3:1–4). The Father’s sending of the Son and the Holy Spirit is thus how God’s good creation, ruined by sin, is redeemed and transformed to become the kingdom of God that shares in God’s life. All the fullness of deity dwells in Christ bodily, and He as risen and ascended Lord is reconciling all things to himself through the blood of Christ as his Holy Spirit heralds his saving word throughout the world (Col 1:18–23, 3:1, 1:8).</p>
            <p>Hence, the Trinity and incarnation are integral to how creatures can enter into a saving relationship with the God of the gospel: the Holy Spirit unites us with Christ, the God-man, sent by the Father. Further, there is no competition for prominence between being Trinity-centered or Christ-centered, for these inter-related foci are concentric circles. When we rightly so “fix our eyes on Jesus” (Heb 12:2) and devote rapt attention to the person and work of Christ, we will continually rediscover afresh just how profoundly Jesus’ own center is the glory of the Father in the Holy Spirit. Once our focus is there, we will be driven back to Christ as we discover how the Father and Spirit glorify the Son.</p>
            <h3 class="section-heading">Union with Christ as the Key to How Redemption Is Accomplished and Applied</h3>
            <p>Union with Christ describes the focus of the gospel on what God has objectively accomplished in Jesus Christ and subjectively applies to us as the Spirit unites us with Christ through faith. The gospel is Son-centered; “the gospel of God,” promised beforehand in the Scriptures of the Old Testament,” is first and foremost “concerning his Son” (Rom 1:1–3). The gospel is not mainly about us, or the benefits that Christ has given us. The gospel is not good advice about what we should do; it is good news about Jesus and what he has done. The gospel is not something we do; it is a heralding of what God has singularly accomplished in Jesus Christ. It is about Jesus Christ, first and foremost, and when we apprehend Christ, we share in him and all his saving benefits.</p>
            <p>When you say the Apostles’ Creed and the Nicene Creed, you will notice that particular outward works of God towards creation are appropriated by various members of the Trinity. Though, as one God, the three persons always work together, in the first article of the creed the work of creation is especially appropriated by the Father, though the Son and the Spirit are involved. The second article of the creed is devoted to the person and work of the Son of God. The third article of the creed might seem to end with confessing simply that “we believe in the Holy Spirit,” before a list of random-seeming things such as the church, baptism, forgiveness, and the resurrection of the dead follow. However, we should understand that implicit within the creed is a vision of how union with Christ explains redemption, accomplished and applied. The incarnate career of the Son of God accomplished an objective salvation to renew all things, and the Holy Spirit unites us with the Son such that we are vindicated with him, joined with the communion of saints in the one holy, catholic, and apostolic church, we are raised with him, and share in Christ’s everlasting life. It is not spelled out in either creed that the Spirit unites us with Christ through faith such that we share in his distinct but inseparable saving benefits, but that is nonetheless the key to the logic of confessing our faith in the words of the creed.</p>
            <p>A real redemption was accomplished when the eternal Son of God, who had taken to himself a truly human nature, was “delivered up for our trespasses and raised for our justification” (Rom 4:25). The Son was not an unwilling victim; the Father, Son, and Holy Spirit always work together inseparably in all their works from creation to providence, from salvation to consummation. The Son willingly laid down his life, the Father and the Spirit were integral to his resurrection (Jn 10:18; Rom 8:11).</p>
            <p>Thus, when God forgives sinners and declares they are righteous, it is not a legal fiction; a real sentence was carried out upon our sin, since “God has done what the law, weakened by the flesh, could not do. By sending his own Son in the likeness of sinful flesh and for sin, he condemned sin in the flesh” (Rom 8:3). Thereby, the saving righteousness of God is revealed in at least two ways. First, in the same way that a just judge cannot minimize or overlook real evil, God is shown to be “just” in Christ’s shed blood and death; second, God is shown to be “the justifier of the one who has faith in Jesus” as God gives the gift of his saving righteousness to those who are united with Christ (Rom 3:26). Yet, God’s objective accomplishment of salvation entailed far more than the death of Christ. Jesus, by his resurrection from the dead, was “vindicated” by the Holy Spirit (1 Tim 3:16; Rom 1:4). Further, after his humiliation and exaltation by rising from the dead, the Son ascended into heaven. There, he is both the offering and the great high priest; rather than continuing to stand at the altar and offer repeated sacrifices, Christ sat down after making a perfect, once-for-all-time offering of his crucified and risen flesh-and-blood in the heavenly Holy of Holies. Now we, with him, can draw by faith to the throne of grace (Heb 10:11–16; 4:12). Christ does not merely save us by dying in our place for our sins, taking upon himself the just punishment we deserved — though he does. Further, Christ in his own person embodied the ends for which humanity was created. He is the first of a newly restored humanity, the second and Last Adam. Humanity’s prophetic, priestly, and royal vocation to tend and cultivate the garden-temple of God’s cosmos is fulfilled in Jesus’ life, death, resurrection, and ascension.</p>
            <p>Now all that is Christ’s becomes ours. An especially clear passage of Scripture on this principle is 1 Corinthians 1:30–31, namely, “because of him you are in Christ Jesus, who became to us wisdom from God, righteousness and sanctification and redemption, so that, as it is written, ‘Let the one who boasts, boast in the Lord.’”</p>
            <p>Hence, in adoption, by sending the Spirit into our hearts, we become children who share in the Son’s address to God as ‘Abba, father’! As adopted sons, we have both the relational benefits of being in God’s family, and the legal benefits of being heirs of all that is Christ’s. In justification, we who had exchanged the truth about God for a lie in our idolatry and lawless transgression are declared righteous by being in the crucified and risen Messiah, because we share in his vindication; we are not only “justified by his blood” but also “saved by his life,” whereby Christ’s own vindication and triumph has put an end to our enmity and hostility, reconciling us to the God who justifies the ungodly in Christ (Rom 4:5, 5:9–11; Col 1:19–23). Hence, all other means of appraising our worth are called into question, because the righteousness of God is appropriated as the Spirit unites us with Christ by faith, such that we have fellowship with Christ in his sufferings and participate in his resurrection (Phil 3:1–11). We are justified by faith because through faith we are joined with Christ, such that our sin was co-crucified with him and now Christ lives in us, as we live by faith in the Son of God who loved us and gave himself for us (Gal 2:15, 19–20). Notably, whatever all justification entails, it precludes boasting in anything other means of regarding ourselves as worthy than the cross of Christ by which we were co-crucified with Christ, since now his new creation has come (Gal 6:14–15; 1 Cor 1:31). In sanctification, God’s grace does not create licentiousness to continue in sin, but rather we were buried with Christ through baptism and raised with him to walk in newness of life; whereas we were once incapable of responding to God, now through our union with Christ we are no longer agents and servants of the powers of sin and death, but we are agents and servants of righteousness (Rom 6:1–19).</p>
            <p>In glorification, humanity is restored and created towards those ends for which we were created in the image and likeness of God, namely to glorify God and enjoy him forever. Our glorification is already secured in Christ as the firstfruits of what is to come, although we are not yet glorified like Christ with bodies no longer subject to decay, but at the resurrection of the dead we will become like Christ’s risen and glorified body (Phil 3:19–21, Rom 8:30). Hence, we are even now “heirs of God and fellow heirs with Christ, provided we suffer with him in order that we may also be glorified with him” (Rom 8:17). In election, we who deserved to be rejected are the chosen and Beloved of God, because we are in the elected and rejected Lord Jesus Christ (Eph 1:3–11). In regeneration, we who were dead are made alive together with Christ by his grace (Eph 2:1–10). There are further saving benefits of Christ we might enumerate; for example, we who were foolish and darkened now are united with Christ, who is wisdom itself, and whose Spirit enlightens our hearts to behold the glory of God in the face of Jesus Christ (2 Cor 3:17–4:6; 1 Cor 1:30; Eph 1:18).</p>
            <p>Thus, in the gospel, Christ became for us several saving realities, and being ‘in him’ we come to appropriate his distinct but inseparable benefits. That is the key to avoiding innumerable problems. Foregrounding union with Christ as the central doctrine of salvation helps us coherently explain how Christ can simultaneously say to us “Come to me, all who labor and are heavy laden, and I will give you rest” but also “deny yourself, take up your cross daily, and follow me” (Matt 11:28; Lk 9:23). For example, this helps us avoid thinking we can become lax about sin since justification is a gift of mercy to the undeserving. Why? The only way we appropriate forgiveness is through union with Christ, and Christ is one whole person, whom we cannot tear him apart. We cannot be united with only one part of Christ, such as the part of Christ that forgives us, without also being united with the whole of Christ, who also calls us to be conformed to his image in obedience and raised us to walk in newness of life (Rom 6:1– 4). Simultaneously, having a clear grasp of union with Christ can help us avoid the grave problems that emerge when we fail to distinguish between his saving benefits, such as collapsing justification into a summons to transformed living that makes our right standing before God depend upon our moral performance, which would be far from ‘good news’ to all who know themselves to be sinners. Justification as our legal status before God, is not a matter of our striving to become more righteous, but a gift received by faith alone, even though the faith that receives Christ and all his benefits will never be alone, but we also by the Spirit must grow in our sanctification, becoming conformed to the image of Christ in holiness.</p>
            <h3 class="section-heading">The Means of Grace Whereby Christ Unites Us with Himself in the Church</h3>
            <p>The triune God uses the ordinary means of grace in the church to unite us with Christ, principally, the word of God and the holy sacraments of Baptism and the Eucharist.</p>
            <p>The word of God is <em>alive</em>. Far from an inert artifact from ancient history, the word of God is “living and active”; it is sharper than a two edged sword, piercing us to expose the thoughts and intensions of our hearts, it slays and makes alives (Heb 4:12). Like a surgeon’s scalpel, the word of God cuts but only in order to heal. Upon saying farewell to the Ephesian elders, Paul commended them to “God and to the word of his grace, which is able to build you up and to give you the inheritance among all those who are sanctified” (Acts 20:32). God does everything by his explosive, lively, unsettling, life-giving Word. At creation, by his word all things were made, speaking creation into existence out of nothing; in salvation, by his word God gives life to the dead. In the fulness of time, his word became flesh and dwelt among us in Jesus Christ, who is Immanuel, “God with us,” and his Spirit now works his word in to our hearts such that we pray back to God his words of praise, both in the very breath in our longs and in the words of the Psalms (Rom 4:17; Jn 1:14; Matt 1:23; Rom 15:6).</p>
            <p>But it is particularly through the word of God that we become united with Jesus Christ. We receive the Holy Spirit “by hearing with faith” (Gal 3:2, 5). The word of God, which is our very life as his covenant people, is not a hidden or esoteric mystery which we must go on a quest to ascertain, or which only an enlightened visionary can reach (Deut 32:47; 30:11–14). God in his kind condescension has brought his word near to us in Christ’s death and resurrection; it is now in our hearts, because “faith comes from hearing, and hearing through the word of Christ” (Rom 10:17). The word of God is a key means whereby the Holy Spirit unites us with Christ, since “in him [Christ] you also, when you heard the word of truth, the gospel of your salvation, and believed in him, were sealed with the promised Holy Spirit, who is the guarantee of our inheritance until we acquire possession of it, to the praise of his glory” (Eph 1:13–14). Hence, in his 1517 lectures on Hebrews, Martin Luther hyperbolically captured how “if you were to ask a Christian what his task is and by what he is worthy of the name of Christian, there could be no other response than hearing the Word of God, that is, faith. Ears are the only organs of the Christian” (LW 29:224). Jesus especially promised to work through his word. Through the public proclamation of the word of God in the church’s assembled worship, and through the private ministry of the word in the best and worst moments of life, Christ is reconciling the world to himself (2 Cor 5:16–21). The assault of grace upon the old age liberates us to enter with Christ into the new creation through his powerful, lively, precious, lifegiving word (Psa 119:25; 2 Cor 5:17).</p>
            <p>The word of God creates the church by uniting her with Christ. Crucially, God not merely uses his word to personally unite individuals with Christ, and it is not merely the case that individuals who are in Christ happen to also become joined with one another at church. The church is created by the word which God uses to unite us with Christ, and the church is the context wherein individuals become personally united with Christ as Christ in the church works through his word and sacraments. A wide array of beautiful metaphors are used in Holy Scripture for the union between Christ and the church: marriage between a husband and wife is a great mystery which signifies the union between Christ and the church (Eph 5:22–31). Not only are our minds or spirits united with Christ, but life in the body is inexorably transformed when we are no longer our own but become “one” with the Lord; the intimacy of the one-flesh union between a husband and wife, wherein the two become “one,” is analogous to how the church is united with Christ, such that it is like we are dragging Christ himself into sin when we misuse our bodies (1 Cor 6:12–20). The union between Christ and the church is like a body composed of significantly varying members (1 Cor 12:12–31; Rom 12:4–8).</p>
            <p>The Holy Spirit indwells the bodies of those who are in Christ similarly to how the one God of Israel dwelt in the temple (1 Cor 3:16; 6:19). The church’s union with Christ is also analogous to the wandering people of God in the wilderness; our forerunner has already burst ahead to enter into God’s heavenly rest by his ascension as prophet, priest, and king, and soon he will take us to himself so that we can enter into his promised rest (Heb 3–4; 12:1–2). The church’s union with Christ is also analogous to how a royal figure embodies a whole people; Jesus, specifically as “Christ,” that is, “the Messiah” or “the anointed one,” is the church’s Savior King who shares in his triumph and reign through the way of the cross now, and glory later (2 Tim 2:12; Rom 6:5–14; 2 Cor 1:20–22). Jesus himself likened the church’s union with Himself to the relationship between a vine and its branches (Jn 15:1–11). Branches that are fruitless are taken away, and those that do bear fruit are pruned, disrupting our expectations for instant gratification. But we have no life apart from him, and in the inexpressible glory of his kindness, he invites us: “abide in me, and I in you” (Jn 15:4). What that entails is eminently simple: letting the word of Christ abide in us (Jn 15:7), keeping his commandments, and thus abiding in his love (Jn 15:9–10).</p>
            <p>As the church’s one foundation, Christ works not only through his Word but also through what St. Augustine called the ‘visible words’ of the holy sacraments. The sacraments which our Lord Jesus Christ himself instituted are not only a sign of profession or of the love that Christians ought to have among themselves, but they are “certain sure witnesses, and effectual signs of grace, and God’s good will towards us, by the which he doth work invisibly in us, and doth not only quicken, but also strengthen and confirm our Faith in him” (Article XXVIII, 2019 BCP 781). Throughout the whole of covenant history in both the Old and New Testaments, God uses physical, material, tangible signs in order to signify and seal the promises of the gospel (Rom 4:11). Through baptism, we are buried with Christ (Rom 6:4); through the washing of regeneration, Christ is at work (Tit 3:5); and as a means of grace God uses baptism to save us (1 Pet 3:21), even though “the efficacy of baptism is not tied to that moment of time wherein it is administered” (WCF 28.6).</p>
            <p>In the Eucharist, we have <em>koinonia</em>, that is “participation” or “sharing” or “fellowship with” the body and blood Christ (1 Cor 10:16). Our Lord Jesus Christ himself said “Whoever feeds on my flesh and drinks my blood abides in me, and I in him,” and hence Christ uses the sacrament of Holy Communion, which Jesus called his body and blood of the new covenant, to unite us with himself (John 6:56), through the life-giving Holy Spirit (Jn 6:63). The Heidelberg Catechism explains what it means to eat Christ’s flesh and to drink his blood in terms of both believing the word of the gospel where Christ’s death and resurrection save us from our sins, but it is also “to be united more and more to his sacred body through the Holy Spirit, who lives both in Christ and in us,” since we remain on earth while Christ is ascended into heaven as our prophet, priest, and king (Heidelberg Catechism Q&amp;A #76). As Fred Sanders put it, “Baptism shows our being in Christ; the Lord’s Supper shows Christ being in us” <em>(Union with Christ and the Life of Faith</em>, 124).</p>
            <p>Finally, Christ promised to unite us with himself through prayer. We do need a mediator between humanity and God, and we have the singular, only sufficient one in the God-man, Jesus Christ himself (1 Tim 2:5). There is a <em>poverty</em> to our prayer; we do not know how to pray as we ought. Yet, the triune God works within us to make our prayers effective as God speaks to God through God. The Holy Spirit intercedes within us and our risen and ascended Lord Jesus also intercedes for us (Rom 8:26, 34). Hence, we can find mercy and grace to help in time of need, because we have a great high priest in heaven (Heb 4:14–16). Further, especially when we pray God’s word back to God, we are participating in Jesus’ own prayers since he is both the singer and the subject of the Psalms (Heb 2:12; Rom 15:3). Both personal, private devotion, and especially corporate or common prayer with others are means Christ uses to commune with and build up his people.</p>
            <p>Hence, the outward and ordinary means whereby Christ unites us with himself are the word of God, the sacraments of baptism and the Eucharist, and prayer. As Robert Letham captures:</p>
            <p>Salvation is our being united with Christ; the Father unites us to the incarnate Sno, who effects our salvation, by the Holy Spirit. This is one movement of God’s grace, for all three persons work in harmony and indivisible unity. The means the Spirit uses, from our side, is faith. This is because our salvation is the result of grace, something achieved for us, not anything we ourselves could do. It therefore comes to us from outside ourselves. However, saving fatih does not occur in a vacuum. We are brought to salvation in the context of the church. There is an outward side to what happens within. Union with Christ comes to external expression in baptism, as we are united with Christ’s church… and union with Christ is to be cultivated, developed, nourished. To that end, the Father has appointed that our union with the Son be matured by the Holy Spirit through the means he has appointed for that purpose… the preaching of the Word, the sacraments, and prayer… transformation in union with Christ occurs through the means of grace, until the church is entirely conformed to Christ, when it sees him as he is and is like him in glory” (Robert Letham, <em>Systematic Theology</em>, 608).</p>
            <h3 class="section-heading">The Pastoral and Practical Significance of Union with Christ: Suffering, Ethics, Mission, Liturgy</h3>
            <p>Finally, union with Christ practically and pastorally transforms practical Christian living, especially our lived experiences of suffering, virtue formation, mission, and worship.</p>
            <p>Suffering, in a modern and secular imagination, is either a necessary means whereby higher forms evolve from lower means (per Hegel and Darwin), or it is an utterly meaningless and randomized fate of the unfortunate, to which we can either respond by ordering our interior lives towards virtue (Neo-Stoicism) or by acquiring as much pleasure and material comfort as possible.</p>
            <p>Suffering indeed can feel meaningless (Ecc 1:2). We live and die amidst heartache within, and horrors without. There is hidden, psychological agony that we struggle to understand ourselves let alone articulate to others. Then, our bodies betray us, loved ones suffer, and there is the wreckage of history and unspeakable atrocities past and present that leave us speechless.</p>
            <p>Amidst it all, a Christian’s imagination of suffering will be gripped by our union with Christ. Her hope will be anchored to the one who endured not only unspeakable pain and shame, but real, cursed, godforsakenness; he entered the depths of an abyss and a shadowy darkness which none can fathom — yet did so in order to overcome the present evil age, as the first herald of the new creation, who lays his right hand upon us and says “Fear not, I am the first and the last, and the living one. I died, and behold I am alive forevermore, and I have the keys of Death and Hades” (Rev 1:17–18). Despite it all, we therefore become enable to say, with St. Paul:</p>
            <p>I want to know Christ—yes, to know the power of his resurrection and participation in his sufferings, becoming like him in his death, and so, somehow, attaining to the resurrection from the dead. Not that I have already obtained all this, or have already arrived at my goal, but I press on to take hold of that for which Christ Jesus took hold of me. Brothers and sisters, I do not consider myself yet to have taken hold of it. But one thing I do: Forgetting what is behind and straining toward what is ahead, I press on toward the goal to win the prize for which God has called me heavenward in Christ Jesus.</p>
            <p>Christians worship a God who is praised in Holy Scripture as the God of all consolation, even as he calls us to participate not only in the comfort which Christ alone can provide but also to suffer:</p>
            <p>Blessed be the God and Father of our Lord Jesus Christ, the Father of mercies and God of all comfort, who comforts us in all our affliction, so that we may be able to comfort those who are in any affliction, with the comfort with which we ourselves are comforted by God. For as we share abundantly in Christ’s sufferings, so through Christ we share abundantly in comfort too. If we are afflicted, it is for your comfort and salvation; and if we are comforted, it is for your comfort, which you experience when you patiently endure the same sufferings that we suffer. Our hope for you is unshaken, for we know that as you share in our sufferings, you will also share in our comfort. (2 Corinthians 1:3–7)</p>
            <p>Christian ministry specifically takes on new meaning when it is understood in light of our union with Christ, since it is fleshing out Christ’s otherwise hidden afflictions (Col 1:24). We not merely teach and warn in order to present people mature in Christ; it is actually Christ himself who is at work within us (Col 1:24–25). Christ is the one who makes an appeal through us, as we herald his sacred and imperishable gospel of reconciliation to the whole cosmos (2 Cor 5:19). God is the one who “in Christ” leads us in triumphal procession, and God is the one who “through us spreads the fragrance of the knowledge of him everywhere,” as the fragrance of Christ is the aroma of life to those who are being saved and of death to those who are perishing (2 Cor 2:14–17).</p>
            <p>Union with Christ is not the kind of thing one grasps and then moves on from; it is an infinite, inexhaustible well that is the key to the whole of the Christian life. It is an unshakeable anchor of hope in our suffering and an unquenchable fire that fuels worship, mission, striving after holiness, and growth in practices that forge virtue for all who can say “I have been crucified with Christ. It is no longer I who live, but Christ who lives in me. And the life I now live in the flesh I live by faith in the Son of God, who loved me and gave himself for me” (Gal 2:20).</p>
            <p class="contributor-bio">Rev. Dr. Joshua Heavin (PhD, Aberdeen) serves as curate for pastoral care at Christ Church Cathedral in Plano, Texas; he is the book reviews editor for <em>Pro Ecclesia</em>, the journal of the Center for Catholic and Evangelical Theology; and he is an adjunct professor in the School of Christian Thought at Houston Christian University and West Texas A&amp;M University.</p>
          </section>

          <!-- ====== ARTICLE 5 ====== -->
          <section id="apocalypse-now" class="content-section">
            <h2 class="section-title">Apocalypse Now</h2>
            <p class="essay-author">By Kirsten Sanders</p>

            <p class="essay-heading">Martha Park. <em>World Without End: Essays on Apocalypse and After</em>. Hub City Press, 2025. $24.00. 240 pp.</p>
            <blockquote><p><em>“The oceans are burning. The deserts are flooding. UFOs are real, according to Congress, and glaciers are turning to liquid. There’s also the threat of nuclear war, regular war, and according to scientists we’re in the midst of the sixth great extinction. What better moment in human history than now to take a big, long celebratory drink or five?”</em></p></blockquote>
            <p>So writes Jennifer McCartney in opening her 2025 book, <em>Cocktails for the Apocalypse</em>. This tongue-in-cheek collection of cocktail recipes makes no attempt to give a serious answer to the problems of the day. But it only exists because there are a lot of people who get the reference, creating a market for such a book. If the oceans are on fire and the world has been destroyed, why shouldn’t we make a drink and wait for the end to come? Everyone, it seems, is talking—and writing—about the end of the world. Consider <em>Theology for the End of the World</em> (by Marika Rose, 2023), <em>Making a Life at the End of the World</em> (Lisa Wells, 2021), or <em>Coming of Age at the End of the World</em> (Daniel Sherrell, 2021). Or maybe <em>The End of the World is Just the Beginning</em> (Peter Zeihan, 2022). Or the <em>Ends of the World</em> (Peter Brannen, 2022). Or <em>The World of The End</em> (David Jeremiah, 2022). There is <em>The Beginning [that] Comes After the End</em> (Rebecca Solnit, 2026). Or just <em>The End of the World</em> (Ernesto de Martino 1908-1965, who got an early jump on the topic. His book was translated in 2023).</p>
            <p>This publishing trend unveils a great interest in “the end of the world”—whatever each author means by this. But which world is ending? How do we know? And what on earth are we to do about it?</p>
            <p>Climate activism especially invites this sort of apocalypticism. Mara van der Lugt’s <em>Hopeful Pessmisim</em> addresses this penchant and pushes back on climate pessimism. Though things are bad, she suggests we should still act as if there is hope, even if there isn’t.</p>
            <p>Van der Lugt’s book identifies an important thread. It is a philosophical, theological, and psychological knot that goes like this. If the situation is truly hopeless, then people will stop trying to improve it. False hope is bad, but <em>no</em> hope might be worse. It can encourage apathy and disengagement, the sorts of behaviors that make bad situations snowball into catastrophic ones. How, then, can we live so that we engage with the world we have if the world really <em>is</em> falling apart? How can we motivate people to do the things humans do—make love and have babies, make art and work to better the world—if they are persuaded that the world is ending soon? Do you need to love the world to live well in it?</p>
            <p>These are good questions. I’m inclined to think, put together, that they might be <em>the</em> theological questions of our day.</p>
            <p>Christians have to answer them, though sometimes they seem to skip them instead. Our forms of life have too often mimicked the tone of the secular world, listing all the terrible things that might soon be lost, and not taking stock of the graces that remain. It is not even graces, to be clear. It is the sorts of things that a simple observation of the world might remind us of— the flower bulbs that show up in spring, chipmunks that dig them up and hoard them under chicken wire in the shed, the lichen that forms on trees, the pleasures of a good meal, or playing cards with a child.</p>
            <p>Frankly, we too often live in the world apologetically, like we don’t want to be a bother, really. We’ll just stay here a minute and not touch anything.</p>
            <p>Christian hope, it is true, is not confident ownership or even the ease of a renter with a good landlord. It works and tidies even as it sets down to rest. It is passionate about maintenance. It resists both optimism and despair—a heady feat, given the constant temptations to the latter. Despair, though not an unforgivable sin, certainly prevails against a Christian belief in a God who is somehow working, against all odds, to make all things new.</p>
            <p>Christian apocalypticism is often assumed to be a belief in an imminent, dramatic ending of the world, a not-to-be-missed pyrotechnic display of rupture and separation, of wailing and also of restoration. But though apocalyptic literature implies an ending, what makes Christian apocalypse distinct is that it brings with it the promise of a beginning. The ending is inextricably tied to a beginning that is promised just outside the frame.</p>
            <p>As far as I can put the pieces together, Christianity without these impossible things is a panacea, a warm bit of milk to soothe the stomach. It might make us feel better, temporarily, but its logic—hope without resurrection—is little more than a bit of comfort. The best it can do is soothe us as we continue on in the same direction we’ve been going. But I have to say this is true only “as far as I can put the pieces together,” because I’ve never been one of these kinds of Christians. I’ve spent a good bit of time adjacent to them. But to qualify that I believe in God, but don’t believe some of the harder things–resurrection, for instance, or eternal life–has never seemed worth the mental gymnastics. You either buy the ticket and take the ride, or you sit it out.</p>
            <p>Martha Park, author of <em>World Without End: Essays on Apocalypticism and After</em>, was one of these kinds of Christians too. She confesses that she “never actually read the Book of Revelation, in all my years growing up in the church.” When she finally did read it, at thirty years old, she finds not a fearful destruction of the world but something more like the Greek <em>apocalupsis</em>, meaning “unveiling” or “uncovering.” What she reads is more like “a flash of new understanding, often seeming to come from outside of ourselves. Rather than a new world, revelation leads to new sight, a new way of seeing the world that’s always been there.”</p>
            <p>The book of Revelation, she writes, brings “God’s kingdom to come… conveyed in earthly language: there is a river ‘bright as crystal,’ a fruit-bearing ‘tree of life.’ Even after the supposed end of the world, there is life abundant, ‘healing of the nations,’ a world un-degraded. ‘Nothing accursed will be found there anymore.’”</p>
            <blockquote><p>“It seemed there was no real end of the world to be found here- only massive and continual change, not so unlike the world we live in now. I wonder if change might be harder to imagine- maybe even harder to bear- than a definitive end.”</p></blockquote>
            <p>Park’s essays revolve around two endings—the ending of her religious faith, and the ending (or near-term ending) of aspects of the Southern ecosystem that she holds dear. She mourns the loss of the Torreya pines in Florida and the presumed extinction of the ivory pileated woodpecker. In a particularly moving essay, she writes about the environmental cost of traditional burials and the religious people who are promoting natural burial in the Southeast.</p>
            <p>Along the way she reflects on the Methodist faith she has also lost. She only realizes she’s lost her faith when her father, a Methodist minister, retires. “Growing up”, she writes, “Wesleyan values like reason, discernment, and grace hovered far above other Christian concepts— like salvation, redemption, and sin—that I didn’t hear about so much. My father never preached much about any other life other than this life, or any world other than this one.” She found her eventual reading of the book of Revelation, as an adult, to be startling, the “physical world’s destruction: the sun turned black and the moon, red as blood; ‘the sky vanished like a scroll rolling itself up’; even the stars fell to earth.”</p>
            <p>The Methodist church gave Park a faith that rested, like a cloth, over the world as she knew it. There was no “mere” symbol to the biblical language she encountered. It was this world, the world she knew, as she experienced it. She writes lovingly of living in parsonages where “I could see the church sanctuary from every room in our small brick ranch-style house.” The churches were shabby but reverent, filled with “white-haired old ladies… lining up to shake my father’s hand in the doorway after church”; her father would ring a bell in the entry to quiet everyone down for worship. God was there, among them.</p>
            <p>“The world was drenched, then,” she writes, “with a presence around me called God. I walked with one hand aloft at my side for him to hold.”</p>
            <p>Park’s loss of faith, as is the case for many, corresponds with her passage into adulthood and with her father’s retirement from the ministry. After he retires, she no longer attends church, but in the year preceding it, she attends every Sunday, “crying in the second row.” Park’s father was a liberal mainline minister, of the type who is now mostly retired or extinct. She considers herself one of the “nones,” that demographic group that claims no religious affiliation. But she expresses a deep ambivalence toward her loss of faith. She has memories of the church that are deeply meaningful not because of what she believed or what it accomplished, but because of the crack of light that she saw throughout her time there.</p>
            <p>She finds her father one evening burning his old sermons in an outdoor fire and imagines it to be a form of ritual for a way of life, and a way of belief, that is now gone. Park’s descriptions of his ministry demonstrate her fondness for him. This love for her father and love for the church is all tangled up, inseparable. But once her father retires, she finds her faith is over, too.</p>
            <p>It would be too cheap a trick to say that Park is still a believer. But the stories in her essays are vivid and all alike in one way: They imagine a world that is gone yet dream of a means of return.</p>
            <p>When Park writes of the Torreya pines in Florida, she tells stories of conservationists who work hard for the survival of the species. The story starts with Elvin Edison Callaway, who read the book of Genesis and decided the garden of Eden must be northern Florida. The Torreya pine, which he thought must be the gopher tree that Noah used to build the ark, was already in decline by the 1980s. The roles of conservationists butt up at times against private landowners who seem comfortable to let nature’s decline take its course. It is an old and somewhat tired story of those who seek to save the world and those who are content to let nature take its course of inevitable decline.</p>
            <p>Fundamentalists abound. They show up in Callaway, but also in Park’s visit to Ken Ham’s “Ark Encounter,” in her stories of preppers who are preparing for “the end of the world,” and in the Bible church that builds an ugly, but well-intentioned natural cemetery. They show up in her chapter on the Scopes trial, for which she travels to Dayton, Tennessee and attends a reenactment. Her husband was formerly an evangelical, and she has other friends who spent time in these circles. But unlike many contemporary treatments of fundamentalists and evangelicals that are satisfied with caricature, Park writes about them with curiosity, mostly interested in their certainty. At times she seems jealous of it. Though she is opposed to their conclusions—what she sees as a lack of concern for their neighbors, for the climate, and for “science” broadly conceived—you can’t help but notice a kind of longing for the way these people live in the world. They live in the world as if it belonged to them, whereas Park’s experience is one of estrangement.</p>
            <p>The world she knows is one of loss. She sees it everywhere. My favorite essay told the story of the ivory billed woodpecker.</p>
            <p>The ivory billed woodpecker is presumed extinct. Much resides with that one word, “presumed.” Nicknamed the “Lord God Bird” (too good to be true!), the woodpecker was about twenty inches in length with a stunning thirty-inch wingspan. The bird is black with a red crested head and streaks of white down the length of its wings, as well as an ivory beak. The last known sighting of the bird was in 1944. But in 2004, reported sightings of the bird drew interest from ornithologists, bird watchers, and journalists alike.</p>
            <p>News of the bird promised a potential economic windfall for the town of Brinkley, Arkansas, the location of the purported woodpecker sighting. Local businesses posted signage of the bird’s discovery, and the town geared for an influx of tourists, scientists, and birdwatchers. The missing bird was an emblem of forgotten hope, a harbinger of a species presumed dead, and now alive.</p>
            <p>You see where all of this is going. The woodpecker— the <em>Lord God</em> Bird—was back from the dead! Except it wasn’t. The documentary <em>Ghost Bird</em> (2009) presents a dramatic, slightly comic story of the event. The “true believers”—birdwatchers and scientists who are convinced that the bird is alive—and scientists and locals who think it was a misidentification, probably for the more common pileated woodpecker. The obsessive zeal of the birdwatchers comes across as almost religious. Some people just desperately wanted to believe in this bird. The documentary is really fun to watch. The birdwatchers are comic and even slightly heroic in their attempt to believe in this long-gone bird. It reminded me that believers are everywhere.</p>
            <p>Park writes movingly of the woodpecker. She seems certain, or nearly certain, that it is gone. But she wants so much to live in a world where this bird lives. He is a talisman for the hope she wishes she had. We so much want to believe impossible things.</p>
            <p>“I’ve often wondered how these old stories,” Park writes, “written in the midst of ‘their then’ still manage to speak to this world, to ‘our now.’ I’ve always sympathized with these anxious disciples, asking over and over again what the future will be like, and being told only to look around them; being told, over and over again, <em>this.”</em></p>
            <p>Her conclusion is the conclusion of many a mainline Christian. She stakes her hope in this world, making the apocalyptic the inevitable change that the world always promises. She even stretches to give this acceptance of the world a philosophical bent: “Removing the promise of another world, the challenge of faith shifts: rather than waiting for some unseen world to take this one’s place, faith becomes about seeing this world as singular and sacred. Lately, it has felt painful to stay awake to a world so clearly suffering; difficult to experience wonder or holiness on these patches of earth I am no longer sure I recognize, almost impossible to be present to a changing world without knowing how this story will end.</p>
            <p>But faith without an apocalypse- where there is no rapture, no promise of escape from this world to some other unseen, unscarred one- might be a way back to this world, not away from it. This kind of faith mirrors the uncertain world in which we live, the one that never really ends but keeps on changing.”</p>
            <p>It is the cycles of <em>dharma</em> and the “vanity, vanity” of Ecclesiastes, the story of worlds beyond worlds. It is a good story, perhaps even a beautiful one. It contains within it the reality of the world’s suffering and the grief that we can feel when we observe it. Unlike some Christian stories, it admits that this world is a place of loss, a vale of tears.</p>
            <p>Christians believe several impossible things—or, at least, we are supposed to. We believe that God became human. We believe this God-man passed through life doing both ordinary and miraculous things. And then we believe that we killed him, a crowd both power-hungry and disappointed in the appearance of this King. After three days, unlikely things strike again, when this unlikely, crucified King rose from the dead.</p>
            <p>There have always been those who didn’t believe. For them, the tomb contains this King, still dead. But Christians historically were defined as those who <em>did</em> believe. The others, who didn’t believe, by definition, were not Christians. One uniqueness of the mainline is that it “resurrected” Christians who didn’t believe in the resurrection. What would have been a clear line-in-the-sand became an optional footnote. Perhaps he was, perhaps he wasn’t—it’s a good story either way.</p>
            <p>Park does not seem to believe in resurrection. But she believes most deeply in apocalypse: “Rather than a new world, revelation leads to new sight, a new way of seeing the world that’s always been there.” She is a mainline Christian, through and through, perhaps most clearly in the fact that she no longer believes in anything at all.</p>
            <p>But she wants to. She misses the church of her youth. She wants faith to mean something. She wants a faith that leads us to live deeper into the world: “But a faith without an apocalypse—where there is no rapture, no promise of escape from this world to some other unseen, unscarred one—might be a way back to this world, not away from it. This kind of faith mirrors the uncertain world in which we live, the one that never really ends but keeps on changing.”</p>
            <p>In the Christian vision of the end-of-the-world, the dead will be raised. It is a privilege, really, to have known this world as something to love. But the hope that God founds, at the end of the world, does not just return to us what has been lost. It is this—and more.</p>
            <p>Park thinks that apocalypse can tuck us deeper into the world, like the dead who are buried in the natural cemeteries she describes. It is moving and beautiful. The burial practices themselves are deeply resonant with a Christian vision of caring for the bodies of the dead. But she cannot hold onto the belief that accompanied this vision for burying the dead. She wants the expansiveness, the opening to somewhere beyond, but has lost the taste for belief itself.</p>
            <p>I wish she knew she could have all this. Belief can be an adjustment to two truths—that so much has been lost, and yet all things will be made new. The apocalypse we can receive is the reality that there is a beyond. It is like one eye opening, a small speck of light. The world is in better hands than ours. The ivory billed woodpecker, the Torreya pines, the gravesites in the trees—they will all be renewed. Her father’s wrinkled hand will be made young again. In this apocalyptic vision, you can almost see him standing just out of sight. His hand is poised just above the sanctuary bell. Worship is about to begin. All she needs to do is walk in the door.</p>
            <p class="contributor-bio">Kirsten Sanders (PhD, Emory University) is a writer and theologian. She lives with her family in Massachusetts.</p>
          </section>

          <!-- ====== ARTICLE 6 ====== -->
          <section id="from-babel-to-belonging" class="content-section">
            <h2 class="section-title">From Babel to Belonging</h2>
            <p class="essay-author">By Annie Crawford</p>

            <p class="essay-heading"><em>C.S. Lewis’s Fairy-Tale Cure for the Modern Machine</em></p>
            <p>In 2026, we clearly live at the end of a world. While it may not be the end of <em>the</em> world, the massive technological and social changes moving through the West are quickly dissolving the old order and ushering in something whose exact contours remain to be seen. Rapid advancements in AI, declining trust in institutions, social polarization, and the collapse of family structures drive widespread alienation, mental illness, and addiction, unraveling the social fabric and fueling fear of an apocalyptic future.</p>
            <p>Our predicament is not exactly new. Like Yeats’s beast slouching slowly toward Bethlehem to be born, the modern Machine has been remaking the world into its own image for hundreds of years now. In the name of advancing rational enlightenment and personal autonomy, our lives have been slowly wired into the structures of what E. M. Forster, Paul Kingsnorth, and others have called “The Machine,” a massive, emergent techno-industrialist globalism that controls modern life through its Kafkaesque systems of bureaucratic state power. This system, as Lewis Mumford observed, transforms the world into “an archetypal machine made of human parts,” reducing communally integrated persons into isolated, interchangeable cogs. Ironically, every bit of independence we think we gain from nature is in reality a little bit of dependence we have given to the Machine. When you no longer depend upon the sun or woodfire for warmth, you have not become personally independent of nature; you have become dependent on the energy company. Robert Nisbet, in <em>The Quest for Community</em>, diagnoses the unintended consequences of this shift: “Individualism has resulted in masses of normless, unattached, insecure individuals who lose even the capacity for independent, creative living.” We think our technology liberates us from the limits of nature, but the techno-industrialist machine actually re-organizes our lives toward dissipation so our attention and energy can feed the Machine. The Machine’s fuel, as Paul Kingsnorth starkly notes, is life itself: “The fuel is nature... The fuel is you.” In the 21st century, we have clearly moved across a kind of tipping point where industry no longer serves the genuine interests of man; man now serves the interests of the Machine.</p>
            <p>The industrial strength horrors of the World Wars began to awaken the West to the Faustian bargain she had made with technology. Post-war writers like C. S. Lewis, E. M. Forster, and Robert Nisbet worked to understand how the Machine dissolves the fundamental structures of a humane community and to explore paradigms for its recovery. The alienation, disintegration, and spiritual anxiety created by life in the modern ouroborosian Machine led to a renewed search for the roots of genuine human community. Many find themselves longing for the renewal of embodied local relationship and a connection to nature. Nisbet again observes the trend: “In our own time we are confronted by the spectacle of innumerable individuals seeking escape from the very processes of individualism and impersonality which the nineteenth-century rationalist hailed as the very condition of progress.” In this post-human, techno-bureaucratic world, we find ourselves desperate to recover coherent communities that can provide a sense of personal agency, identity, meaning, and value.</p>
            <p>Yet, ironically, as a people well-catechized by the technopoly, we automatically look for technological solutions to our technological problems. We try to resolve the problem of unprecedented power with more power. Like Boromir, we put our hope in finding the One Machine to rule them all. Concerned about the existential dangers of AI, we rush to create more AI. We look for solutions from the very Machine that erodes coherent forms of embodied relationship with each other and with the created order.</p>
            <p>In his masterful sci-fi dystopian novel, <em>That Hideous Strength</em>, C. S. Lewis suggests that the answer to our predicament lies not in direct confrontation with our Techno-industrial Tower of Babel, but at the subversive level of personal community where we can know and be known. As Lewis warns in <em>The Abolition of Man</em> and illustrates in <em>That Hideous Strength</em>, those who live by the manipulation of nature will die by the manipulation of nature, for the inexorable logic of The Machine will digest, corrode, and assimilate everything that adopts as fundamental its structures and methods. Lewis shows that the restoration of localized husbandry as distinct from mass-scale manipulation is essential to the survival of man, if not physically then essentially and spiritually. Through an ironic contrast between the apocalyptic imagery of judgement and the symbolic structures of community life, C. S. Lewis offers us a map out of the Machine and back into the membership of humane communities.</p>
            <p>In <em>That Hideous Strength</em>, C. S. Lewis dramatizes both the breakdown of humane social bonds and the search for a renewed community. It is a story about how to live with faith and hope in a world threatened by chaos and tyranny. It is a story for such a time as this. On the title page, Lewis indicates that his novel is both a “Modern Fairy Tale for Grown-Ups” and a modern dystopia about the rebuilding of Babel, similar in theme and style to <em>1984</em> or <em>Brave New World. That Hideous Strength</em> is dystopian in that it shows how the rationalist values and scientistic institutions of the modern world are pulling us into a totalitarian nightmare, yet it is unlike the other great dystopian novels of the 20th century in that it promises a happy ending and a fairy-tale counterpoint to the shadow of Babel.</p>
            <p>The symbolism of Babel is central to Lewis’s novel, for it serves as the archetypal model of human history, thereby giving us the key to our future through an understanding of our past. The story of Babel reveals that God created our world to be ordered according to a sacred structure, but we repeatedly rebel against the natural order and try to replace sacred structures with technological structures. Throughout Scripture beginning with Genesis, and all throughout the history of empire, we see rebellious men attempt to overcome the curse of sin and death through technological control rather than obedience to the kingdom of God. This is what the Tower of Babel symbolizes: the human attempt to build independent kingdoms patterned not by the temple of God but by the temple of the self.</p>
            <p>Set in post-World War II England, <em>That Hideous Strength</em> juxtaposes the traditional English village of St. Anne’s with the institutional life of the new National Institute for Coordinated Experiments (NICE), a quasi-governmental organization ostensibly devoted to progress and human betterment through scientific and social engineering. For Lewis, the community of St. Anne’s symbolizes life lived according to the sacred structures of nature, while the NICE symbolizes the modern Tower of Babel, the technological city of man that works to gain power over nature. Mark Studdock, the main protagonist in the novel, becomes entangled with the NICE, drawn in by ambition and a desire to belong. As Mark rises in the ranks, he discovers the organization’s true aim: a transhuman utopia where individuality, morality, and freedom are sacrificed to overcome death through technologies created by transhuman intelligences which Lewis calls “Macrobes.”</p>
            <h3 class="section-heading">The Futility of Direct Resistance</h3>
            <p>As the plot progresses, Lewis portrays direct confrontation with the Machine as futile. Ransom, the leader of St. Anne’s, explains, “The poison was brewed in these West lands but it has spat itself everywhere by now. However far you went you would find the machines, the crowded cities, the empty thrones, the false writings, the barren beds: men maddened with false promises and soured with true miseries, worshipping the iron works of their own hands.” The omnipresence of the globalist Machine upon which our livelihood now depends renders human strategies for escape impotent, and to fight technological control with more technological control is simply to accelerate the very power that dehumanizes us and degrades our social structures. As Lewis reminds us in <em>The Abolition of Man</em>, “Man’s conquest of nature … means the rule of a few hundreds of men over billions upon billions of men. There neither is nor can be any simple increase of power on Man’s side. Each new power won by man is a power over man as well. Each advance leaves him weaker as well as stronger.” Moreover, as finite, temporal creatures, humans will never be in an epistemological position to master nature or control history, no matter how advanced our AI tools. We cannot see the whole causal landscape of the past, present, or future, much less see into the hearts of men or the purposes of God. Rescue from the Machine will not be engineered by greater control over the Machine; victory lies in a renewed obedience to the created order, not greater dominion over it.</p>
            <p>In <em>That Hideous Strength</em>, the resistance community at St. Anne’s does not develop their own technology or form a political action network; rather they wait in prayer, seek the truth, build relationships of trust, grow a garden, tend the animals, and break bread together. They refuse to live by lies or participate in corruption. They discover harmony, stability, and strength through obedience to God’s created order. In short, they resist the Machine by remaining stubbornly human. In “On Living in an Atomic Age,” Lewis argues that when faced with apocalyptic crisis “The first action to be taken is to pull ourselves together. If we are all going to be destroyed by an atomic bomb, let that bomb when it comes find us doing sensible and human things— praying, working, teaching, reading, listening to music, bathing the children, playing tennis, chatting to our friends over a pint and a game of darts—not huddled together like frightened sheep and thinking about bombs. They may break our bodies (a microbe can do that) but they need not dominate our minds.”</p>
            <p>When faced with an anti-human enemy, the community at St. Anne’s fights by returning to the fundamentally human things. The great question that differentiates those who live in the City of God from the City of Babel is this: will we conform our will to the good and beautiful order of nature or will we try to conform nature to the pleasures of our will? The key to victory over the Machine is to be a certain kind of people who live a certain way, a way that obediently conforms to the divine order of the world.</p>
            <p>When the resident skeptic MacPhee wonders “how any man in his senses thinks we’re going to defeat a powerful conspiracy by sitting here growing winter vegetables and training performing bears,” Ransom replies, “You have done what was required of you… You have obeyed and waited. It will often happen like that.” In Lewis’s novel, those on the side of humanity <em>are</em> given their providential, practical tasks to complete, but those tasks are revealed to them through the demands of virtue particular to situations as they arise, not through grand schemes for taking control.</p>
            <p>Lewis is not suggesting that we withdraw into our caves and retreat from responsibility, waiting idly for the Lord’s trumpet to sound. Rather, Lewis’s fairytale calls for a transformation of the way in which we live in the world and build our communities; he bids us reject the modern desire to “subdue reality to the wishes of men” and return to the paradigm of “wise men of old [for whom] the cardinal problem had been how to conform the soul to reality.” At some point this transformative return will require a refusal to participate in institutions or systems so compromised by the values of the Machine that they actively war against the Kingdom of God. In <em>That Hideous Strength</em>, neither Mark nor any person of integrity could work for the NICE and still retain their soul, but Mark’s rejection of the NICE is not a withdrawal from society <em>per se</em>; the Studdock’s move to St. Anne’s is a re-rooting into the kind of rightly-ordered community that can build and sustain a genuine society.</p>
            <h3 class="section-heading">Bacchanalian Judgment and the Fall of Belbury</h3>
            <p>At the end of <em>That Hideous Strength</em>, it is not the stratagems or efforts of men but the supernatural working of Providence that brings down the NICE and ends the tyranny of Babel. Through the narrative juxtaposition of the NICE and St. Anne’s, Lewis reveals the eucatastrophic irony in Babel’s fall: the very powers that destroy the Machine are also those that restore the natural community. The waters that flood the Tower of Babel also water the garden of God. This double-aspect of the prophetic tradition is developed through Lewis’s use of both Biblical and Bacchanalian imagery in the climactic scenes of <em>That</em> <em>Hideous Strength</em>.</p>
            <p>Seen most clearly in Isaiah, Joel, Amos, and Zechariah, the prophetic tradition of Israel continually presents the Day of the Lord as simultaneously both a day of judgement and a day of blessing. The exile from Eden, the Flood of Genesis, the scattering of Babel – all these are examples of the Day of the Lord, that day when the Lord calls a people to account, that day when a man or nation reaps what it has sown. As Obadiah tells us, “The day of the Lord is near for all nations. As you have done, it will be done to you; your deeds will return upon your own head.” Ultimately, there will be a great and final day of the Lord, when the whole cosmos is brought to account, every man and nation is judged, and the true Kingdom of God is revealed, but meanwhile we see all throughout Scripture and human history many “local” days of judgement.</p>
            <p>Saint John the Apostle condenses this tradition in Revelation 19, which tells us that the Beast of Babylon will fall on the great Day of the Lord when the King returns with his white robed army to judge and make war. Surprisingly, Revelation first describes this day of destruction as the marriage supper of the Lamb, a day of great rejoicing and exultation. However, when the Bridegroom arrives for the feast, His first task is to lead the armies of heaven and strike down the rebellious nations. An angel calls “to all the birds that fly overhead, ‘Come, gather for the great supper of God, to eat the flesh of kings, the flesh of captains, the flesh of mighty men, the flesh of horses and their riders, and the flesh of all men, both free and slave, both small and great.’” This strange mingling of feast and slaughter reveals the paradox of divine judgement: the same event that destroys Babylon renews creation. For those who serve the Beast, the feast is ruin; for those who serve the Lamb, it is wedding joy.</p>
            <p>Greco-Roman traditions also suggest a polyvalent understanding of civilizational judgement. In <em>The Bacchae</em> by Euripides, we see that the rites of Dionysus also suggests the dual nature of feasting: the god brings wine to Thebes to celebrate his divinity, yet he destroys King Pentheus for rejecting it, rousing the Maenads to tear him apart in ecstatic frenzy. At the climactic turn of <em>The Odyssey</em>, Homer sets the hero’s violent judgement over the invading suitors in the context of a feast. The moment before he begins his deadly vengeance, Odysseus cries, “Now is the time for their dinner to be served!” and as the first suitor falls, Homer describes the goblet of wine slipping from his hand while blood bursts from his face. Prepared for a banquet, the suitors find destruction instead. Throughout the ancient myths, wine is both the joyful gift of civilization and a potent image of violence; it symbolizes both the human and divine, the rational and ecstatic, and the civilized and primal.</p>
            <p>Lewis draws upon this ancient symbolism in his own mythic worlds. At the end of <em>Prince Caspian</em>, a story with many parallels to <em>That Hideous Strength</em>, Lewis evokes this Bacchanalian imagery to depict the renewal of Narnia through the divine judgment of Aslan. Like Ransom, the hidden King Caspian leads a resistance movement against the usurping tyrant Miraz who, like the NICE, desires to control nature and eradicate the common folk of Narnia. When Aslan returns to free the Narnians of Telmarine control, all of true Narnia breaks out into a Bacchinalian feast; trees and fairy folk dance as grape vines grow over all the land. Under Aslan’s rule, Bacchus, Silvanus, and their retinue bring life and joy back to Old Narnia, yet for the tyrant and the faithless, the renewal of nature brings dethronement and destruction. Lewis again draws from this same Bacchanalian duality to structure the climax of <em>That Hideous Strength</em>. Lewis ends his novel with a tale of two banquets, one at Belbury Manor for the leaders, funders, and administrators of the NICE and another for the resistance community living at St. Anne’s.</p>
            <p>Judgment day for the NICE thus begins with the banquet at Belbury where the leaders of the Macrobe-controlled technopoly gather to celebrate their recent triumph over Bracton College. The wine flows freely—symbolizing both the gifts of civilization and its penchant for violence, both the gladness of genuine community and the chaos of dissipation. Like Pentheus who rejects wine and the divine rites of Dionysius, the people of Belbury discover that the power which is a drink of joy to those who honor the gods is the blood of violence for those who reject them. Lewis mirrors this Janus-faced quality of wine when the same drink that fosters joy among the faithful at St. Anne’s turns to the blood of violence for those who scorn nature and nature’s God at Belbury.</p>
            <p>As the men of the NICE unknowingly drink to their own demise, judgement unfolds through the collapse of language—the curse of Babel returned. Having long abused words, filled the world with lies, and obfuscated the truth, the ability of members at the NICE to communicate collapses entirely. As one character declares, <em>“Qui Verbum Dei contempserunt</em>, <em>eis auferetur etiam verbum hominis”</em>: They that have despised the word of God, from them also shall the word of man be taken away! As the curse of Babel descends, language turns to nonsense and panic fills the halls of Belbury—a name whose very syllables foretell its judgment. Belbury, from the Anglo-Saxon <em>bealu</em> meaning bane and <em>burh</em> meaning fortress, has become the ‘evil stronghold’, the Tower of Babel rebuilt in modern England.</p>
            <p>The collapse of language leads to the second stage of judgment: having rejected moral law for the sake of technological power, all sense of restraint or duty fails, and the intoxicated people of Belbury turn on one another in violence. Fairy Hardcastle shoots Jules, the public frontman for the NICE, unleashing a stampede of “kicking, struggling, leaping on tables and under tables, pressing on and pulling back, screams, [and] breaking of glass.” As the prophet Zechariah warned, "on that day a great panic from the Lord shall fall on them, so that each will seize the hand of another, and the hand of the one will be raised against the hand of the other.” With apocalyptic frenzy, language and law alike collapse under the weight of Belbury’s institutionalized deceit.</p>
            <p>Yet meanwhile, far from the pandemonium of Belbury, a very different feast begins. At St. Anne’s—where no lies were admitted—language serves as a medium for wisdom and camaraderie, binding the community together rather than tearing it apart. In <em>That Hideous Strength</em>, Lewis imagines the medieval planets as angelic beings who descend upon Earth to wage war against the evil powers controlling the NICE. When Mercury, the Lord of Language, descends upon Belbury, words fail those who live in rebellion against the Created order of God, yet at St. Anne’s, where Ransom and his friends live in obedience to the divine <em>Logos</em>, Mercury’s descent inspires such a celebration of language as even Shakespeare himself would rejoice to hear. Lewis, a great lover of words, takes time to describe the sheer joy of good conversation: “If not plays upon words, yet certainly plays upon thoughts, paradoxes, fancies, anecdotes, theories laughingly advanced yet well worth taking seriously, had flowed from them and over them with dazzling prodigality... never in her life had she heard such talk—such eloquence, such melody (song could have added nothing to it), such toppling structures of double meaning, such skyrockets of metaphor and allusion.”</p>
            <p>When the Lord of Language descends on the wicked, babbling men of the NICE, their speech is confused and reduced to nonsense; when the same Lord of Language descends on the faithful people of St. Anne’s, their speech is enlivened and elevated. The same spirit of judgement that is chaos to the wicked is life to the faithful.</p>
            <p>Ultimately, the breakdown of language and moral order at Belbury summons the final reckoning of nature and nature’s God. As Saint Paul tells us in Galatians 6, the Lord of Heaven and Earth is not mocked and a man will one day reap what he sows. Though wicked men attempt to twist, suppress, and overcome the natural order, inevitably it rebounds and reasserts itself. Animals liberated from the experimental laboratories of the NICE invade the banquet hall and devour their tormentors. Evoking Bacchus’s beastly retinue together with Revelation’s “winepress of the fury of the wrath of God,” Lewis describes a tiger hunting her prey and a huge elephant trampling “like a girl treading grapes, heavily and soon wetly tramping in a pash of blood and bones, of flesh, wine, fruit, and sodden tablecloth.” Even Mr. Bultitude, the pet bear of St. Anne’s, joins the fray and eats the very head of the NICE, performing what Lewis calls "the best deed that any bear had done in Britain.”</p>
            <p>In the ruins of Belbury, nature becomes the instrument of judgement, yet in the village of St. Anne’s, it becomes a servant of grace. At St. Anne’s, nature is not foe but friend. Unlike Belbury’s exploited beasts, St. Anne’s menagerie lives in harmony with humans— providing help, companionship, and cheer. The Director is able to tame all manner of creatures, from the mice to the jackdaw to the lumbering bear who naps on the bathroom floor and begs for treats in the kitchen. Lewis gives his bear, Mr. Bultitude, a key role in the climactic resolution to his novel. Like Bacchus himself, whose presence joins terror and delight, Ransom’s bear embodies not only the dangerous strength, ferocity, and wildness of nature but also its maternal, affectionate, and protective aspects. Mr. Bultitude not only kills the great enemy, he also plays an important role in St. Anne’s redemptive symbolism. Ransom, we discover, is a descendent of King Arthur and inheritor of the title Pendragon, ruler of Logres. Mr. Bultitude thus provides a proper mascot for this Arthurian household, for Arthur’s name is connected to the old Celtic word for bear <em>(Artio)</em>. He is the Bear King whose dual nature as beast in war and angel at table made him a bulwark in ancient Britain against the dark agents of chaos. King Arthur stood as a fierce, protective wall between the chaos of invading darkness and the light of Christian community. So too, Ransom’s kitchen companion, Mr. Bultitude, leads the revenge of nature in his own day to keep the hideous shadow of Babel at bay one generation more.</p>
            <p>In the last stage of judgement, even the earth itself rebels against the Machine—floods, quakes, and fires destroy all the buildings under the control of the NICE. The fundamental powers of nature—language, morality, and natural law—arrogantly denied by the progressive ideology of the NICE, now exact their retribution, toppling the hideous strength of Babel. Yet in the order of divine justice, destruction and renewal arrive together. As Belbury falls, the membership at St. Anne’s feasts in Arthurian splendor—robes, crowns, table, and dance all foreshadowing the marriage supper of the Lamb. The same forces of nature that demolished the NICE—the Lord of Language, the King of Beasts, the Goddess of Desire—also strengthen and rejoice the hearts of virtuous men. At St. Anne’s, for those who remain human to the end, wine gladdens rather than curses, language binds rather than breaks, and nature serves rather than devours.</p>
            <p>This double-edged eucatastrophe at the end of <em>That Hideous Strength</em> exposes the self-destructive logic at the heart of The Machine. The world turns according to the eternal nature of the <em>Logos</em>, and the created order cannot be undone by the machinations of those within it and dependent upon it. Babel, Belbury, and all those who rebel against the goodness and beauty of creation ultimately only fuel their own demise. As Ransom concludes in the last chapter, “Those who call for Nonsense will find that it comes.” Mother nature will have her revenge.</p>
            <p>Finally, for Lewis, and the symbolic tradition on which he stands, the essential key to remaining human is to remember our nature as male and female made together to image the glory of God. Marriage is the inescapable foundation for a proper connection to the created order. <em>That Hideous Strength</em> begins with the word matrimony, which literally means mothermaking, and the story ends with the restored and repentant Mark finally making a mother of his wife, Jane. The fruitful union of man and wife, symbolic of the marriage between heaven and earth, is the means by which the world is reborn. The abolition of marriage is the abolition of Man, but the renewal of marriage is the restoration of Man. Mother-making marriage is both the origin and the goal of human life, for it is by the marriage of heaven and earth that we are created and it is <em>for</em> the free, conscious, consummation of this marriage that we are destined. This is Lewis’s fairytale ending: not technological liberation from nature, but loving harmony with nature and nature’s God through the faithful marriage of heaven and earth. Only through obedience to the order of creation are we essentially joined to the Creator of that order.</p>
            <p>Through the perennial symbolism of the apocalyptic tradition, <em>That Hideous Strength</em> charts a path out of the modern Machine and back into human community. The vision Lewis gives us is no passive, fearful retreat but a radical reaffirmation of our fundamental humanity against the anti-humanism of the Machine. Those who steward creation according to natural law will endure when judgment falls while those who rebel against the created order pull down Deep Heaven upon their heads. Nations rise and nations fall, but the truly human—prayer, work, friendship, love—abides. More technology cannot redeem us from technology’s curse; greater control cannot rescue us from tyranny; only faithful husbandry in obedience to the created order can—by God’s grace—renew our world.</p>
            <p class="contributor-bio">Annie Crawford is a cultural apologist and classical educator with a Master of Arts in cultural apologetics from Houston Christian University. She is the director for the Certificate in C.S. Lewis Studies at the Logres Institute for Classical Liberal Studies, and her work on story, symbolism, and education has appeared at the Blyth Institute, <em>The Consortium Journal</em>, <em>Salvo</em>, <em>An Unexpected Journal</em>, <em>The Symbolic World</em>, and <em>Sehnsucht: The C. S. Lewis Journal</em>.</p>
          </section>

