          <section id="we-still-need-fairy" class="content-section">
            <h2 class="section-title">We Still Need Fairy Tales</h2>
            <p class="essay-author">By Ashley Hales</p>

            <p class="essay-heading">Jack Zipes. <em>Once Upon a Time There Was Truth or, Why We Need Fairy Tales</em>. Yale University Press, 2026. $28.00. 200 pp.</p>
            <p>We live in a moment in which the mere act of paying attention is a problem, or perhaps a challenge. We know it’s possible. Simply think about the enchantment you felt with stories in your childhood: Do you remember your own wide eyes and mouth agape as you leaned in to listen to a fantastic tale? Perhaps it was King Arthur and his round table or the pixie dust that Tinkerbell bestowed so the Darling children could fly that took you from your modern world to some place outside of time and place. Maybe it was the tales of the <em>One Thousand and One Nights</em> or Robinson Crusoe’s daring adventures that opened up the possibility of enchantment, change, and perseverance through bleak trials. Fairy tales give us a framework for imaginative possibility. They might also restore our attention.</p>
            <p>Jack Zipes, professor emeritus of German and comparative literature at the University of Minnesota, has been writing fairy tales—and writing about fairy tales—for sixty years. Now approaching 90, he still believes the genre is borne from our utopian “hopeful desires.” <em>Once Upon a Time There was Truth or, Why We Need Fairy Tales</em> is a collection of his essays on the development of the fairy tale from oral tales to the genre’s more contemporary “Disneyfication.” The genre has changed along the way. And yet, he writes, “if there is any genre of storytelling that can raise our spirits and make a contribution to humanity…it is the fairy tale.”</p>
            <p>As a collection of separate essays, most of the book’s chapters focus on various tales: “Puss in Boots,” “Hansel and Gretel,” “The Nutcracker,” Disney’s versions of classic tales, and <em>The Wizard of Oz</em>. Throughout, Zipes draws attention to the pedagogical and socializing functions of fairy tales, especially as they begin to be told to children. For Zipes there is a hardening of the genre throughout the 20th century as Disney’s pervasive influence flattens the genre’s subversive possibilities. Yet, the fairy tale isn’t just about a “happily ever after.”</p>
            <p>Zipes traces the evolution of the form from oral tales to a literary form that flourished from the early 19th century to the early 20th century. Different from myth and oral wonder tales, the fairy tale both substantiated civilizing norms while also subverting them. There is a logic to their telling: a protagonist violates a norm and is then faced with banishment or an impossible task. She or he then has various encounters—with a villain, or magical animals or a creature who bestows gifts, thereby enabling the protagonist to tackle the task while a wonder or a miracle also reverses his or her fortune. The result is often marriage and wealth.</p>
            <p>Especially in the genre’s golden age, folklorists (like the Brothers Grimm) very much saw their work of gathering and writing down these oral tales to be an integral part of the nation-building project. These “middle-class collectors [gave] voice to the lower classes… and wrote in defense of their native languages and in the interests of national and regional movements.” The nineteenth century literary fairy tale, then, began as a political statement—one that validated the common folk— and while contemporary tales have been more likely to “entertain and soothe a child’s anxieties,” the genre still maintains the power to inspire and subvert. Zipes’ project, then, is not only to recover the history and shifts of the genre but also to name an “impulse for hope for better living conditions.”</p>
            <p>But is the fairy tale simply a social construct, meant to either civilize or comment on social norms? When literary critics evaluate the genre, is it only to class and nation we must look? Must we understand the Disneyfication of fairy tales in terms of perpetuating patriarchal norms? While the fairy tale offers commentary on our present, something much deeper is going on in the genre. This is the nature of truth and reality.</p>
            <p>Zipes’ title, <em>Once Upon a Time There Was Truth</em>, links “truth” with the fairy tale, but this truth is located within the fairy tale itself. The implication is that truth cannot be found in the readers’ present circumstances and that the tale motivates the reader to align the truth of the tale with the truth of experience. By presenting the world as it should be—where anyone is capable of social climbing and the villain gets his just due—the fairy tale as Zipes writes in his Epilogue, offers “happily ever after” and “once upon a time” as “a persistent refusal to accept life as it is and a demand that utopian longings be fulfilled.” For Zipes the genre presents possibilities for transformation as we compare our society with the counter world of the fairy tale.</p>
            <p>But there is little surprise in Zipes’ readings. There <em>is</em> talk of wonder and magic but there is little magic in the prose. In the book there is not much that gestures to deeper human longings beyond political, national, or gender-based categories. The responsibility is squarely on the reader to recognize “something [is] still missing, deeply missing even, in our lives” and through the world of the fairy tale “to seize on [glimmers of hope] as we tell our tales, and futures, today. Zipes argues that we “insert ourselves cunningly into our daily struggles to turn the course of the world’s events in our favor.” While he has no love for Disneyfication, ultimately Zipes advocates for the same project—to turn our tales into self-made dreams.</p>
            <p>Zipes correctly diagnoses how fairy tales name the longing for the world to be different than it is, but he misses the cure. This realization that the world is not how it should be and a resulting longing for a magical intervention aligns with the Christian reality. C.S. Lewis famously wrote in <em>Mere Christianity</em>: “If I find in myself desires which nothing in this world can satisfy, the only logical explanation is that I was made for another world.” Fairy tales elicit this longing. Lewis places the truth not in the gap between tale and unfulfilled longing, but in the transcendent reality of God’s creative work. Christian hope tells a story—not of writing one’s destiny and future—but of a good created order, a fall into sin, redemption through the work of Christ, and a future restoration of all things.</p>
            <p>Fairy tales—fairy stories, or true myths as Lewis and Tolkien call them—still have an important role to play. They awaken us to imaginative reversal. J.R.R. Tolkien wrote about the moment of recognition, or joyful turn, implicit in the fairy tale. Delivered first as a lecture, he wrote in <em>On Fairy Stories</em>: “It is the mark of a good fairy-story, of the higher or more complete kind, that however wild its events, however fantastic or terrible the adventures, it can give to child or man that hears it, when the ‘turn’ comes, a catch of the breath, a beat and lifting of the heart, near to (or indeed accompanied by) tears, as keen as that given by any form of literary art, and having a peculiar quality.” More than just “an agent of socialization,” the fairy tale can hit at something deeper: a moment of surprise, a <em>eucatastrophe</em>. This is the narrative moment where we have, in Tolkien’s words, a “sudden glimpse of the underlying reality or truth.”</p>
            <p>The truth that fairy stories give is “not only a ‘consolation’ for the sorrow of this world, but a satisfaction, and an answer to that question…In the ‘Eucatastrophe’ we see in a brief vision that the answer may be greater—it may be a far-off gleam or echo of <em>evangelium</em> in the real world.” This moment of realization, where all is restored in a manner that is both good and true, also ushers readers and hearers into what Tolkien calls a “Secondary Belief,” a real enchantment, an experience of good news.</p>
            <p>Martin Shaw in his new book, <em>Liturgies of the Wild: The Myths that Make Us</em>, reminds us how our stories point to true myth. Like Zipes, he notes that myths or fairy tales serve a social and pedagogical function. He believes myths make us human, they help us grow up, and they help us locate ourselves. “Far from being untruths, myths have traditionally been forefront in the shaping of human beings. They show us what to defend, what to care about, the need for pluck, sacrifice, and generosity.” Myths are guides.</p>
            <p>But in moments, myths are more than guides. They pierce through the immanent frame, to get at the heart of reality. <em>“Myth is a Sacred Story,”</em> Shaw writes, “And some of the most sacred of those stories circle around the tale of a dying and resurrected king. Who carried a great burden for his people and turned that burden into an extraordinary act of love. And some of us believe that the most sacred story crash-bang-walloped into the kind of time we can all register, <em>ours.”</em></p>
            <p>Zipes would argue that we ultimately need fairy tales so that we can assert ourselves between the gap of our reality and the world as it should or could be. Shaw (and Tolkien and Lewis) would instead point to the nature of narrative itself: The best fairy tales do not primarily tell us what to do but what must be done on our behalf. They hold out a world where justice rolls down like streams of mighty water, where the lion lays down with the lamb, where the destitute pauper becomes a king. The best fairy tales succeed to the extent that joy catches in our throats and tear ducts so that finally we <em>see</em>: Our King has come, and He will come again to make all things new.</p>
            <p class="contributor-bio">Ashley Hales holds a Ph.D. in English from the University of Edinburgh and is director of storytelling with the Denver Institute, co-founder of the Willowbrae Institute, and author of several books.</p>
          </section>

          <!-- ====== ARTICLE 9 ====== -->
          <section id="to-shew-the-truth" class="content-section">
            <h2 class="section-title">“To Shew the Truth Of the Evangelical Historie”: The Apologetics of Nathaniel Lardner</h2>
            <p class="essay-author">By Daniel K. Williams</p>

            <p><em>This excerpt from</em> The Search for a Rational Faith: Reason and Belief in the History of American Christianity <em>(2026) is published with permission from Oxford University Press</em>.</p>
            <p>Any Londoner who visited Chew’s Coffeehouse on a Monday evening in the 1720s or 1730s and made his way through the haze of smoke coming from clay pipes and the excited chattering of small groups of men passionately debating the latest news would have encountered, among other gatherings at the scattered tables, a meeting of a local literary club. Coffeehouse intellectual clubs were common in the early eighteenth century, when Enlightenment would-be philosophers, gadflies, and local wags from the middle and upper classes gathered in a caffeine-fueled environment to discuss the latest ideas and test them with the standard of reason. The gathering at Chew’s Coffeehouse was, like others of its type, a venue for the intellectually curious. Each Monday evening, the chairman of the club proposed two questions for debate, and each member of the club presented an essay for discussion.</p>
            <p>These clubs were sometimes a magnet for deists and skeptics, but at this particular society, one of the members, Nathaniel Lardner, was a London minister—but one who was just as committed to reason as any deist. He was already in his forties at this point and so hard of hearing that he had trouble figuring out when the choir at his church had stopped singing, but he still loved to discuss ideas with people who held contrary opinions. He was also intellectually flexible enough to change his mind when presented with new evidence.</p>
            <p>As a lifelong Dissenter, he was used to being a nonconformist theologically and intellectually, and as a result, he enjoyed corresponding with deists, even though he thought they were entirely wrong. He was certainly no stranger to skepticism, because his pursuit of graduate training in theology had taken him to such leading centers of skeptical opinion as Utrecht, Leiden, and finally back to London. While participating in the literary club, he was in the process of rethinking his view of the Trinity. At first, he ardently defended the orthodox position, but after a few years of study, he became convinced by Unitarian arguments that the Son was not eternally preexistent, as orthodox Christians claimed ever since the Council of Nicaea.</p>
            <p>In some respects, Lardner was a bit of an oddball. He never married, he lived with his father until he was in his mid-fifties, and, despite his graduate education at some of Europe’s best universities, he found it impossible to secure a permanent ministerial post until he was forty-five—and even then, he served in pulpit ministry for only a short time.</p>
            <p>The chief obstacle that he faced in finding ministerial employment was probably not his passion for argument or his propensity to change his mind on such key points of Christian doctrine as the Trinity—the latter of which might have alienated him from some of the more theologically conservative Presbyterian churches of his youth, but which was no obstacle for the increasing number of Unitarian-minded congregations among the English Dissenters. Rather, one of his biographers speculated, his greatest barrier to parish ministry was probably his near-deafness, which prevented his friends from communicating with him except by passing him written notes.</p>
            <p>Lardner’s coffeehouse friends were apparently willing to tolerate the inconvenience, but he seems to have felt self-conscious about imposing his disability on a congregation. When a leading Dissenting church invited him to serve as co-pastor, he turned down the offer. Though he did preach frequently, he would primarily make his mark on Christian apologetics through his writings. And it would be through the written word that he would remain devoted to the life of the mind, exploring new ideas and choosing to accept or reject them on the basis of reason.</p>
            <p>Changes in intellectual conviction, when presented with sufficient evidence, were common for Lardner, because he believed in freedom of thought. In the late 1720s, when the London deist Thomas Woolston was arrested and imprisoned for repeatedly publishing material denying Jesus’s miracles, Lardner argued that the deist had the right to hold his own religious opinions without coercion, even if they might be erroneous. “Nothing is so voluntary as Religion,” he wrote, quoting the Dominican friar Raymond Martini. “Faith is to be wrought by persuasion, not by compulsion.”</p>
            <p>In the interest of producing this “persuasion” by rational means, Lardner published a book explaining why it was “rational, consistent, and credible” to interpret the accounts of three of Jesus’s most spectacular miracles— the raising of three people from the dead—as “literal histories.” He accomplished this task by carefully comparing each of the gospels with the others in order to show that the gospel writers were not prone to distortions and exaggerations when recounting the same events, but instead demonstrated the qualities of careful, trustworthy reporters. The deists therefore had no grounds to dismiss the accounts of Jesus’s miracles. Lardner’s <em>A Vindication of Three of Our Blessed Savior’s Miracles</em>, as he titled this refutation of the deists’ arguments, was only the beginning of his career as a Christian apologist. After spending his Monday evenings each week at the coffeeshop conversing with the literati, he spent his Tuesday evenings among ministers whom he hoped to turn into apologists.</p>
            <p>The young Dissenting ministers who gathered with Lardner each Tuesday at a meetinghouse in the heart of London’s financial district were an eclectic bunch theologically, because “Dissenter” in early eighteenth-century England was a broad category that encompassed nearly every Protestant outside the Church of England— which meant everyone from Puritans to Unitarians. Besides Lardner, the group included two Baptists, three Presbyterians, and one Congregationalist whose views on scripture were suspected of being somewhat liberal.</p>
            <p>Perhaps they could have spent their time bickering over their divergent interpretations of Christian doctrine if they had wanted, but they decided instead to focus on a more constructive project: the defense of the Christian faith against deism. Each minister in the group, they decided, would deliver a short series of sermons on some aspect of the “the evidences of natural and revealed religion”—in other words, natural theology and a reasoned defense of the divine origin of scripture.</p>
            <p>For his own set of three lectures, Lardner chose to focus specifically on refuting historical criticisms that deists made of the four gospels. The topic quickly became a life focus for Lardner. His three lectures expanded into a two-volume book, published in 1730, but this was only the beginning: he continued working on the project throughout his life, until it eventually reached seventeen volumes.</p>
            <p>Lardner’s work functioned as the first comprehensive encyclopedia of historical criticism of the gospels, organized around a list of problematic passages. For each passage whose historical accuracy skeptics had questioned, Lardner engaged in a detailed, exhaustive analysis of every classical reference or piece of historical evidence that might have bearing on the question. He left no stone unturned. His discussion of Luke’s one-verse reference to the census of Quirinius, for instance, continued for eighty-six pages. In each case, Lardner attempted to show that historical evidence vindicated the gospel accounts. The census of Quirinius, he argued, was historically plausible, despite what the deists had said. And so was every other gospel passage that the deists had questioned.</p>
            <p>Lardner especially focused on the question of whether information that was mentioned in only one of the four gospels—or that was never mentioned in Josephus or any classical histories—could be trusted. For instance, was it reasonable to believe John’s account of the raising of Lazarus when that miracle—as spectacular as it was— did not appear in any of the Synoptic gospels? Was it reasonable to believe that the Roman emperor Augustus had ordered an empire-wide census, as Luke suggested, even though this was never mentioned in any classical source?</p>
            <p>Lardner treated these skeptical questions seriously and respectfully, and unlike many previous Christian apologists, he never resorted to invective or denigration when debating his skeptical opponents. Instead, he treated their criticisms of the biblical text as an opportunity for serious intellectual investigation, and at each point, he thought that an exhaustive study of all relevant ancient sources and all possible objections would show that, in the end, the biblical account was credible. His work became a massive compendium of all possible confirmations of the historical narrative of the four gospels from the nonbiblical sources of classical antiquity.</p>
            <p>Lardner believed in the historical accuracy of the Bible, but he was hardly an adamantine reactionary; he was open to novel interpretations, as long as they sustained the Bible’s historicity. In the case of the census of Quirinius, for instance, he argued for a very limited imperial order that applied only to Judea—which explained its absence from classical sources. This meant that the Authorized Version’s rendering of Luke 2:1 as “all the world should be taxed” was incorrect; a more accurate translation, Lardner argued from the Greek, was “all the land should be taxed”— thus making it possible that the census could apply only to Judea.</p>
            <p>Lardner’s methodology and presuppositions were in keeping with Enlightenment rationalism, but his end goal was conservative: he wanted to defend the historical integrity and authenticity of the original Greek biblical text, because he believed, as John Locke had, that if he could prove that the gospel narrative was historically trustworthy, its divine origins and authority would be confirmed as well. Jesus’s miracles and resurrection, he believed, could have occurred only through the power of God, so if he could prove that these miracles really happened—that is, that the reports about them were historically credible—he had essentially proved the truth of Christianity and subverted the deists. This was such an important goal that it was worth becoming the project of a lifetime.</p>
            <p>Three decades after embarking on his project, Lardner, now seventy years old, reflected on the purpose of his undertaking as he picked up his pen to begin the fourteenth volume of his ever- expanding work. “The design of this work, from the beginning, and all along,” he wrote, “has been to shew the truth of the evangelical historie, and thereby the truth of the Christian Religion. For if the facts related in the Gospels, and confirmed by the Epistles of the New Testament, may be relied upon, the Christian Religion is from heaven.”</p>
            <p>In 1757, thirty-three years after he had begun the project—and after battling several severe fevers that nearly killed him—Lardner finally finished the seventeenth volume and put down his pen. But even though he wanted to write more, the multivolume analysis that he did produce was so comprehensive that it was not repeated for more than a century.</p>
            <p>Lardner taught orthodox Christians that they did not have to be afraid of skeptical objections; exhaustive historical study, he showed, would refute the critics’ charges and uphold the historicity of the biblical text.</p>
            <p class="contributor-bio">Daniel K. Williams teaches American history at Ashland University. His other books include <em>The Politics of the Cross: A Christian Alternative to Partisanship</em>, and <em>Defenders of the Unborn: The Pro-Life Movement Before Roe v. Wade</em>.</p>
          </section>

          <!-- ====== ARTICLE 10 ====== -->
          <section id="something-wicked-this-way" class="content-section">
            <h2 class="section-title">Something Wicked This Way Comes</h2>
            <p class="essay-author">By Beatrice Scudeler</p>

            <p class="essay-heading">Carrie Gress. <em>Something Wicked: Why Feminism Can’t Be Fused with Christianity</em>. Sophia Institute Press, 2026. $21.95. 256 pp.</p>
            <p>Carrie Gress’ new book certainly begins on an ominous note. Her central argument, as the subtitle has it, is that “feminism can’t be fused with Christianity.” It’s either God or the devil. Choose at your own peril.</p>
            <p>The story goes something like this. Once, before Enlightenment rationalism and revolutionary ideals took over—replacing faith in God and in God-given hierarchies—Western people lived peacefully. Women accepted patriarchy as ordained by God in his wisdom, and the relationship between the sexes was harmonious. When Mary Wollstonecraft joined forces with the big bads of Enlightenment philosophy (Hume et al.), she threw the “woman issue” into the mix. Over two hundred years later, feminism has repeatedly aligned itself with dangerous ideologies, from Marxism and communism to occult practices, and more recently to the pro-choice movement. It’s time to eradicate this way of thought and free women from their feminist shackles. Only then will Christianity prevail once again.</p>
            <p><em>Something Wicked</em> proposes an attractive intellectual history of feminism. Yet overly tidy narratives are often untrue. I believe it’s important for Christians to consider arguments both in favour and against any given ideology, but we should focus on the best of those arguments, so that we may develop our capacity to reason well. Unfortunately, Gress doesn’t deliver on her promise of a rigorous, methodical takedown of feminism as an ideology. Her account is partial at best, and misleading at worst.</p>
            <p>This would not particularly matter, were it not for the fact that Gress has considerable public credibility: she holds a PhD from the Catholic University of America (where she is a fellow at the Institute for Human Ecology), she is a former fellow of the Ethics and Public Policy Center, and has several books already under her belt (including her popular <em>Theology of Home</em> series). <em>Something Wicked</em> has been endorsed by well-respected scholars such as the psychiatrist and philosopher Ian McGilchrist, and Gress regularly promotes her books on Catholic media outlets. She has enough influence over young Christian women that we must engage with her ideas, if only to decide that we won’t be recommending <em>Something Wicked</em> to our sisters, nieces, and daughters.</p>
            <h3 class="section-heading">A False Premise</h3>
            <p>The book’s greatest flaw is that it is based on a false premise. Gress claims that Christian women have been fooled into trying to reconcile two incompatible ideologies, feminism and Christianity. As feminism has grown more powerful, Christianity correspondingly declined.</p>
            <p>There are several problems with this claim.</p>
            <p>First, it posits Christianity and feminism as competing ideologies. This is false. While feminism fits the description of an ideology (i.e., a set of beliefs which can be used to shape social, economic, or political action), Christianity can only be considered an ideology if viewed as a cultural set of practices and morals (what one might call “thin Christianity”). By contrast, “thick” Christianity is a practice of faith. It’s an all-encompassing worldview—in the words of C.S. Lewis, “I believe in Christianity as I believe that the sun has risen: not only because I see it, but because by it I see everything else.” Of course, some ideologies are more dangerous or, conversely, more aligned with the Christian faith than others; but anything good to be found in any given ideology (whether that is conservatism, socialism, etc.) will inevitably be contained within Christianity.</p>
            <p>There is no competition there, Gress implies. There is, however, a choice for Christians. They may choose to retreat altogether from the political issues of secular society as they view engagement as futile or even corruptive. Or they may choose to engage with particular political ideologies that they judge to be conducive to the fostering and spreading of Christian faith, doctrine, or moral teaching.</p>
            <p>This will inevitably lead to disagreement. Some have objected to the Marxist influence on 20th century Catholic liberation theology. Others point out that Christianity has repeatedly been weaponized by far-right governments (most famously, of course, during Hitler’s regime). But as long as it remains clear that our true “citizenship is in heaven” (Philippians 3:20), Christians can engage in public life by subscribing to specific sets of political goals. It is, in theory at least, entirely possible for individuals to identify as “feminists” while retaining their overarching, inalienable allegiance to the Kingdom of God.</p>
            <p>The question, then, is not actually whether Christianity can be fused with feminism, as Gress claims, but whether feminism is worth supporting. Gress very confidently answers in the negative. She sees such a vast divide in how philosophers pre- and post-Enlightenment envision ideas of virtue, duty, and faith, that any post-Enlightenment social and political movement, such as feminism, is in the grip of secularism. Thus it can’t contribute anything morally acceptable to faithful Christians.</p>
            <p>This is a compelling narrative, because it provides a simple solution to a very complex problem. But here we stumble upon the second issue with Gress’s central claim: Correlation does not necessarily imply causation.</p>
            <p>Gress has correctly identified the inverse correlation between the rise of Enlightenment philosophy and the decline of Christianity. It is the rest of her argument that gets muddled. Two of her main targets, Mary Wollstonecraft and Erika Bachiochi, exemplify her view of the role of feminism within this decline. She blames Wollstonecraft, an 18th-century writer who advocated for improved female education within the developing philosophical context of human rights, for promoting a genderless idea of woman. And she blames Bachochi, a contemporary legal scholar who has argued in her book <em>The Rights of Women: Reclaiming a Lost Vision</em> that Wollstonecraft in fact presented a view of rights based on mutual duties and responsibility, for trying to merge feminism with Christianity.</p>
            <h3 class="section-heading">The Enlightenment Chasm</h3>
            <p>Gress requires her readers to make several assumptions as part of this blame game. First, we would have to agree that feminism began with Mary Wollstonecraft. Next, we’d have to accept that Wollstonecraft was irredeemably immersed in rationalistic and empirical philosophy, to the point that her views on women’s rights and her political priorities more broadly can’t be separated from her Enlightenment-style abandonment of God and the embracing of Deism. We would also need to agree that she did, in fact, abandon a belief in the divine. Then, we would have to prove that all the figures associated with the women’s rights movement and with subsequent waves of feminism accepted Wollstonecraft’s views and continued directly on that path, and that the views of contemporary feminists are indeed a direct result of her ideas.</p>
            <p>Gress simply doesn’t substantiate these claims. The first inconsistency is her treatment of the industrial revolution. She is not unaware of the legal and technological changes which contributed to the sweeping changes of the 18th century. In her introduction, she <em>does</em> name industrialization, marriage and property laws (which left women unable to support themselves if their husbands passed away or if they were abusive), secularization, and middle to upper class women being treated as ornamental as relevant factors.</p>
            <p>And yet, she promptly forgets all this. In the second part of <em>Something Wicked</em>, she goes into a little more detail, claiming that “Homelife was significantly disrupted by a surge in factories and the mechanization of work, as small cottage industries were swallowed up by enormous factories. The home ceased to be the locus of activity.” This is a well-known phenomenon in social history, with which I entirely agree, and to which Christopher Lasch devoted much of his seminal book <em>Women and the Common Life</em>. But the next step in Gress’s argument is a historical non sequitur. “The Industrial Revolution,” she continues, “coupled with the disvaluing of female gifts created a vacuum that the feminist ideology easily moved into. The simultaneous loss of home and mother eliminated the glue that holds Western civilization together.”</p>
            <p>Gress does not take into consideration the fact that many “feminists” (though the term is anachronistic for the late 18th century) in fact campaigned against the injustices and inequalities that arose as a result of said technological changes. As English society became more industrial and less agrarian, women were still expected to contribute to the household economy, but now they worked long shifts in factories, away from the home, and often were paid less than men (another change was that wage work became the norm). Small children were often brought along and had to start working while still very young until child labour laws kicked in in the latter half of the 19th century. None of this is the consequence of “feminist ideology.” Under such conditions, is it surprising that women started to campaign for fair wages? Or for the right to purchase property in their own right?</p>
            <p>The less interdependent society becomes, the more harm women and children incur. On that, Gress and I agree. But why, I kept thinking as I read this book, does she not put two and two together, and realize that figures of the women’s rights movement were in large part critiquing a situation not of their own making?</p>
            <p>When it comes to middle- and upper-class women, Gress admits that in the 18th century their role was often ornamental. That is precisely Mary Wollstonecraft’s complaint, and yet Gress fails to notice any affinity between Wollstonecraft’s thought and her own. Her argument instead rests on the premises that 1) Wollstonecraft is the mother of feminism, and 2) she sowed the seeds of all the societal evils we see today.</p>
            <h3 class="section-heading">Witch Hunting</h3>
            <p>Gress’s first wicked witch is Mary Wollstonecraft. She is not the first woman to write about female education (Christine de Pizan and Mary Astell are two prominent predecessors, though neither is mentioned), but Gress takes Wollstonecraft’s work as the beginning of all the evils of feminism.</p>
            <p>One reason for this is that, along with the French proto-feminist Olympe de Gouges, Wollstonecraft was an early supporter of the French Revolution, which Gress takes to signify a wholesale rejection of any notions of hierarchy or authority. Sadly, she neglects to mention that writers such as de Gouges were ultimately guillotined for criticizing Robespierre’s Reign of Terror, and that Wollstonecraft herself became disillusioned with the excesses of the revolution. Gress also criticizes Wollstonecraft’s promotion of female education as opposed to Christianity, despite the fact that Wollstonecraft’s <em>Female Reader</em>, an anthology for young girls, includes many scriptural passages.</p>
            <p>Her greatest accusation, however, is that Wollstonecraft has an erroneous understanding of virtue. She believes that Wollstonecraft followed David Hume, who saw morality as merely based on feelings of approval or disapproval and as detached from divine authority. This allows Gress to discredit the entirety of Wollstonecraft’s thought as being severed from the Aristotelian-Thomistic tradition of Christian philosophy.</p>
            <p>However, Wollstonecraft’s account of the virtues does not, in fact, square with Hume’s. She simply doesn’t advocate for moral relativism in the way that Gress needs her to do, in order to prove that feminism was rotten from the very beginning.</p>
            <p>Many other witches follow Wollstonecraft in Gress’ account, from those who engaged in occult practices, like Charlotte Perkins Gilman and Elizabeth Cady Stanton, to those who were hostile to Christianity, like Matilda Gage; from the terrorist UK suffragettes like Emily Pankhurst, to the abortion rights advocates of second-wave feminism like Kate Millett and Shulamith Firestone.</p>
            <p>Yet for each of these figures, there are women whom Gress neglects. Many first-wave women’s rights advocates, contrary to Gress’ claim that “Feminism is abortion’s engine,” openly opposed abortion. Susan B. Anthony and Mary Fenn Davis are just two of the examples Monica Klem and Madeleine McDowell discuss in <em>Pity for Evil: Suﬀrage, Abortion, and Women’s Empowerment in Reconstruction America</em> (2023). Not all feminists were irreligious, either. The 19th-century abolitionist and women’s suffrage activists Sarah and Angelina Grimké, for example, were devout Quakers. In the UK, between the late 19th- and early 20th-centuries the suffragettes were opposed by the <em>suﬀragists</em>. While also seeking women’s suffrage, the suffragists campaigned peacefully under the leadership of Millicent Garrett Fawcett. Among their ranks was Josephine Butler (not to be confused with the contemporary gender theorist Judith Butler), a devout Anglican Christian who, among other efforts, also campaigned for the abolition of child prostitution and human trafficking. Closer to our time, Gress ignores Andrea Dworkin, who radically (and unpopularly) condemned both prostitution and pornography in the 1970s and 80s, just as sex-positive feminism was gaining momentum. These women don’t deserve to be forgotten.</p>
            <h3 class="section-heading">The Decay of the Christian West</h3>
            <p>The purpose of Gress’s witch-hunting is to prove that all kinds of contemporary social ills, including porn addiction, transgender ideology, single-sex parents adoption, child trafficking, prostitution, and drug addiction (her list, not mine) can be blamed on feminism. Men bear no blame in this calculus. According to Gress, men are rejecting marriage because women have made themselves too sexually available after the sexual revolution; the existence of the manosphere, while unfortunate, is a predictable reaction against feminism; domestic violence has become “commonplace,” but that is also, somehow, the fault of women.</p>
            <p>As we have seen, Gress is arguing that Christianity declined because of feminism, and that the only way for it to flourish again is for feminism to be eradicated. Just as the original fall is (reminds Gress) about Eve’s sin, so has our contemporary decline been at the hands of women. <em>Something Wicked’s</em> target readership is young girls and mothers, and I doubt that coming this close to outright misogyny is a helpful strategy for the supposed goal of converting women’s hearts.</p>
            <p>It’s very difficult to read such an account and not walk away with the suspicion that <em>Something Wicked</em> is somewhat of an example of grifting. Given the number of commentators who have recently argued in favour of cultural Christianity as a means to reclaim traditional Western culture, Gress’s book catches the zeitgeist of a certain right-wing, religious-adjacent subculture. For example, she extensively engages with (and praises) the thought of Jordan Peterson— who, while very open about the West’s need for the Christian faith, has not openly converted. She likewise praises Erica Komisar, who, as of 2022, was openly opposed to restrictions on abortion in the wake of the overturn of <em>Roe v. Wade</em> (a view which should be anathema to Gress).</p>
            <p>Yet on the other hand, she accuses “a new group of Catholic women” of trying to “forge together” the teachings of Pope John Paul II with those of feminists and proto-feminists like Mary Wollstonecraft. Gress even comes dangerously close to slandering one of said women, Erika Bachiochi, by claiming that the blending of Catholicism and secularism—as evidenced the fact that Catholic women “contracept” and “abort” at similar rates to secular women—is best seen in Bachiochi’s work. This could easily be interpreted by unaware readers to mean that Bachiochi herself approves of contraception and abortion—but, in fact, she is a Catholic mother of seven who is publicly pro-life. If not outright slander, this is at best an <em>ad hominem</em> attack.</p>
            <p>My point is not that Gress isn’t allowed to take secular thinkers seriously—there is indeed a long Christian tradition of doing precisely this—but rather that her intellectual allegiances reveal a confused set of priorities. Why would Gress close off potentially fruitful debate with fellow Christians in such a vindictive manner?</p>
            <p>Ultimately, Gress will leave readers unsure of whether the goal is to develop a truly Christian vision of womanhood, or to restore political patriarchy and with it the “Christian West.” Gress would undoubtedly see these goals as interdependent, but they are not necessarily so. I wholeheartedly agree with Gress that women and children have historically been most cherished and safeguarded in those cultures where the influence of the Christian faith has been stronger. But this is because of the very tenets of the Christian faith. Contrary to Gress’s claim that we needed political patriarchy for the “dissemination and maintenance of Christianity,” Christianity had already been spreading before Constantine I made it the official religion of the Roman empire, in part because its insistence of equal dignity was so attractive to women.</p>
            <p>And to Gress’ claim that “civilization depends” on finding a road out of feminism, I would retort that it depends on finding a road out of secularism instead. St Augustine argued that Rome fell because of its own moral decadence, and that peace can only be found in the city of God. So, too, we should remember that adopting the Christian faith should not be a matter of civilizational flourishing, but rather of belief. It’s disappointing to see Gress, a sincere Christian, tap into the trend of cultural Christianity.</p>
            <p>Whether the intention is the genuine flourishing of a Christian society or merely the restoration of the Christian West, a movement as diverse in both political aims and philosophical underpinnings as feminism is the perfect scapegoat. I share Gress’ desire for motherhood and domestic work to be once again valued. But I can’t take <em>Something Wicked</em> seriously as a work of scholarship, because it is lacking in rigour and riddled with logical fallacies. In fact, its greatest flaw is not that it fails as an intellectual history of feminism (though it does that, too), but that it will inevitably teach its intended audience (i.e., young women) to reason badly. It teaches them to look for single-factor, simplistic causes to explain complex social phenomena spanning centuries. And it teaches them to adopt a tribal outlook in their intellectual life, dismissing any thinkers who don’t neatly fall into the group who hold the “right opinion,” rather than examining thinkers individually, their good and bad ideas alike.</p>
            <p>I will not be giving this book to my daughter once she is old enough. I <em>will</em> expose her to arguments both for and against feminism so that she may grapple with them and come to her own conclusions, but <em>Something Wicked</em> won’t be my book of choice.</p>
            <p class="contributor-bio">Beatrice Scudeler is a writer and journalist, whose work examines how literature and storytelling more broadly intersect with issues of religion, feminism, and motherhood. She is a featured author at Fairer Disputations, and she has also written for <em>The Critic</em>, <em>First Things</em>, <em>Plough</em>, and <em>Public Discourse</em> among others.</p>
          </section>

          <!-- ====== ARTICLE 11 ====== -->
          <section id="man-vs-infectious-disease" class="content-section">
            <h2 class="section-title">Man vs. Infectious Disease</h2>
            <p class="essay-author">By Matthew Loftus</p>

            <p class="essay-heading">Susan Wise Bauer. <em>The Great Shadow: A History of How Sickness Shapes What We Do, Think, Believe, and Buy</em>. St. Martin’s Press, 2026. $30.00. 352 pp.</p>
            <p>Death from infectious disease feels wrong to us in the twenty-first century. Such deaths are rare enough in developed, wealthy countries to make headlines or launch inquiries after they happen to young, otherwise healthy people—because they are often preventable. Such deaths do not happen every day in my work at a mission hospital in rural Kenya, but when they do, almost always one can identify the point where the system broke down and a preventable death became inevitable.</p>
            <p>This feeling of wrongness is the result of what scholars call the Second Epidemiological Transition—the massive changes in quality and length of life brought about by modern medicine, mostly in the first half of the twentieth century. Before antibiotics and vaccines, deadly infectious diseases lurked everywhere and inspired all sorts of human efforts to prevent or cure them. Susan Wise Bauer’s new book <em>The Great Shadow: A History of How Sickness Shapes What We Do, Think, Believe, and Buy</em> catalogues these efforts and attempts to put them in context as we ride the wave of the Third Epidemiological Transition—our modern era of antibiotic resistance and mutated viruses.</p>
            <p>Bauer, the founder of Well-Trained Mind Press and author of the <em>Story of the World</em> books used by millions of homeschoolers, is well-equipped to tell the story of sickness. <em>The Great Shadow</em> joins recent releases like John Green’s <em>Everything is Tuberculosis</em> and Timothy Winegard’s <em>The Mosquito</em> as a history of human culture examined through the lens of infectious disease, taking readers through thousands of years of humans trying to figure out how to prevent and treat infections. The threat of infection and death is life-altering, as Bauer writes:</p>
            <blockquote><p>Sickness is not “just” sickness. Sickness is the most intimate expression of our vexed relationship with reality, the place where smooth functioning suddenly fractures and spins apart without warning. Sickness is the great mirror that reflects back our most urgent question: <em>Why does calamity descend without warning? How can we explain it? How do we avoid it? How do we fight back?</em></p></blockquote>
            <p>As a historical catalogue of maladies and our reactions to them, Bauer has assembled a solid (if somewhat incomplete) narrative. However, whenever the book tries to draw contemporary parallels, it generally falls flat, detracting from an otherwise fascinating story. The story of sickness is much bigger than infectious disease, and the wisdom of human response to infections is more complicated than Bauer seems willing to give people credit for.</p>
            <h3 class="section-heading">How Sickness Shapes Us</h3>
            <p><em>The Great Shadow</em> begins with the First Epidemiological Transition, when humans started living together in static settlements and made themselves vulnerable to all kinds of viruses, bacteria, fungi, and parasites that hunter-gatherers eluded by virtue of their migratory lifestyle. Irrigation required standing water, domesticating animals allowed new infections to jump from species to species, and managing our bodily waste became a perennial issue. Ancient myths from places as diverse as Mesopotamia, China, and West Africa told just-so stories linking settled, agricultural civilization with the rise of disease. The tone is overly dramatic; the best way to describe it is as if Bauer has written the script for a documentary. For the most part, this approach works but at times it is tiring.</p>
            <p>Disease raised spiritual and moral questions, as people assumed that there must be a cause for every effect. From the Biblical book of Job to the Epic of Gilgamesh, humans began to tie guilt and blame to the existence of illnesses and found ways as diverse as priestly dreams to studying animal entrails to find the why. <em>The Great Shadow</em> frequently returns to the idea of human responsibility, seeing it as a thread that ties together many different human responses to sickness. While acknowledging that there are some sicknesses that are indeed the fault of the sick person, repeatedly the book shows examples where blame was unfairly placed throughout history.</p>
            <p>This idea only intensifies as the book turns to Hippocratic medicine, which explained sickness as an imbalance of humors within the body and saw miasma (“bad airs”) as a critical piece of the disease puzzle. The bottom line for Hippocratic medicine (which came to dominate the culture of medicine for thousands of years) is, in Bauer’s words: “More than ever before, <em>illness is your fault.”</em> (Emphasis in the original.) <em>The Great Shadow</em> argues that this idea is still with us: “Striving for wholeness and balance, for moderation and temperance: these all seem like worthy goals to us. But we have carried over, from Hippocratic times, a complicated and lingering sense of failure when our lives do not flow with ease.”</p>
            <p>Medieval pogroms took the idea of blame to another level, blaming Jews for various illnesses and then carrying out violence against them. This idea recurred with even deadlier consequences in the Nazi regime, which Bauer places in a “straight line” leading to conspiracy theories about COVID-19 as a Jewish bioweapon. Similarly, the story of “Typhoid Mary” still animates contemporary fears about foreigners carrying illnesses.</p>
            <p>As humans got closer and closer to scientific facts about microorganisms causing disease, they often came into conflict with the reigning theories about balancing humors and clearing miasma. Ignaz Semmelweiss was hounded into a mental asylum by his contemporaries after calling for his fellow doctors to sterilize their hands between autopsies and delivering babies, while intense debates broke out about the wisdom of vaccination.</p>
            <p>What seems like common sense to us was the result of hard-fought battles—battles that Bauer argues we are still fighting today. She makes the case that because miasma theory was still predominant well into the Second Epidemiological Transition, a number of products we still use today (such as plug-in air fresheners and Listerine) are still in use because “our noses have inherited the nineteenth-century fear of miasma.”</p>
            <p><em>The Great Shadow</em> concludes by taking readers through the brief “exhilaration” of antibiotics and vaccines that was almost immediately deflated by the emergence of antibiotic-resistant bacteria, novel viruses like HIV and Ebola, and cultural works like <em>Night of the Living Dead</em> or <em>The Last of Us</em> that use zombies as a metaphor for our fears about contagion. In the book’s conclusion, Bauer argues that the response to COVID-19 (particularly in the U.S.) “replayed, unthinking, almost every beat of the last few centuries of our relationship with infectious disease.” And while “[s]ome of these reactions might well have been sound and well-considered,” she argues that “[m]ost were not.” Six-foot distancing represented our fear of miasma, fearing immigrants repeated the ugly patterns of blame from the past, and the overuse of sanitizing agents made us too much like the Victorians of the past.</p>
            <p><em>The Great Shadow</em> ends with a somewhat hopeful note that understanding this history will help answer the question whether, during the next big pandemic, we will or won’t “coat ourselves in chemicals, rail against bodies that don’t look like ours, and accuse scientists of corruption?” This is Bauer’s apparent purpose: in order to prevent reactions that are not “sound and well-considered,” we must appreciate how some of these irrational responses are deeply hard-wired into human culture.</p>
            <h3 class="section-heading">Blame and Responsibility</h3>
            <p>Bauer’s primary assumption is true: sickness really does force us to confront a number of existential realities and the brokenness within our world. However, the apparent secondary thesis, that guilt and blame (as well as other misunderstandings) have inappropriately lingered in our social imaginary is not as straightforward as she makes it out to be.</p>
            <p><em>The Great Shadow</em> is not really about sickness in general; it’s mostly about <em>infectious</em> diseases caused by foreign microorganisms. Only the chapters about painkillers and hysteria dwell at any length on noninfectious diseases. A history of all sickness would be much longer and far more complicated, for there are many diseases that are <em>not</em> caused by infections and some that hew more closely to Hippocratic ideals and miasma theory. Both of these ideas—indeed, nearly all of the reactions and behaviors that Bauer describes— contain a kernel of truth. Bauer readily concedes this point but does not ever really let it refine her thesis.</p>
            <p>Air pollution, for example, is estimated to be responsible for millions of deaths worldwide every year. To eliminate these deaths would simply take cleaning up the air and improving household ventilation, since cleaning circulated air is valuable in preventing both infectious and non-infectious diseases. Some studies suggest that retrofitting school buses can even improve students’ academic performance! Whether “blame” is the correct frame for air pollution is a debate for a different space, but certainly we must as a society figure out where the pollution is coming from. Regulating and occasionally punishing its producers would also help.</p>
            <p>There are a few other notable gaps in the story. Sexually transmitted infections have been identified as such for over 500 years, and the politics of blame around HIV get only a passing mention in the book. The Tuskegee Syphilis Study, in which the government was very much to blame for allowing a disease to destroy vulnerable people, is not mentioned at all. The history of eugenics might have fit in as well, given the significant overlap between the scientific consensus about the spread of germs and health authorities’ approach to genetic causes of disease a century ago.</p>
            <p>I do not know when exactly the book went to press, but even the story <em>The Great Shadow</em> tells about COVID-19 is not as simple as it sounds. The US government was funding gain-of-function research (a fascinating response to the specter of infectious diseases worthy of discussion in and of itself) in Wuhan, and many of the government’s earliest critics were right to smell a conspiracy when Dr. Anthony Fauci claimed otherwise. Given the possibility that the Chinese Communist Party’s cover-up of the disease may have cost millions of lives, pointing some fingers and assigning responsibility is eminently reasonable.</p>
            <p>When it comes to responsibility, Bauer mixes straightforward reporting about past misunderstandings of hysteria and “the passions” with criticism of diagnoses that shifts any responsibility for getting better onto the sick person themselves. Yet psychosomatic illnesses (that is, illnesses that are thought to be due to a patient’s internal or psychological stress manifesting as physical symptoms) are estimated to make up 20% of visits to primary care doctors. The most rigorous scientific studies about chronic pain suggest that the patients who do better are the ones who take the most responsibility for their own wellness, as Haider Warrach’s book <em>The Song of Our Scars</em> discusses carefully.</p>
            <p>While miasma theory only reflects a half-truth about illness (and, as <em>The Great Shadow</em> details, a lot of the work to cleanly dispose of sewage was the right thing for the wrong reason), Hippocratic medicine endures because it reflects truths about our bodies and souls: Patients want to feel like their lives are balanced, nutrition and exercise are key factors in preventing chronic diseases, and our environment (whether it’s clean air or a walkable neighborhood) does have a role to play in our health. The lesson to learn from Hippocrates’ staying power beyond the Second Epidemiological Transition is one that Bauer doesn’t seem to find of interest: while many aspects of our health are indeed beyond our control, there’s still a lot that we can do to find balance and wholeness.</p>
            <h3 class="section-heading">In the Shadow of the Great Physician</h3>
            <p>Most importantly, though, none of the reactions that <em>The Great Shadow</em> discusses seem like positive developments for human culture. As Lydia Dugdale discusses in her must-read book <em>The Lost Art of Dying</em>, the possibility of death and disease made our ancestors far more careful about not only dying well but also living in such a way that they were ready whenever death might come. It is certainly possible to allow fear of sickness and blame (of others or of ourselves) for infections to lead to atrocities—but there is a far richer history of love and care to be found in traditions like the <em>ars moriendi</em> that forms the focus of Dugdale’s book.</p>
            <p>One omission from <em>The Great Shadow</em> that would illuminate this sort of love and care is the story that Rodney Stark details in his book <em>The Rise of Christianity</em>. Christians in the third century, facing a deadly plague, did not run or blame foreigners. Rather, they sacrificed themselves to care for their pagan neighbors. So began a tradition of medical care despite the risk of infection that endures to the selfless heroism of health professionals (Christian and otherwise) who rush to “hot zones” of Ebola epidemics nowadays. The capricious nature of the gods in ancient times gave way to Francis Bacon’s insistence that God gave us technology to relieve pain and suffering—an enormous part of what we think and believe now that we count history by the life and death of the Great Physician.</p>
            <p><em>The Great Shadow</em> is, of course, coming out at a time when America’s own Secretary of Health and Human Services may not actually believe in germ theory and many people are in need of an anti-miasma correction. Some have made the ideals of Hippocratic medicine into a story of karma that unnecessarily heaps blame and shame on suffering people. However, as we have seen throughout the COVID-19 pandemic and elsewhere, telling only parts of the story will continue to engender the sort of responses that are harmful to others. The history told in <em>The Great Shadow</em> is vast but incomplete, telling the truth about the ugliest aspects of human encounters with infectious disease while leaving out the ways in which our awareness of fragility became a light that chased the shadows away.</p>
            <p class="contributor-bio">Matthew Loftus grew up in a family of 15 children and completed his medical training in Baltimore, Maryland. Since 2015, he and his family have lived in East Africa, where he currently teaches and practices family medicine at a mission hospital. His work has appeared in outlets such as <em>The New York Times</em>, <em>The Atlantic</em>, <em>The New Atlantis</em>, and <em>Mere Orthodoxy</em>. His first book, <em>Resisting Therapy Culture</em>, is forthcoming from InterVarsity Press. You can learn more about his work and writing at www.matthewandmaggie.org</p>
          </section>

          <!-- ====== ARTICLE 12 ====== -->
          <section id="stewarding-land-and-language" class="content-section">
            <h2 class="section-title">Stewarding Land and Language</h2>
            <p class="essay-author">By Jake Meador</p>

            <p>There was a time when Wendell Berry was a passionate and even amusing critic of Christianity. This is seen perhaps most explicitly in one of his earliest published essays, "The Long-Legged House."</p>
            <p>In the essay, Berry describes the experience of repairing an old cabin built by a relative of his. That cabin would become the home that he and his soon-to-be wife would move into it after their wedding. (For long-time readers of Berry’s work, the cabin by the river that Jayber Crow moves to in his dying days is the fictional imagining of this actual cabin where Mr. and Mrs. Berry began their married life together.) Much of the essay is taken up with the meaningfulness of the work of restoring such a place, the ways in which that work tied Berry to his place and his people, and how the preparing of that place for his wife would also set something of a course for their life together.</p>
            <p>Then Berry turns to the church, an institution about which he had virtually nothing to say up to that point. But as the wedding grew closer, it became impossible to avoid the church—which is what Berry had been doing quite happily and successfully. When you read his account of things, it is hard to blame the man for wanting to stay well clear of the church:</p>
            <blockquote><p>Our wedding reminds me a little of the Kentucky Derby. The main event, which lasted only a couple of minutes, required days of frantic prologue. During this commotion one must discontinue one’s own life and attempt to emulate everybody. Nobody can be still until convinced that this marriage will be like everybody else’s. The men insinuate. The women gloat. The church is resurrected and permitted to interfere. As at funerals, the principals cannot be decently let alone, but must be overhauled, upgraded, and messed with until they are not recognizable.</p></blockquote>
            <blockquote><p>During the week up to and including the wedding I am sure I was at the center of more absurdity than I hope ever to be at the center of again. One of my unforgettable moments, as they are called, was a quaint session with the minister who having met me for the first time that moment, and being scarcely better acquainted with Tanya, undertook to instruct us in the marital intimacies. A display of preacherish cant and presumption unusual even for a preacher, and all carried out with a slogging joyless dutifulness. As we were leaving he handed us a book on marriage.</p></blockquote>
            <blockquote><p>In a country less abject before “expert advice,” the effrontery of it would be incredible. Well, be damned to him and his book, too. We thrive in spite of him, and in defiance of some of his rules. We are, I like to think, his Waterloo—though I know that, like most of his kind, he had come to his Waterloo before that, and survived by his inability to recognize it.</p></blockquote>
            <p>The text is obviously Berry, though written when he was only in his early 30s: You can hear the language of his male characters in it—of Jack Beechum and Carter Keith and Nathan Coulter. The phrase "survived by his inability to recognize it" is also vintage Berry and a classic example of his ability to find simple, under-stated ways of making a devastating critique. The disdain for "experts" is there as well, as is the revulsion at ostentatious displays. It is not at all surprising to think that the man who wrote these words would, within only a few years, write <em>The Memory of Old Jack</em>. Indeed, we can find a similarly disdainful attitude toward the church in that book, the first of Berry's truly great novels, alongside <em>Jayber Crow</em> and <em>Hannah Coulter</em>.</p>
            <p>In this particular case, the church manifests as villain in the final pages of the novel, as the departed old farmer Jack Beechum is fraudulently reconstructed as a dutiful son of the church by his affluent and spoiled daughter, Clara, who hasn't known her father well for at least 30 years, if she ever did at all. In a particularly pointed passage, Beechum's true heir and closest relative, Mat Feltner, instructs the preacher to simply read a few Psalms at a small graveside service. When the preacher ignores Mat's request and launches into a showy and dishonest prayer after reading the Psalms, Berry describes Mat's reaction to this fictionalized version of "preacherish cant":</p>
            <blockquote><p>And then Mat exercises for the first time his prerogative as the oldest man. He turns his back and gazes upon his fields.</p></blockquote>
            <p>The gesture is subtle, perhaps even polite in its own way, yet its meaning is unmistakable: For Berry, at this time at least, the church is merely one of the institutions of the world, seeking to make better theoretical persons in better theoretical places, without any recognition of the world as it is, of people as they are, and gratitude for all the good things we have. The church, like virtually all other institutions, is perpetually trying to improve its members, exhorting them to make something of themselves. None of them see and love the world as it is, see and love people as they are. They only love imagined, far off places and imagined, far off people who exist only in the false prayers of mendacious preachers.</p>
            <p>There is another passage in <em>Old Jack</em> that names the failure Berry recognizes in the church, but not in the church alone; it is a failure of imagination, a failure of seeing. In the passage I am about to quote, Mat Feltner is reflecting on the bizarre life of a couple renting a home from him on his property. This couple, the Berlews, are travelers, thrill chasers. They avoid hard work as much as they can, seeking only to work enough to make enough to facilitate their thrill chasing.</p>
            <p>For the privilege of such a life they end up working far harder to maintain their old car that facilitates that life than they ever would dream of working in the day for pay. Then Berry remarks, comparing the Berlews with Beechum's extremely rich and successful daughter and son-in-law the Pettits,</p>
            <blockquote><p>It comes to Mat suddenly—so that he first grimaces and then grunts—that the Berlews and Pettits represent the two halves of the same distraction. What the Pettits are is what the Berlews dream of being. Is not the Berlews' old car the hopeless dream of a Cadillac? Is not their tireless going a persistently frustrated pilgrimage in search of Easy Street? That the Pettits <em>live</em> on Easy Street makes no essential difference between them and the Berlews. If the Pettits are what the Berlews would like to be, they are also what the Pettits would like to be; they have imagined nothing higher than they are.</p></blockquote>
            <p>We could put it this way: There is a way we people, perhaps especially we <em>modern</em> people, have of imagining nothing higher than we are. And once we have started from that point, there is some sense in which Christianity can't help us. It can only be abused and twisted and perverted by us. This is what Berry recognized: The church we find in "The Long-Legged House" and <em>The Memory of Old Jack</em> is a church that simply takes for granted the belief that this world has nothing worthy to offer us. These churches believe without thought or question that the only good thing in this life is to seek to save our own souls out of the world—and if that is the only thing of ultimate importance, than everything else doesn’t much matter, which perhaps explains why such radical spiritualism can often be found closely associated with rampant materialism and spiritual complacency. The ease with which a kind of fundamentalist Baptist or Methodist Christianity of the old south can be melded with a life that imagines nothing higher than we are is actually quite astonishing: Christianity simply becomes one means of serving ourselves (by giving us a sense of superiority), of exalting ourselves (by highlighting our own righteousness in the eyes of others), of protecting ourselves (by acquiring the requisite fire insurance to protect our immortal souls).</p>
            <p>You might say that this is a Christianity that lacks both a doctrine of creation and a doctrine of resurrection; it is bloodless and disembodied and, for precisely those reasons, has very little to offer the world and very little of use to protect its members from violating themselves, their neighbors, and the world.</p>
            <p>This is a point worth lingering over. There is a passage in the Gospels where Jesus warns the religious leaders that all their religious teaching serves only to make their followers twice the children of Hell that they themselves are. The Christianity we encounter in Berry's early work is one example of how that can happen. Only leave the great idol of the heart—the supremacy of the self—unchallenged and Christianity, of a kind at least, becomes a tool for spreading and facilitating unimaginable wickedness.</p>
            <p>To put it another way, when Berry looked at the goodness of the world he walked about in every day and set that against what he found in the church, he found the church's account of goodness entirely inadequate. Indeed, he found the church routinely taking the side of evil while the world itself shined unnoticed. What Treebeard says to Merry and Pippin in <em>The Lord of the Rings</em> could just as well be said by the land and, perhaps, by Berry himself in Berry's fiction: "Side? I am on nobody's side, for nobody is altogether on my side."</p>
            <p>It's important to understand this because this is a different sort of objection to Christianity from those familiar to most Christians. It is not claiming that Christianity’s moral norms are stuffy or antiquated. It is not an objection founded on some sort of moral relativism, or even on some sort of strong moral critique <em>against</em> Christianity.</p>
            <p>The failure, as someone like Jack Beechum would see it, is that he lives "hard up against mystery" every day (to use a phrase from <em>Jayber Crow)</em> and when he sets what he sees in the world, both its beauties and its many evils, he simply finds the Christian account to be small and stuffy, and, as a result, deeply unconvincing. Christianity is too small for the men of Berry's novels—the gratitudes and mercies they receive every day by virtue of their life as human beings (and, particularly, as farmers) seem far more real to them than anything they ever heard in a church.</p>
            <p>It's not that there is some negative criticism of Christianity that Berry accepts and this prevents him from believing; that framing presupposes belief as a kind of default mode which will only be deviated from if presented with some sort of sufficient obstacle. But that isn't how the early Berry regards Christianity. It's not that Christianity is necessarily immoral or corrupt or unjust. It's that when Berry encounters the given earth with its pleasures and mercies and difficulties and sets it against Christianity he finds the world far more beautiful.</p>
            <p>That, I think, is why Christianity, though seldom treated kindly in Berry's early work is also not something he spends an immense amount of time addressing. Berry is not a misotheist, a god-hater, in the way of a Nietzsche or a Christopher Hitchens. Religion is almost wholly absent from <em>The Memory of Old Jack</em> and from most of Berry's short stories, for example, and most of his early essays. To the degree he considers religion at all, it is simply as one more example of an institution more concerned with its own life than the life of the world and filled with people more concerned with their own lives than the lives of others or of their place.</p>
            <p>And yet that is not where the story ends. By the time we come to <em>Jayber Crow</em>, published in the early 2000s, Berry has retained many of his criticisms of certain Christian communities and even of certain Christian ideas. But the tone is measured and careful, as if he has learned to do what Frederick Douglass learned a hundred years prior: to distinguish between a sort of Americanized folk Christianity aimed at self-exaltation at the expense of all else and the "pure peaceable" Christianity of Christ. The point is not that the theological questions have evaporated for Berry, or at least for Jayber Crow. They don’t. They linger. But the experience of living in the world and finding love in it caused Jayber to reconsider his relationship to the faith—and in the Gospels, at least, he finds an account of love that anchors and deepens his own experience of it.</p>
            <p>And so the rage we find in Berry’s older work has dissipated by the time we arrive at <em>Jayber Crow</em>. What remains is an ordinary man vexed by certain theological questions still, yet convinced that God's mercy can work to overcome any evil and that Christ's law of love is the pinnacle of moral wisdom. How did this change happen?</p>
            <p>A neglected volume of essays published in the early 1980s provides an answer—and for my money this particular volume is Berry's greatest accomplishment as an essayist. The collection is called <em>Standing by Words</em> and in it Berry considers poetry, poetic forms, the limits inherent in language, and how all these things relate to the ways we imagine the world. Why is it the volume that is so pivotal in his reconsideration of Christianity?</p>
            <p>I think the answer is here: What Berry knew all along is that he found the life of the world more entrancing and satisfying than the life of the church as he knew it. What becomes apparent in <em>Standing by Words</em> is that he needed an account of <em>why</em> the life of the world is marked by the beauty, abundance, and superfluity that so delighted him. The answer he comes to, through various means, is that the life of the earth is marked by forms that it did not choose for itself, so to speak, but also cannot be overwritten, erased, or remade without causing great and unnecessary harm. The good life and our capacity for good work are both largely bound up with how we are able to relate to the order, place, and people that have been given to us. When we regard these things with indifference, rage, or contempt it turns out that we can violate the fields that Mat Feltner turned toward in <em>Old Jack</em> just as easily as we can violate churches or universities.</p>
            <p>What Berry is reckoning with in this collection of essays is the way in which his own callings as both a writer and a farmer have been compromised and set at odds with the givenness of things, coming under the sway of specialization and control and so being drawn out of the world, one might say. In the first essay of the volume, in which Berry laments the "specialization" of poetry he writes, "the poet is isolated and specialized and.. the old union of beauty, goodness, and truth is broken." Later he continues, writing about poets in much the same way he had previously written about bankers and professional religious people:</p>
            <p>One of the oldest doctrines of specialist-poets is that of the primacy of language and the primacy of poetry. They have virtually made a religion of their art, a religion based not on what they have in common with other people, but on what they <em>do</em> that sets them apart. For poets who believe this way, a poem is not a point of clarification or connection between themselves and the world on the one hand and between themselves and their readers on the other, nor is it an adventure into any reality or mystery outside themselves. It is a seeking of self in words, the making of a word-world in which the word-self may be at home. The poets go to their poems as other people go to the world or to God—for a sense of their own reality. ...</p>
            <p>It may be argued that this emphasis was in some sense forced upon poets by their peculiar isolation in a society dominated by specialists of more practical kinds. But this way of looking at language and at poetry also completes and enforces the isolation of poets, not just within a sort of literary ghetto or subghetto, but within themselves. Mark Strand is evidently not the only poet who would say that, 'the self in a sense is all we have left.'</p>
            <p>There are strong echoes here of what Berry wrote of the Berlews and Pettits in <em>The Memory of Old Jack</em>, but whereas in <em>Jack</em> Berry seems to isolate that narrowed spirit, in "The Specialization of Poetry" he is suggesting that it has become ubiquitous. In <em>Old Jack</em> the villains are bankers and drifters and the heroes are farmers. By the time we come to <em>Standing by Words</em> Berry is reckoning constantly with the ways in which farmers and poets–his two vocations–can be just as complicit in the unmaking of places as the bankers and drifters. The problem, in short, can’t be concentrated or distilled down to “corrupt institutions indifferent to the health of the land smothering men like Jack Beechum.” The problem Berry wants to address strikes at the heart of what it means to steward both land and language, the two great preoccupations of Berry’s life.</p>
            <p>What this means is that he no longer can focus his ire primarily on bankers, like the Pettits, or lazy dreamers, like the Berlews. Rather, he has had to turn this critique loose on everyone, including the people closest to him and, indeed, on himself. But then that also suggests that the contest we see at the end of <em>Old Jack</em> in which Mat Feltner turns away from the rude and pontificating preacher to turn toward the fields is not <em>quite</em> so clear as that image might suggest. The fields themselves can become a subject of "specialization" and can themselves be cut off. If you are seeking an escape from preacherish cant, it is no sure thing that you will find it in the fields. You might find the same desecration there that one can find in so many other places.</p>
            <p>It turns out that one cannot simply pit the life of the world against the life of institutions. Rather, even the world itself can, in the twistedness and lostness of human beings, be set against the life of the world. It is a challenge to see the life of the world truly. And if it is a challenge, that suggests that aids are needed in order to do so. This is where we begin to see a reconsideration of Christianity in Berry's work.</p>
            <p>In the essay, "Standing by Words," Berry considers the problem of language and naming in ways quite similar to what Lewis sought to address in <em>The Abolition of Man</em>. And he comes to quite similar answers, though he gets there via a different route. The problem, for Berry, concerns the ways in which language itself becomes divorced from any kind of external control or referent aside from power and will.</p>
            <p>He rightly sees that the health of the world and of people and their many communities is dependent on the notion that words stand for something real; language is not simply an expression of tastes or a tool for acquiring power. Language is how we try to name what is real and true and worth living for. The trouble is that when language becomes materialized or politicized, it loses its referent. He illustrates the point with two diagrams, one of which he suggests is a model of healthy communal and natural life while the other is the industrial semblance:</p>
            <p>In the first diagram, everything that is part of our life in the world is encompassed in some sense. The second, by contrast, has removed anything that can’t be quantified or financialized. This is how Berry names the change that happens when you move from the economy depicted in the first diagram to the economy of the second diagram:</p>
            <p>So long as the smaller systems are enclosed within the larger, and so long as all are connected by complex patterns of interdependency, as we know they are, then whatever affects one system will affect the others. It seems that this system of systems is safe so long as each system is controlled by the next larger one. If at any point the hierarchy is reversed, and the smaller begins to control the larger, then the destruction fo the entire system of systems begins. This system of systems is perhaps an updated, ecological version of the Great Chain of Being. That is, it may bring us back to a hierarchical structure not too different from the one that underlies <em>Paradise Lost</em>&mdash;a theory of the form of Creation which is at the same time a moral form, and which is violated by the ‘disobedience’ or <em>hubris</em> of attempting to rise and take power above one’s proper place.</p>
            <p>What does Berry propose to fix this? As is implied above, he suggests that what we need is a category above 'nature' that controls and names it, and that category, he suggests, is "religion.” The issue he sees is that his “system of systems” is fragile and easily destroyed because its only measure is truth and truth is easily ignored or suppressed. A moral witness is needed from <em>outside</em> Berry’s system, something to help the members of a community recognize why it is good for them to tune their lives to the lives of neighbors and the life of the world. That something, he thinks, is religion: “There is no reliable standard for behavior anywhere within the system of systems except truth. Lesser standards produce destruction–as, for example, the standards of public relations make gibberish of language. The trouble, obviously, is that we do not know much of the truth…. And so yet another term has be introduced. The system of systems has to be controlled from above and outside. There has to be a religious interest of some kind above the ecogenetic. It will be sufficient to my purpose to say simply that the system of systems is enclosed within mystery, in which some truth can be known but never all truth.”</p>
            <p>In other words, the members of the world can never know the world exhaustively. And so a certain deference to mystery and to our own creaturely ignorance is often necessary to prevent us from committing monstrous acts. You might say that this is Berry’s articulation of the famous Chesterton’s Fence aphorism in which the British writer G. K. Chesterton said that a fence should never be knocked down by someone who doesn’t know why it is there. The only time it is safe to knock a fence down is when you <em>do</em> know its purpose and can explain why it is no longer needed. The recognition of ignorance, in other words, should provoke us toward gentleness, caution, and care–and this provocation, Berry thinks, is what is needed to preserve healthy places, which include the land and the people living from the land.</p>
            <p>Interestingly, Berry actually pressed the religious language even further. Earlier in the essay he had teased at this solution when he suggested that the Christian doctrine of the Incarnation would offer a way of grounding our language, such that we can claim that our language is not simply taste or power-grabbing, but can actually be an attempt to speak of reality. Specifically he argues that in the Incarnation true Word and true Flesh are found together, suggesting that not only should we try to speak of reality truly, but that we actually <em>can</em>.</p>
            <p>In other words, by the time we arrive at <em>Standing by Words</em> Berry has become aware that the deeper evil he wants to oppose is a kind of human wilfulness that, flowing out of the idea that there is nothing higher than ourselves, reduces everything it finds to consumable goods. The battle is not, necessarily, between “the peace of wild things” and the mechanical. The evils of the machine can come to reign in the wild as easily as they can in churches, farms, and universities.</p>
            <p>What is needed, rather, is some account that offers us a way to name the things that are higher than us, a way to love those things, and a way to tune our lives to them—and that work can happen as much in the fields as it does in the church. The way Berry learns to make this critique is through a return to Christianity, through considering the ways in which theological themes in Christian scripture help us to see and love what is higher than us, to know that Higher Good so well that we can tune ourselves to it wherever we go, whether that be the wild places, a classroom or, even, a church.</p>
            <p class="contributor-bio">Jake Meador is the editor-in-chief of Mere Orthodoxy. His writing has appeared in <em>The Atlantic</em>, <em>Commonweal</em>, <em>First Things</em>, <em>Books &amp; Culture</em>, <em>National Review</em>, <em>Comment</em>, and <em>Christianity Today</em>. He is a contributing editor with <em>Plough</em> and a contributing writer at <em>The Dispatch</em>. He lives in his hometown of Lincoln, NE with his wife and four children.</p>
          </section>

          <!-- ====== ARTICLE 13 ====== -->
          <section id="cultivating-a-well-ordered" class="content-section">
            <h2 class="section-title">Cultivating a Well-Ordered Anthropology in a Dehumanizing World</h2>
            <p class="essay-author">By Nadya Williams</p>

            <p class="essay-heading">Freya India. <em>Girls®: Generation Z and the Commodification of Everything</em>. Henry Holt and Co., 2026. $29.99. 384 pp.</p>
            <p class="essay-heading">Jen Wilkin. <em>Dust to Dust: Aging Wisely in an Anti-Aging World</em>. Crossway, 2026. $16.99. 208 pp.</p>
            <p>The blessed gods alone live forever, ever youthful and unchanging. Everyone in the ancient world knew it. And yet, since time immemorial, people have been on an unrelenting quest to eternal life. The Mesopotamian epic hero and king Gilgamesh traveled beyond the bounds of the known world, conversed with the barmaid at the edge of the universe, and communed with the man who managed to survive the flood and gained the gift of eternal life. But Gilgamesh himself failed in his quest—then died. Greek epic heroes fought for eternal fame, settling for it as the next best thing, since bodily immortality wasn’t on offer. And Roman emperors declared themselves living gods in wishful name-it-claim-it thinking that, in due time, always proved false. Each one of these self-made gods died.</p>
            <p>But, myths declared, select mortals did find eternal life. The results, however, were not typical—nor they were quite so good at the end. When the Greek goddess of dawn, Eos, fell in love with a mortal prince, Tithonus, she begged Zeus for the gift of eternal life for her beloved. Zeus granted her request. Except, it turns out, it was no blessing. The foolish Eos forgot to ask for eternal youth for Tithonus alongside eternal life. And so, eternal life didn’t help him as he aged along the same timeline as normal mortals do, shriveling, shrinking, declining physically and mentally, until he became just a cricket—nothing but a faint chirping sound devoid of recognizable humanity. Eos quickly fell out of love.</p>
            <p>We may laugh at tales like this one, but secular modernity puts its own twist on them. Consider the tech entrepreneur Bryan Johnson, whose Instagram account has 2.4 million followers and boldly declares: “We may be the first generation who won’t die.” His own personal motto is, in fact, “Don’t Die,” and he has been spending $2 million per year on treatments and procedures to make this goal possible. Johnson will turn 50 next year, but he works hard daily to maintain the appearance and health of an 18-year-old. How? It is a delicate science: “When Johnson first wakes up before 5am, he weighs himself on a high-tech scale that looks not just at his weight, but his body fat and hydration level. Then he measures his body temperature—through the ear, thankfully… He takes 54 pills alongside a drink he calls the ‘green giant' containing creatine, cocoa flavanols and collagen peptides, and wears a cap on his head that pumps red light into his scalp to stimulate hair growth—because no man wants to go bald in his old age. He then exercises, undergoing a 25-step process that includes 10 minutes of backward sleds, Nordics, reverse Nordics and ‘IsoTib ankle rotations’ to try and increase his diaphragm strength. Then he eats breakfast, ‘which is a few pounds of vegetables like broccoli, cauliflower, garlic and ginger,’ he explains, all of which are chosen for their health-boosting benefits.”</p>
            <p>While this list sounds intense, it is less bizarre than some of the other treatments Johnson has used experimentally. At one point he tried regular blood and plasma transfusions from his teenage son before abandoning the practice, because he couldn’t see measurable benefits—unlike the obviously measurable benefits of eating pounds of broccoli with garlic and ginger for breakfast. The Romans joked that mushrooms were the food of the gods (after poisoned mushrooms led to the death—and prompt deification—of the emperor Claudius). Reading about Johnson’s routine, it is clear that broccoli is the food of this modern self-made god.</p>
            <p>Something else is clear from these stories, ancient and modern: Anthropology (our thoughts and beliefs about people) and theology (our thoughts and beliefs about the divine) are intertwined. A pagan theology results in a pagan anthropology—the desire to make oneself into a god by any means possible. A theology that recognizes God’s power over all his creations, including the human beings God had created in his own image (Gen. 1:27), results in an anthropology that emphasizes human finitude and utter dependence on God in all seasons of life. We have not created ourselves, and this is good news.</p>
            <p>Two new books show the implications of poor theology and anthropology for the very young (Gen-Z) and those a bit older respectively. Writer Freya India analyzes the horrific physical, mental, emotional, and spiritual toll that social media has taken on Gen-Z women, effectively stripping them of their personhood and making them into products to optimize with the aid of yet more products. Meanwhile seasoned Bible teacher Jen Wilkin considers our society’s firmly baked-in anti-aging attitudes and presents a response: phases of life are part of God’s plan for our lives; they are a blessing, not a curse.</p>
            <p>There is nothing new under the sun. Still, technological advances present ever more creative ways of dehumanizing God’s image-bearers—not for our good, and definitely not for God’s glory.</p>
            <p>***</p>
            <p>It is 8:00pm on a school night in 1999, and I’m on the family computer in that little nook under the stairs in our old rental, doing homework while also logged into AOL Instant Messenger. It is the only way I have of communicating with friends outside of school, aside from just calling a friend’s landline, which is rather awkward and inconvenient—my phone use is strictly monitored and limited. Besides, perhaps it isn’t so much a phone conversation that you want at that moment. You just need to feel a little less alone in suffering through this biology lab report due in the morning.</p>
            <p>Of course, teens being teens, Instant Messenger was also how some really inappropriate gossip got passed around, but to not be in the know would have led to an embarrassment of its own in school the next day. Information is power, historians affirm, analyzing past empires and civilizations entire. Every teenager can confirm this simply from experience. But the problem with gossip chains is that sometimes you’re just an observer, but other times, you’re at the center of it, and consequences can be emotionally devastating, amplifying insecurities that are already outsized.</p>
            <p>There is no question about it. Adolescence is not easy, as bodies change fast (I recall habitually bumping into corners, as my legs and arms were in the morning somehow longer than they were the night before), hormones surge, emotions overflow, and wisdom is lacking to make sense of it all. Girls have always felt these changes particularly abruptly or at least in ways different from the typical adolescent boy. And adults around adolescents habitually have felt ill-equipped to help, even while noticing that a crisis is afoot. Indeed, in 1994, as I entered my own teenage years, psychologist Mary Pipher wrote <em>Reviving Ophelia: Saving the Selves of Adolescent Girls</em> to try and make sense of the problems she saw in her patients: “Many girls come into therapy with serious, even life-threatening problems, such as anorexia or the desire to kill or physically hurt themselves. Others have problems less dangerous but still more puzzling, such as school refusal, underachievement, moodiness or constant discord with their parents. Many are the victims of sexual violence.”</p>
            <p>Pipher asked outright in her introduction: “Why are so many girls in therapy in the 1990s?” Why, she wondered, were the adolescent girls she was treating so much more troubled than she recalled in her own girlhood in the 1960s? Clearly her questions resonated with many readers. The book spent three years on the New York Times bestseller list, selling 1.5 million copies along the way.</p>
            <p>The answer, Pipher concludes, is the overly sexualized “girl-poisoning culture” into which adolescent girls found themselves thrust, and from which they could not escape whole. Music, movies, television, and other media all encouraged that culture—in turn destroying girls’ emotional well-being and fostering toxic behaviors to match.</p>
            <p>Freya India was not yet born when Pipher wrote her book. Still, India’s new book, <em>GIRLS®: Generation Z and the Commodification of Everything</em>, does for Generation Z what Pipher attempted to do for Generation X and millennial girls: try to make sense of the anxieties so readily visible all around. What is happening? What about this crisis is old—and what is utterly new and unprecedented? Last but not least, what can we do about it?</p>
            <p>*** “All the world’s a stage,” Shakespeare said. We’re doing one better (or worse) as a society of late: all the world’s a lab. Specifically, when it comes to Gen-Z girls, the past two decades have been a disastrous lab experiment with human subjects. India describes the experiment and its horrific results based on her own experience as a Gen-Z British woman and from additional evidence of interviews and social media.</p>
            <p>“My basic argument is simple,” she says. “Modern digital technology amplified the age-old anxieties adolescent girls have always felt. Throughout history, girls and young women have always worried about their appearance, their emotions, their social status, their friendships and families, their romantic relationships, and their futures… The problem, though, is more sinister than that. Not only does the modern world magnify these age-old anxieties, it ruthlessly exploits and profits from them.”</p>
            <p>India describes, for instance, the rise of Snapchat and Instagram filters for girls’ faces. At first tools were available for girls to modify or filter their faces online with playful tools, creating illusions or temporary avatars of themselves. But pressures kept rising to look ever better, ever younger, ever more like the celebrities and influencers whose videos of doing makeup or getting ready for the day regularly get 15 million or more views. Next came the products for sale— injectables, fillers, makeup, and much, much more: “beauty influencers were getting rich and famous. Girls moved on from watching relatable young women reviewing affordable beauty products to influencers casually vlogging their lip filler appointments and cosmetic surgeries, sometimes as sponsored ads.”</p>
            <p>These pressures amplified mental health crises, such as eating disorders, to which teenage girls have already been much too prone (as Pipher argued in 1994). But the new mental health crisis, fostered by the many hours Gen-Z girls have been spending online, is only fueled further by online tools that promise to diagnose and medicate girls—all for a price. Online AI therapists are available day or night—for a hefty fee, of course. But more than that, the real problem that goes untreated is “social contagion”—behaviors that spread like the plague among peer groups.</p>
            <p>Many of the mental health issues Gen-Z girls are battling are the direct result of digitally facilitated social contagion, India argues. These include suicidal ideation and the epidemic of gender dysphoria. Worst of all, the reliance on online tools to solve these problems, caused by endless scrolling and swiping to begin with, only leaves girls more disconnected and detached from other people—lacking real friendships and relationships not only in the crucial teenage years but well into adulthood.</p>
            <p>In response, not only AI therapists but also AI friends and romantic partners, also available for sale and rising in popularity, promise to step into the breach, ensuring that one could live an entire life without hazarding any interactions with other enfleshed beings. Today’s crisis relies on the same insecurities and longings of girls that the 1990’s “girl-poisoning culture” fostered. Yet it is unprecedented in its technological progress— its ability to deliver the familiar toxins to girls’ brains 24/7, all via tiny rectangular devices in their hands and pockets.</p>
            <p>Yet the real crisis, when we peer beneath all the superficial layers, is always spiritual. The therapeutic nature of online AI tools only emphasizes this further: “while this therapeutic culture mimics prayer, confession, and even answers our biggest existential questions, one thing is missing: Who is God? In this religion, <em>we</em> are the gods.”</p>
            <p>We’re today’s Roman emperors, declaring ourselves gods. It’s going about as well for us as it did for folks like Nero. So what is the solution? India suggests the restoration of relationships, friendships, connections with other people in the flesh: “An important thing to do, I think, is to defy this cultural message to emotionally detach, this lie that it is liberating to be alone. Not because every young woman should follow the same traditional path, but because it is a tragedy— an outrage, actually—that we have been convinced that human connection is an inconvenience, that other people are obstacles.”</p>
            <p>The digital media world treats persons as machines, dehumanizing them and remaking them as products to be molded for profit. In response, we must remember (as the Sunday School cliché goes) “who we are and whose we are.” We are God’s image-bearers, created for his joy, pleasure, and glory. We need to live our lives in accordance with this truth—a reality that has repercussions not only for how we interact with the digital world but also for how we think about aging.</p>
            <p>***</p>
            <p>Another way to summarize the phenomenon India describes, this utter dehumanization of girls through online media tools that make persons into products, is that it is a story. The media world has been attempting (and all too often succeeding) to sell a particular life story to Gen-Z girls. It is a poisonous story of self-creation, self-making and remaking. People are story-tellers—just as God is. Not coincidentally, God gave us his Word, the Bible, in the shape of beautiful and powerful stories. But there is one important distinction between the stories people come up with of their own accord and imagination, and the story that the Bible presents: not all of the stories people tell are true. All the stories in the Bible are.</p>
            <p>Beyond the realm of social media, another area of our world that is filled with false stories is aging. In her new book <em>Dust to Dust: Aging Wisely in an Anti-Aging World</em>, Jen Wilkin confronts these false stories, puts them side-by-side with the biblical vision, and reminds us what it means to be a human being, precious in God’s sight and created in his image in all phases of our lives, from birth to dust. “The stories we believe about God, ourselves, and the world shape our feelings and govern the way we live,” she reminds, stating a truth equally applicable to India’s observations about the ravages of social media on girls.</p>
            <p>The problem is, our society glorifies youth and deeply fears aging and old age, so it tells false narratives about age—exemplified by such sayings as “age is just a number.” Many products exist to hide aging or slow it down—from milder and less visible plastic surgery procedures (the OB-GYN office I went to for the first part of my second pregnancy had walls plastered with Botox ads) to more extreme measures of the sort Bryan Johnson lives by. For Johnson, the goal is to perpetually maintain an epigenetic age of 18. Anything over it is a failure. There are ramifications, though, to such false narratives. They destroy our relationships with others, and they deny a basic fact of what it means to be human, limited and finite: We will all grow old and die. True, as Christ promised, “the last enemy to be destroyed is death” (1 Cor. 15:26), but until then, this is our lot and denying it through false narratives will not negate their outcome.</p>
            <p>So what does the Bible say about personhood at every stage of life? To help answer this question, Wilkin structures the book as an extended chiasmus (or chiasm). Bible readers may have heard of a basic chiastic structure—when the narrative proceeds by structuring the two halves of a poem or a story to mirror each other. Consider how you might narrate a walk down the block and then back again. This basic chiasm has an ABBA structure, with just two elements that are mirrored from the first half of the story to the second.</p>
            <p>But elaborate extended chiasms are common in ancient literature, with elements that mirror each other across an entire book scroll, such as the ABCDEDCBA structure of Book 3 of Homer’s <em>Iliad</em>. We talk about the importance of beginnings and endings in stories—and they certainly matter for ancient literature as well. But the key in lengthy chiastic narratives is to recognize the importance of the middle—for to it the first half of the narrative points, and from it the second half proceeds. In the case of Book 3 of the <em>Iliad</em>, that crucial middle features the duel between Menelaus and Paris, Helen’s respective husband and kidnapper-lover. The duel is important, as it could potentially just end the war. But the mirroring elements on either side of the middle are no less significant either and heighten the drama of the duel. The same is true for the drama of our lives.</p>
            <p>Our entire life, from birth to old age, Wilkin argues, can be structured as an extended chiasm. First, our birth and our death mirror each other, as both involve a labor followed by first breath and final breath respectively. Then our early years of life and the final years of life generally involve complete dependence on others for basic needs. Next, the years of adolescence with their increasing independence mirror “senior adulthood” with its decreasing independence. In the final pairing, our early adulthood and “late midlife” are both defined by caregiving—of children in the first case and of aging parents in the second. Finally, in that important middle spot of the chiasm, to which and from which the other mirroring pairs proceed, we find midlife—age forty to fifty-five.</p>
            <p>“If you have ever hiked to a mountain peak, you know the sensation of looking back toward the way you came with clearer perspective. And you know the sensation of looking at the return path and getting a sense of the distance and the effort it will require. Midlife is like that, an opportunity for taking stock… Midlife is simultaneously a liminal space and a point of no return.” It took turning forty for me to gain an interest in my family history and my past as an immigrant twice over. Only when I turned forty did I understand how incredibly difficult it must have been for my parents to emigrate from the country where they had spent their entire lives up to that point. And now, in my own midlife, I begin looking ahead at what lies on the downward path of this mountain peak.</p>
            <p>It is easy for us to treasure cute babies and to accept their utter dependence on us for all their needs, including in the middle of the night. It is more difficult to treasure old age—whether ours or that of those we love, as they grow as needy as babies over time. But all of these life stages are gifts from God. We are persons, beloved of God, at each point in life’s arc, Wilkin reminds her readers.</p>
            <p>These reminders are necessary. Because of our culture’s devaluing of old age and idolization of youth, we fail to see not only how God sees older men and women, but also how invaluable the Bible considers their role in the local church. Older men and women are called to teach those younger than them (Titus 2), but in our churches today, pastors barely out of college are not unheard of. Those serving in youth ministry, in particular, are sometimes barely older than the teens they are called to disciple. What kind of story are we telling in the process about different life stages in our churches?</p>
            <p>On and on life goes, if we are blessed to live a long one. The chiastic pairing of the respective stages on either end of midlife reminds us that all of them matter, all of them a gift to us and to those around us, if only we use our time here to serve. “Bodies age, people develop,” Wilkin repeats as a mantra throughout her book. God shaped the first man out of dust, and to dust we will all return. But also, God has numbered all our days, so along with the Psalmist, Wilkin encourages us to cling to this truth: “So teach us to number our days that we may get a heart of wisdom” (Psalm 90:12).</p>
            <p>We are not gods. We’re not even emperors. And we are certainly not God. But we are his workmanship, a true masterpiece (Eph. 2:10), children of dust though we are. God created us for a relationship with him, for however long our life span on this earth will last. More importantly, we know that we will spend eternity with him. And until then, perhaps broccoli in moderation won’t hurt either.</p>
            <p class="contributor-bio">Nadya Williams is the books editor at Mere Orthodoxy. She holds a PhD in classics from Princeton University and is the author of <em>Cultural Christians in the Early Church</em>; <em>Mothers, Children, and the Body Politic: Ancient Christianity and the Recovery of Human Dignity</em>; and <em>Christians Reading Classics</em>. She and her husband Dan joyfully live and homeschool in Ashland, Ohio.</p>
          </section>

          <!-- ====== ARTICLE 14 ====== -->
          <section id="bringing-up-nietzsche" class="content-section">
            <h2 class="section-title">Bringing Up Nietzsche</h2>
            <p class="essay-author">By Vika Pechersky</p>

            <p class="essay-heading">Michael McEwen. <em>The Devil Reads Nietzsche: A Public Theology for a Post-Christian Age</em>. B&amp;H Academic, 2025. $27.99. 224 pp.</p>
            <p>As a young parent, I was not prepared for how early my children exhibited skeptical tendencies and posed very difficult questions. My son was four years old when he told me, in all seriousness, that he was bigger than God. It happened during a very tense moment, when he repeatedly refused to obey me and finally demanded that I tell him why he should do what I asked. I was not prepared for a metaphysical discussion on the nature of obedience, but I knew that a simple “because I told you so” would not suffice. The situation demanded a good answer, as I faced an open revolt from a very serious four-year-old. I fumbled through something along the lines of how obeying our parents helps us learn to obey God, our heavenly Father. It is at this point that my son proclaimed that he is bigger than God, hence no obeying is required. How did I get a little Nietzsche on my hands?</p>
            <p>It was, of course, a very comical situation, but the seriousness with which my son was speaking scared me. Really, I thought, questioning faith can start this early! In the years since that day, more questions followed. Some were not openly skeptical. When he was about six years old, he asked me where he was before he was in my belly. At this point, I already knew that my kid could handle abstract talk. Recognizing that he was deeply troubled by the question of his existence, I told him that before he appeared in my belly, he was in the mind of God. That answer actually satisfied him, for it was better to think that he was somewhere, rather than nowhere at all.</p>
            <p>Still, another time, when I felt it was my duty as a Christian parent to encourage my son to pray more for a specific situation, he asked me, “What if, when I pray, I just talk to myself and nothing more?” How did I end up with a young Nietzsche and Feuerbach in one? I really had a hard time answering that one, as I could sense the depth of that doubt and the void that was opening up in front of my son. One misstep on my part, and he might fall into it. The best I could do on that occasion was to explain that ultimately God permeates all of creation and our whole being, for he is a creator of everything. Therefore, even when it feels like we talk to ourselves, we, in fact, speak to God.</p>
            <p>As his younger sister grew older, she too joined in with questions that were far more difficult for me to handle. As a middle schooler, she asked me why God doesn’t just free everyone from hell and save everyone. If, as we were taught in weekly sermons, God is truly good and there is nothing we can do to save ourselves, He has to save us all. I realized then that I have a little skeptic, who listens to Sunday sermons, analyses them, and looks for logical inconsistencies.</p>
            <p>Imagine for a moment that I told her that God, a supreme being, has the right to send those who disobey him to eternal torment. Would this be a satisfying answer to a skeptic? In her question, the issue was not only the fate of helpless human beings and apparent unfairness, but the very character of God. What kind of God is it, and would she want to believe in that God? Once again, I knew that I had to tread carefully and give an answer that would be satisfying to a young, skeptical mind. I told her that it is good to wish that everyone would be saved. It is something we should hope for and pray for whenever we can. But we cannot tell God what he should or shouldn’t do, for he is indeed God—and is greater than all our thoughts about Him.</p>
            <p>What I found most striking about all these questions is that my children asked them when they were fairly young, before these questions could be appropriately attributed to profound cultural influence. In other words, their questions arose not from the outside culture, but internally, from their own interactions with church and experiences with Christianity. Now, suppose I did not answer these questions, perhaps shut them down, or gave a simplistic answer that they found unsatisfactory. How would my children have thought and felt once they got older and came into direct contact with the raging atheistic culture all around?</p>
            <p>Most parents or Sunday school teachers are not prepared intellectually or emotionally to answer the difficult questions children so naturally ask. Oftentimes, they can’t answer them for themselves, let alone for little schoolchildren or defiant teenagers. As a result, we desperately need popular-level engagement with skepticism, especially the Nietzschean kind, in our churches. Michael McEwen’s new book <em>The Devil Reads Nietzsche</em> is a timely resource for many Christians, who might feel beaten down by the skepticism and even nihilism of our times.</p>
            <p>To engage Nietzsche is no small feat; he is a fierce and powerful opponent whose writing aims to intimidate, demoralize, and even hurt anybody who wishes to stand up to him. Therefore, McEwen deserves due respect for approaching such a formidable adversary. He also, it must be noted, approaches Nietzsche charitably, in good faith, wishing to understand Nietzsche’s line of thought and arguments, rather than operating with flat generalizations or, worse, false pretenses.</p>
            <p>Treating Nietzsche charitably is the only way to approach such a thinker, not only because our faith demands such treatment of human beings or out of pity (Nietzsche’s capacious mind drove him to madness), but, more important, because Nietzsche rebelled against a lifeless cultural Christianity of his time. As Nikolai Berdyaev said, behind any atheistic rebellion against God is the rebellion <em>for</em> a greater vision of God against a low and unworthy view of the Divine. Nietzsche was, first and foremost, a cultural critic, not a metaphysical philosopher. It was Heidegger who first attributed metaphysics to Nietzsche. It is fitting, then, that McEwen reads Nietzsche carefully and points out Nietzsche’s project as the great skeptic himself understood it—that is, dismantling cultural and religious ideologies.</p>
            <p>While McEwen offers a charitable reading of Nietzsche, he makes his stance clear: He does so with a full commitment to the truthfulness of the Christian metanarrative. McEwen, in other words, offers a critique of Nietzsche, a necessary pushback to his narrative from the point of view of Scripture, exposing the logical inconsistencies in Nietzschean proposals.</p>
            <p>Building on the work of such giants of contemporary theology as Charles Taylor and John Milbank, McEwen follows the prompting of Christopher Watkin, who calls on contemporary Christians to think critically about the narratives with which secular culture operates, rather than passively absorbing them. This critical posture requires understanding the ideas and ideologies we are exposed to and offering an alternative, thoroughly Biblical narrative in their place. McEwen provides just that: an attempt to understand Nietzsche on his own terms, while pointing out his fallacies, an account of how he has influenced our current culture, and finally a counter-narrative.</p>
            <p>How does he do that? McEwen follows a twofold pattern of argumentation. First, he demonstrates how Nietzsche borrows and parodies Christian ideas, twisting them to suit his purpose—to challenge Christianity. Second, he shows how Biblical narrative already has answers to Nietzsche’s challenges. For example, in his chapter on <em>Imago Dei</em> and human identity, McEwen looks at how Nietzsche addresses the question of what it means to be human.</p>
            <p>The classical Christian understanding of human nature combined the Greco-Roman idea of rational (unchangeable) essence with <em>Imago Dei</em>, or Divine image bearers, to formulate a theological anthropology. Nietzsche offers an alternative vision of humans in his <em>Ecce Homo</em>. In this work, he calls for a radical breakaway from the Greco-Christian view of human beings. Human beings, according to Nietzsche, do not have a static essence or unchangeable nature. Rather, they evolve; they become something through their will to power. We do not know ourselves, and the constant becoming means that we change over time. The idea of change and becoming is tied to a particular view of history. Hence, an equally important concept to Nietzsche’s understanding of human beings is the idea of eternal return—a cyclical view of history and human development. While this may sound radically different from classical Christianity, McEwen contends that Nietzsche has retained the idea of eternity and appropriated or re-packaged the Christian concept of sanctification and transformation towards the resurrected body of Christ, but leaving out God.</p>
            <p>McEwen offers an alternative narrative in order to “out-narrate” Nietzsche and show how the Scripture already has a response to his claims. To summarize his response: The New Testament apostolic witness firmly proclaims our future resurrection in the manner of Jesus Christ. In Col. 1:15-18, Paul brings together Christology, our existence, and our identity so that we would know ourselves as Christ’s. In other words, we know who we are when we recognize ourselves as belonging to Christ. The answer to the question of who we are has already been given to us in Christ.</p>
            <p>The mode of engagement McEwen suggests and models in his book is a helpful start. It gives Christian readers hope that even Nietzsche is not impervious to a critique; he can even be beaten at his own game. Although McEwen’s retelling of the Biblical narrative is rather short, it points to the depth and breadth of Christian tradition. And yet, his approach left me with a few questions.</p>
            <p>First, what if our alternative narrative is not that alternative at all, but the same old narrative that Nietzsche reacts against? Nietzsche knew the Bible and came from a religiously devout home. He was well aware of the central role of Jesus Christ in forming our identity and the historical Christian view of human nature. Retelling the same narrative back to Nietzsche may not produce a different result, since this is precisely what Nietzsche is reacting against.</p>
            <p>How does a Biblical narrative help make sense of the history and human development we, as a civilization, have undergone over the last two hundred years? How does our understanding of our identity in Christ help a skeptical person understand dramatic historical and social changes that a given society, say Communist Russia, had experienced in the last hundred years? Nietzsche was not a theologian, not even a metaphysician, as he understood his own work. He was concerned with human culture, something we build or destroy. Therefore, to counter a Nietzschian skepticism, especially when it is raised against Christianity, we have to extract a vision of human history, human culture, and, crucially, development from a Biblical metanarrative.</p>
            <p>No less significant, what if “out-narrating” is not enough to address the widespread skepticism and nihilism in our world today? What if what’s required is not only an alternative critical theory, but something much more demanding—love for people, bearing their burdens, compassion, and giving of ourselves in love? When dealing with skeptics, even very young ones, a robust intellectual response is critical. But at some point, our actions will speak louder than our words.</p>
            <p>McEwen’s book is a much-needed introduction to engaging the intellectual ideas of our time. We need more work like this. Christians can no longer ignore the world of secular ideas. We have entered a new era, where our culture no longer possesses strong Christian intuitions. We will have to learn to live in this culture and engage it anew, drawing from our Christian tradition answers befitting our times.</p>
            <p class="contributor-bio">Vika Pechersky lives with her family outside of Washington DC. Her writing has appeared in <em>Christianity Today</em>, <em>Church Life Journal</em>, and <em>Marginalia Review of Books</em>. You can find her writing about Dostoevsky and Russian sophiologists on her Substack, <em>Reading My Way Back to the East</em>.</p>
          </section>

          <!-- ====== ARTICLE 15 ====== -->
          <section id="the-return-of-the" class="content-section">
            <h2 class="section-title">The Return of the Common Good</h2>
            <p class="essay-author">By James Wood</p>

            <p class="essay-heading">Stefan Borg. <em>The Return of the Common Good: The Postliberal Project Left and Right</em>. Routledge, 2026. $200.00. 162 pp.</p>
            <p>As someone who has been a self-identified postliberal since around 2010, I have been waiting for a book like this for years. Postliberal political theory has recently become a focal point of intense—and often hostile—attention. Critics routinely accuse postliberals of misrepresenting liberalism, failing to offer constructive alternatives, or advancing proposals that amount to thinly veiled authoritarianism. At the same time, confusion persists about what postliberalism actually is, where it came from, and whether it constitutes anything more than a fleeting reaction to recent political developments.</p>
            <p>For several years now, I have frequently been asked whether there exists a single work that could orient readers wholly uninitiated into postliberal political theory. Until now, I have had no monograph-length treatment to recommend—no book that offers a charitable, synoptic account of postliberalism’s intellectual genealogy, internal diversity, and shared concerns. Most existing works either fixate on one recent American faction or engage in sweeping denunciations that obscure the deeper continuities and substantive agreements animating postliberal critiques of the liberal order. In such accounts, opposition to liberalism functions less as an argument worth engaging than as a prelude to warnings about the dangers supposedly lurking beyond it.</p>
            <p>This confusion has only been exacerbated by the way postliberalism is frequently narrated through the lens of the Trumpist moment. Too often, postliberal political theory is treated as little more than an opportunistic response to American populism or an attempt to capitalize on the ascent of President Trump. Meanwhile, postliberal discourse in the United States has taken on a more statist, tribalistic, and narrowly particularistic tone, intensifying uncertainty about its relationship to nationalism. Is Christian nationalism properly postliberal? Is postliberalism simply the political theology on display at National Conservatism conferences? In the absence of careful distinctions, critics— especially in academic and media contexts—have been quick to dismiss the entire discourse as intellectually reckless or socially dangerous for daring to challenge liberalism at a fundamental level.</p>
            <p>Against this backdrop, Stefan Borg’s <em>The Return of the Common Good: The Postliberal Project Left and Right</em> arrives as a welcome—and muchneeded—intervention. Borg’s aim is not to defend every postliberal proposal, but to clarify what postliberalism is, where it comes from, and why it deserves serious consideration. As he puts it in the introduction, the book seeks “to remedy this confusion and present postliberalism as a fully-fledged tradition of thought.” While acknowledging significant internal differences, Borg argues that postliberalism constitutes a coherent—if still underdeveloped and often misunderstood—critique of liberalism, one that has been ignored in part because of liberalism’s own hegemonic status. That critique, he insists, “ought to be taken seriously.”</p>
            <p>Borg’s central claim is that postliberalism is unified by what he calls a shared “common project,” a claim signaled in the book’s subtitle. Despite their disagreements, major postliberal thinkers “mobilize a set of similar arguments about the historical trajectory of liberalism and share an understanding of, as well as having similar misgivings about, the liberal order.” They diverge over what should replace liberalism, but they are united in rejecting liberalism’s “one-sided preoccupation with maximizing individual autonomy” at the expense of goods that sustain political and social life. In Borg’s formulation, “the postliberal project may in part be defined as an attempt to resurrect the common good tradition.”</p>
            <p>Accordingly, the appeal to the common good functions as postliberalism’s central normative standard. At its most basic level, postliberalism insists that claims of individual autonomy must be ordered toward, and constrained by, the flourishing of the political community as a whole. Politics should not be oriented primarily toward autonomy-maximization, but toward sustaining the conditions of shared life.</p>
            <p>In its most ambitious form, Borg argues, postliberalism seeks to retrieve a more ancient and Christian conception of politics—one oriented toward virtue and genuine human flourishing—over against the liberal tendency to construe politics as a mechanism for securing individual freedom alone.</p>
            <p>Importantly, Borg is careful to stress that this “common project” does not imply uniformity. He distinguishes between two broad branches of postliberalism: a British strand and an American one. While there is sufficient overlap to justify speaking of a single project, the differences between these branches—particularly their historical origins—are analytically illuminating. One of Borg’s most valuable contributions is to demonstrate that British postliberalism long predates both Donald Trump’s election and Patrick Deneen’s <em>Why Liberalism Failed</em>. On Borg’s account, postliberalism emerges in the 1990s and develops across theology, political theory, jurisprudence, international relations, and cultural criticism.</p>
            <p>The term “postliberalism” itself (with reference to politics) was first deployed in the early 1990s by the British political theorist John Gray to name his growing disillusionment with liberal assumptions and liberal social orders. Yet Borg traces the deeper roots of British postliberalism to Radical Orthodoxy, the theological movement associated above all with John Milbank. Radical Orthodoxy mounted a far-reaching critique of secular modernity and “what is perceived as its chief ideological expression, namely liberalism.” Its foundational text is Milbank’s <em>Theology and Social Theory</em> (1990), which argued that liberalism rests on a secular ontology that displaces theology as the interpretive framework for social life.</p>
            <p>Postliberalism assumed a more overtly political form in the aftermath of Labour’s defeat in the 2010 British election. Maurice Glasman founded Blue Labour, while Philip Blond—a student of Milbank’s and a contributor to <em>Radical Orthodoxy: A New</em> Theology—advanced related ideas under the banner of Red Toryism through the think tank ResPublica. Around the same time, the term “postliberalism” entered wider public discourse through figures such as David Goodhart, who popularized postliberal critiques of market society and social fragmentation. Across these expressions, British postliberalism combined a rejection of radical individualism and market atomization with an equally strong resistance to collectivism, emphasizing virtue, intermediary institutions, and local forms of belonging.</p>
            <p>At the intellectual core of this tradition stands Radical Orthodoxy. Beginning with <em>Theology and Social Theory</em> and culminating, in Borg’s telling, with Milbank and Adrian Pabst’s <em>The Politics of Virtue: Post-Liberalism and the Human Future</em> (2016)—which Borg describes as “the most theoretically elaborate articulation of postliberalism” and “the most comprehensive statement of postliberalism currently on offer”— Radical Orthodoxy advances a comprehensive critique of liberalism as the ideology of the secular. Liberalism, on this account, insists on carving out a supposedly neutral public sphere free from theological commitments, reducing politics to procedural coordination among competing conceptions of the good. Radical Orthodoxy rejects this neutrality as illusory. Liberalism, Milbank argues, advances its own substantive moral vision, grounded in what he famously calls an “ontology of violence”—a vision of social reality that presupposes primordial conflict and self-interest rather than participation in the divine good. As Borg summarizes, Milbank’s project seeks to reinstate theology as a governing meta-discourse by which secular modernity itself may be judged, and to recover a Christian social vision as a genuine alternative to the liberal order.</p>
            <p>Much of Borg’s account resonated deeply with my own intellectual trajectory. My engagement with postliberalism predates the Trump moment and developed independently of the American cohort associated with <em>The Postliberal Order</em>. Like many others, I was first prepared for postliberal arguments through engagement with communitarian thinkers such as Alasdair MacIntyre and Michael Sandel, as well as figures who bridge communitarianism and postliberal theology, notably Stanley Hauerwas.</p>
            <p>Borg is especially helpful in making explicit the overlap between communitarianism and postliberalism. Postliberals share with communitarians a relational anthropology, a rejection of autonomous individualism, skepticism toward claims of neutrality, and a critique of negative liberty as liberalism’s core value. Yet Borg rightly resists collapsing the two traditions. Communitarianism emerged in a different historical moment and often exhibited a greater reluctance toward state action, and tended toward preservation of inherited social forms— which can drift in a more relativistic direction. Postliberalism—especially in its Radical Orthodox variant—is more overtly political and more explicitly theological, advancing a thicker normative account of political order and the common good.</p>
            <p>The American branch of postliberalism, Borg argues, coalesces later, beginning with Trump’s election in 2016 and crystallizing with Patrick Deneen’s <em>Why Liberalism Failed</em> (2018), which Borg calls “the first defining work of U.S. postliberalism.” Deneen’s critique bears a clear communitarian imprint, hardly surprising given his exposure to the communitarian political theorist Wilson Carey McWilliams during his undergraduate years at Rutgers. Liberalism, Deneen argues, rests on an anthropology of voluntaristic individualism, a conception of liberty as autonomy-expansion, and a severing of the human person from nature. Far from limiting state power, this individualism generates statism: as social bonds erode, the state expands to manage the resulting disorder. Market capitalism intensifies this dynamic by commodifying desire while relying on regulatory expansion. <em>Why Liberalism Failed</em> is primarily diagnostic; Deneen’s later <em>Regime Change</em> (2023) advances more constructive proposals centered on the recovery of the common good. Borg situates these developments within the consolidation of American postliberalism around Deneen, Adrian Vermeule, Gladden Pappin, and Chad Pecknold in the early 2020s.</p>
            <p>Despite these differences, Borg identifies three elements that unify postliberalism as a common project: a genealogical critique of liberalism’s internal contradictions, a social analysis of the orders liberalism produces, and a normative commitment to the common good.</p>
            <p>First, postliberals broadly agree that liberalism is internally self-undermining. Its commitment to atomistic individualism proves corrosive not only to social life but also to the natural world. Liberalism systematically privileges the political and economic spheres over the civic and social, and this imbalance ultimately erodes the latter. Moreover, while liberalism presents itself as neutral with respect to substantive visions of the good, postliberals argue that it in fact enforces this purported neutrality as a comprehensive moral framework, often acting illiberally toward those whose substantive commitments conflict with it.</p>
            <p>Postliberals identify liberalism as a decisive break with classical and Christian accounts of political order, and this is why Thomas Hobbes figures so centrally in their genealogy. <em>Leviathan</em> is commonly understood as “the most forceful articulation of the break with the ancients,” radically undercutting the foundations of common-good political theory. As Borg explains, “modernity is often understood as the process by which Hobbesian assumptions of the world slowly but steadily won out.” Hobbes’s conception of order as an artificial imposition of power designed to manage primordial hostility generates a pervasive suspicion toward social authority and inherited forms of life—one that, over time, functions as an acid on the social fabric. Contractarian models weaken the intermediary institutions that once mediated between the individual and the state, while liberalism’s commitment to negative liberty fosters mistrust toward the traditions, habits, and norms that sustain common life. In a market society these dynamics are further exacerbated, as social relations themselves are increasingly commodified. The result is a mutually reinforcing logic in which the abstract individual, the expanding state, and the market together erode the very sources of social cohesion and shared flourishing on which liberal order ultimately depends.</p>
            <p>Second, postliberals analyze the social orders liberalism produces, with particular attention to the rise of a managerial elite. Drawing on James Burnham, Christopher Lasch, and Michael Lind, postliberals narrate the emergence of a professional class that governs institutions while remaining detached from the communities they administer. This elite combines technocratic power with cultural liberalism, advancing norms that corrode social solidarity while entrenching its own privileges.</p>
            <p>Before turning to the third element, Borg clarifies a frequent misunderstanding. Critics object that postliberals offer an overly narrow, one-sided account of liberalism. Borg concedes that postliberal critiques are not primarily exercises in textual reconstruction. But this, he argues, is precisely the point. Postliberals treat liberalism not merely as a theory but as a sociopolitical project whose consequences matter more than its internal nuances. The question is whether liberal societies can sustain the goods they require to function.</p>
            <p>This leads to the third element: the recovery of the common good. Drawing on Helen Rosenblatt’s authoritative history of liberalism, Borg acknowledges that earlier liberals often affirmed the common good, but he argues that contemporary liberalism—exemplified by John Rawls—treats it as politically inaccessible. Under conditions of pluralism, Rawls contends, the state must remain neutral and secure fair procedures which allow individuals to pursue their disparate ends rather than promote substantive goods. Postliberalism rejects this premise, seeking to recover a politics ordered toward virtue rather than autonomy.</p>
            <p>Borg identifies three postliberal approaches to the common good: pluralist, common good conservatism, and integralism. The pluralist approach, associated with British postliberalism and Michael Lind, emphasizes an open-ended pursuit of the common good through dispersed power, corporatist institutions, relational economies, and strong civil society. Deneen’s common good conservatism places greater emphasis on tradition, social stability, and popular wisdom, articulated through his concept of “aristopopulism.” Integralism, associated with Vermeule, offers the most programmatic and state-centered approach, insisting that political authority should actively promote substantive goods.</p>
            <p>Borg’s typology usefully dispels the claim that postliberalism lacks constructive proposals. Yet the book’s greatest strength lies in re-centering the common good itself—an emphasis increasingly eclipsed by the association of postliberalism with nationalism.</p>
            <p>Echoing his earlier point about communitarianism, Borg argues that postliberals are opposed to radical conservatism in that they are not primarily motivated by the preservation of a given social order—a posture that can easily slide into relativism. And, as Oliver O’Donovan demonstrates in <em>Ways of Judgment</em>, nationalism often functions as a species of identity politics, which postliberals recognize as an expression of liberalism. Postliberals can concede that nationalist critiques of liberal universalism have real force, particularly insofar as liberalism tends to universalize late-modern Western norms. Yet nationalism, Borg suggests, frequently replicates liberalism’s own pathologies. It retreats to a Hobbesian state of nature at the international level and reduces political judgment to collective selfinterest—merely scaling liberal individualism and liberalism’s “ontology of violence” up to the level of nations.</p>
            <p>By contrast, Borg shows that postliberals insist on the irreducible universality of the Christian faith, even while affirming the necessity of historical and cultural particularity. As he puts it, “[U]niversals must always find expression in historically instantiated particulars ….</p>
            <p>Universals are never fully disclosed outside of their (always imperfect) instantiations in particulars.” Where strands of the dissident right radicalize particularity and lapse into an ontology of violence—now transposed to relations between nations—postliberals such as Pabst and Milbank advance a form of Christian internationalism. We should understand nations as neighbors—each seeking in its own way to instantiate the good, with the common good itself requiring forms of international cooperation. On this view, postliberalism decisively rejects frameworks such as Samuel Huntington’s “clash of civilizations” thesis.</p>
            <p>The book also draws attention to the “sex-realist feminists”—figures such as Louise Perry, Christine Emba, Erika Bachiochi, and Mary Harrington—showing how their work advances distinctly postliberal arguments. In particular, they critique contractarian accounts of sexual relations, the commodification of intimacy, the equation of libertinism with empowerment, and what Harrington terms “bio-libertarianism.” This strand of thought is rarely included in standard mappings of postliberalism, yet Borg persuasively demonstrates its family resemblance to the broader postliberal project. On this point, I am entirely in agreement: these thinkers deserve to be recognized as an integral part of the postliberal conversation.</p>
            <p>There are, however, areas where Borg could go further. He rightly notes that not all postliberal positions are wholly or uniformly antiliberal, and he gestures toward the ways liberalism emerged by abstracting and radicalizing certain Christian principles. I would press this insight further: liberalism is best understood as a Christian heresy—or, in Oliver O’Donovan’s formulation, the “prodigal child” of Christendom. Liberalism represents, to borrow G. K. Chesterton’s phrase, a set of Christian “virtues gone mad.” Insofar as liberal orders continue to draw on moral capital they cannot themselves reproduce (à <em>la</em> the Böckenforde dilemma), that capital must be re-tethered to the Christian whole from which it was severed. On this view, the task is not simply to oppose liberalism but to call it home—to re-anchor its moral claims within the ecclesial and theological framework that once gave them coherence and limits.</p>
            <p>Indeed, this points toward a deeper omission. Borg would benefit from drawing the theological thread more consistently throughout the book. Postliberalism—especially in its Radical Orthodox forms—is not merely a sociological critique but a theological one. Postliberals do not merely criticize liberalism for its deracinating social effects—for eroding the resources that sustain common life, producing increasingly atomized individuals alongside an ever-expanding state—but also for its desacralizing logic. This dimension is central to John Milbank’s argument in <em>Theology and Social Theory</em>, where the very category of the “secular” is exposed as a historical and conceptual invention—one that, though emerging from within the Christian tradition itself, was progressively hardened in modernity into a domain conceived as wholly autonomous from and impermeable to theological judgment. At the heart of Radical Orthodoxy, and thus of postliberalism more broadly, is the insistence that theology be recovered as a governing metadiscourse—the <em>regina scientiarum</em> by which other forms of knowledge are ordered and judged.</p>
            <p>This theological critique carries an implication that Borg only partially develops: political order is never religiously neutral. Borg notes that postliberals expose liberalism’s claim to neutrality as illusory and highlight its substantive moral commitments. Yet he stops short of drawing the full implication—namely, that such commitments are always and inevitably theological. He comes close when he observes that deviations from liberal orthodoxies are often treated as a form of “secular sinning,” but he does not carry the point far enough. Every political order, liberal ones included, necessarily renders judgments about God, religion, and their relevance to social life and human flourishing. Borg rightly shows that postliberals understand the human person as socially constituted and embedded in thick networks of relationships. But the most fully Christian expressions of postliberalism press further still, insisting that human beings are situated within a theological horizon, ordered toward God. Without foregrounding this claim, postliberalism risks being reduced to institutional reform rather than a challenge to liberalism’s spiritual architecture.</p>
            <p>Finally, Borg largely omits a crucial postliberal strand: ecclesiocentric postliberalism, represented by the Communio school and figures such as David L. and D. C. Schindler, as well as Peter Leithart and the Civitas network. Though largely North American, these thinkers stand closer to the theological and ecclesiological concerns of Milbank and Radical Orthodoxy than to the more overtly political forms of American postliberalism—complicating Borg’s neat British-American divide. What unites them is the insistence that the Church is not a voluntary association concerned exclusively with “private” and otherworldly realities, but a public body. To be sure, Borg does note that Deneen gestures in this direction, particularly in his critique of post-Westphalian nationalism, wherein the modern state sought to eliminate rival claims to sovereignty—above all those of the Catholic Church. In this settlement, the Church was progressively reduced by secular sovereigns who claimed the authority to determine the boundaries of acceptable religion within their respective realms. Yet Borg does not develop this line of analysis beyond a brief summary of Deneen’s point. Moreover, he appears to treat the Church as merely one intermediate institution among others situated between the individual and the state.</p>
            <p>What is missing—and what the Communio and Civitas strands of postliberalism help recover— is the political significance of the Church itself. Borg rightly highlights how postliberals critique liberalism for recognizing only non-state communities formed on liberal premises: voluntary associations of contracting individuals pursuing shared interests. The Church cannot be understood in these terms. She is not simply one voluntary society within civil society, constructed by individual choice and ranked beneath the state. She is a reality that confronts all persons. She is the body of Christ on earth (Rom. 12; 1 Cor. 12; Eph. 1; Col. 1), the pillar and bulwark of the truth (1 Tim. 3:15), and the Christian’s more ultimate family (Matt. 12:48– 50), nation (1 Pet. 2), and <em>polis</em> (Eph. 2:19; cf. Phil. 3:20; Heb. 12:22–23). The Church is the most comprehensive community, summoning all persons and, in doing so, relativizing and challenging the totalizing claims of other social bodies and purported sovereigns. Yet she does so not by withdrawing from history, but by seeking to be instantiated within every society and to receive—purified and redeemed—the fruits of each. Precisely as a multinational and transnational body, the Church also serves as a source of reconciliation and healing among the nations.</p>
            <p>Leithart explains how liberalism systematically marginalizes the Church by reshaping its ecclesiology, focusing especially on the foundational figures of Thomas Hobbes and John Locke. Hobbes, Leithart contends, domesticated the Church by subordinating it to the civil sovereign, thereby resolving the problem of divided loyalties between temporal and spiritual authority. Locke, by contrast, domesticated the Church by spiritualizing and privatizing it, reducing it to a voluntary association concerned with inward belief rather than public truth. In both cases, liberalism strips the Church of any genuinely public status.</p>
            <p>Leithart’s analysis here echoes the work of the sociologist of religion José Casanova, who has argued that the liberal state’s “preferred form of religion” is a privatized one. On this account, the liberal assumption is that religion is—and ought to remain—a “private affair.” The consequence is that the Church is stripped of any genuinely public status. It is no longer understood as a commonwealth (, no longer a society of “citizens” (gathered in the public assembly of the household of God (Eph. 2:12, 19). Instead, the modern liberal order ultimately recognizes only two actors as politically salient: the sovereign individual and the state. By marginalizing the Church as a public body, liberalism effectively eliminates any competing form of authority or allegiance.</p>
            <p>Modern liberal orders have sought to privatize religion and to emancipate social life from ecclesial authority. The Church’s jurisdiction is thereby restricted to those who voluntarily choose her, while the state is increasingly treated as a comprehensive authority from which no one may opt out. Over time, the state comes to be viewed as the most ultimate and indispensable community, and loyalties once oriented toward the Church are gradually redirected toward political sovereignty. Liberalism’s deepest error, according to these postliberals, is its ecclesiological one: privatizing the Church while rendering the state ultimate. Any postliberalism that fails to recognize this fundamental ecclesiological heresy at the root of the liberal order—and the religious dangers it entails—remains incomplete.</p>
            <p>Despite these omissions, Borg’s book is an indispensable primer on postliberalism. It is the clearest, most charitable map of the terrain currently available—and it will be my default recommendation … at least once it appears in paperback.</p>
            <p class="contributor-bio">James R. Wood is associate professor of religion and theology at Redeemer University (Ancaster, ON). He is also a teaching elder in the Presbyterian Church in America and co-host of the Civitas Podcast produced by the Theopolis Institute and Mere Fidelity with Mere Orthodoxy. His recently published book is <em>Thomas F. Torrance’s Sacramental Ecclesiology</em> (Davenant, 2026), and his next book is <em>Augustine’s Wisdom for Christian Pilgrims: Timeless Insights from the Social Teachings of an Early Church Father</em> (Baker, 2027).</p>
